<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Jeevs Edition — Hardest Exam (Final 100) Quiz</title>
<style>
:root{
  --navy:#12203C;--navy2:#1B2A4A;
  --teal:#1A7A6D;--teal-glow:#3EC4B2;--teal-mid:#2BA393;--teal-light:#E3F5F2;--teal-deep:#125E54;
  --green:#2D8659;--green-bg:#E8F5EE;
  --red:#C44040;--red-bg:#FDF0F0;
  --amber:#C07B1A;--amber-bg:#FEF5E6;
  --gold-bg:#FDF6E3;
  --coral:#E07A5F;
  --ink:#1E1E20;--ink2:#3E4555;--slate:#6B7689;--mist:#A0AAB8;
  --rule:#DBD5C9;--cream:#F6F3EC;--warm:#FBF8F3;--white:#FFFFFF;
  --sh:0 8px 24px rgba(18,32,60,.08);--sh2:0 14px 34px rgba(18,32,60,.10);--sh3:0 28px 70px rgba(18,32,60,.12);
  --tr:0.25s cubic-bezier(.4,0,.2,1);--r:14px;--rs:8px;
  --stem-size:25px;--choice-size:15.5px;
  --app-vh:100dvh;--app-vw:100vw;--viewport-scale:1;--ui-title-size:26px;--ui-control-size:13px;--ui-meta-size:12px;--ui-micro-size:10px;
  --font-ui:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;--font-mono:Consolas,"Courier New",monospace;
}
*,*::before,*::after{margin:0;padding:0;box-sizing:border-box}
body{font-family:var(--font-ui);background:var(--cream);color:var(--ink);min-height:var(--app-vh);-webkit-font-smoothing:antialiased}
body.nbme-lock{overflow:hidden}
.view{display:none}.view.active{display:block}
#viewQuizNbme{height:var(--app-vh);overflow:hidden}
#viewQuizNbme.active{position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:40}

/* ══ SETUP VIEW ══ */
.setup-page{min-height:var(--app-vh);display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;justify-content:center;padding:32px 20px}
.setup-card{background:var(--white);border-radius:24px;border:1px solid var(--rule);box-shadow:var(--sh3);width:min(680px,100%);overflow:hidden}
.setup-top{background:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--navy) 0%,#1B2A4A 100%);padding:36px 36px 30px;text-align:center}
.setup-emoji{font-size:48px;margin-bottom:14px}
.setup-title{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:34px;font-weight:700;color:#fff;letter-spacing:-.15px;margin-bottom:6px}
.setup-title em{font-style:normal;color:var(--teal-glow)}
.setup-sub{font-size:15px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.65);line-height:1.6}

.setup-body{padding:32px 36px 36px}

/* instruction steps */
.how-title{font-size:11px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--mist);margin-bottom:16px}
.steps{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px;margin-bottom:28px}
.step{display:flex;gap:14px;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 16px;border-radius:12px;background:var(--warm);border:1px solid var(--rule)}
.step-num{width:28px;height:28px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--navy);color:#fff;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;margin-top:1px;font-family:var(--font-ui)}
.step-text{font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2);line-height:1.6}
.step-text strong{color:var(--ink);display:block;margin-bottom:2px}
.how-visual{margin:-12px 0 28px;padding:12px;border-radius:12px;background:var(--warm);border:1px solid var(--rule)}
.how-visual img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.1);background:#fff}
.how-visual-caption{margin-top:9px;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--ink2)}

/* prompt copy box */
.prompt-wrap{background:var(--navy);border-radius:12px;padding:16px 18px;position:relative;margin-bottom:8px}
.prompt-meta{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;margin-bottom:10px;padding-right:82px}
.prompt-chip{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;min-height:28px;padding:0 10px;border-radius:999px;background:rgba(62,196,178,.14);border:1px solid rgba(62,196,178,.28);color:#D6ECFA;font-size:11.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.03em}
.prompt-inner{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.68;color:#D6ECFA;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;max-height:220px;overflow-y:auto;scrollbar-width:thin;scrollbar-color:rgba(255,255,255,.1) transparent;padding-right:70px}
.copy-prompt-btn{position:absolute;top:12px;right:12px;padding:6px 12px;background:rgba(62,196,178,.15);border:1px solid rgba(62,196,178,.35);border-radius:7px;color:var(--teal-glow);font-size:12px;font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;font-family:var(--font-ui);transition:all .18s;white-space:nowrap}
.copy-prompt-btn:hover{background:rgba(62,196,178,.28)}
.copy-prompt-btn.ok{background:rgba(45,134,89,.2);border-color:rgba(45,134,89,.4);color:#5EC490}

.divider{height:1px;background:var(--rule);margin:24px 0}

/* paste area */
.field{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px;margin-bottom:16px}
.field:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
.field-note{font-size:12px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--mist)}
.paste-tabs{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;margin-top:4px}
.paste-tab{padding:11px 14px;border-radius:12px;border:1.5px solid rgba(18,32,60,.1);background:#fff;color:var(--slate);font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:13px;font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;transition:var(--tr);box-shadow:0 6px 16px rgba(18,32,60,.05)}
.paste-tab:hover{transform:translateY(-1px);border-color:rgba(26,122,109,.34);color:var(--teal)}
.paste-tab.active{background:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--navy),#2A3562);border-color:transparent;color:#fff;box-shadow:0 12px 24px rgba(18,32,60,.18)}
.paste-tab.has-content:not(.active){background:rgba(26,122,109,.08);border-color:rgba(26,122,109,.18);color:var(--teal-deep)}
.paste-panels{margin-top:12px}
.paste-panel{display:none}
.paste-panel.active{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px}
.paste-panel .ta{min-height:180px}
.lbl{font-size:11px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:1.5px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--slate)}
.inp{padding:10px 14px;border-radius:var(--rs);border:1.5px solid var(--rule);font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:14px;font-weight:400;color:var(--ink);background:var(--warm);outline:none;transition:border-color .18s;width:100%}
.inp:focus{border-color:var(--teal);box-shadow:0 0 0 3px rgba(62,196,178,.12);background:#fff}
.ta{width:100%;padding:13px 15px;border-radius:var(--rs);border:1.5px solid var(--rule);font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:13px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink);background:var(--warm);resize:vertical;min-height:160px;outline:none;transition:border-color .18s}
.ta:focus{border-color:var(--teal);box-shadow:0 0 0 3px rgba(62,196,178,.12);background:#fff}
.ta::placeholder{color:var(--mist)}
.setup-actions{display:flex;gap:12px;margin-top:18px}
.launch-btn{width:100%;padding:16px;border-radius:16px;border:none;background:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--teal-glow),var(--teal-mid));color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:17px;font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;letter-spacing:.15px;transition:all .2s;box-shadow:0 8px 22px rgba(62,196,178,.3);margin-top:18px}
.launch-btn:hover{transform:translateY(-2px);box-shadow:0 14px 30px rgba(62,196,178,.38)}
.setup-actions .launch-btn{margin-top:0;flex:1}
.ghost-btn{padding:16px 18px;border-radius:16px;border:1.5px solid rgba(18,32,60,.12);background:#fff;color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:15px;font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;letter-spacing:.1px;transition:all .2s;box-shadow:0 8px 22px rgba(18,32,60,.08)}
.ghost-btn:hover{transform:translateY(-2px);border-color:rgba(26,122,109,.34);color:var(--teal);box-shadow:0 14px 30px rgba(18,32,60,.12)}
.setup-ui-toggle{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:10px}
.setup-ui-btn{padding:15px 16px;border-radius:16px;border:1.5px solid rgba(18,32,60,.1);background:var(--warm);color:var(--ink);cursor:pointer;text-align:left;font-family:var(--font-ui);transition:var(--tr);box-shadow:0 8px 18px rgba(18,32,60,.05)}
.setup-ui-btn:hover{transform:translateY(-1px);border-color:rgba(26,122,109,.34);background:#fff}
.setup-ui-btn.active{background:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--navy),#2A3562);border-color:transparent;color:#fff;box-shadow:0 14px 30px rgba(18,32,60,.18)}
.setup-ui-name{display:block;font-size:14px;font-weight:900;line-height:1.2}
.setup-ui-meta{display:block;font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:inherit;opacity:.72;margin-top:6px}
.err-msg{padding:10px 14px;border-radius:var(--rs);font-size:13px;font-weight:700;display:none;align-items:center;gap:8px;margin-top:10px;background:var(--red-bg);color:var(--red);border:1px solid rgba(196,64,64,.2)}
.err-msg.show{display:flex}

/* ══ QUIZ VIEW ══ */
.quiz-page{min-height:var(--app-vh);display:flex;flex-direction:column}

/* top bar */
.qt-bar{background:linear-gradient(180deg,var(--navy) 0%,var(--navy2) 100%);display:flex;align-items:center;gap:14px;padding:0 22px;height:52px;flex-shrink:0;box-shadow:0 2px 12px rgba(18,32,60,.22)}
.qt-name{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:calc(var(--ui-title-size) * .67);font-weight:700;color:#fff;flex:1;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;white-space:nowrap;letter-spacing:-.1px}
.qt-btn{padding:8px 14px;border-radius:10px;border:1.5px solid rgba(255,255,255,.2);background:transparent;color:rgba(255,255,255,.75);font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:var(--ui-control-size);font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;transition:all .18s}
.qt-btn:hover{background:rgba(255,255,255,.1);color:#fff}
.qt-btn.active{background:rgba(255,255,255,.16);color:#fff;border-color:rgba(255,255,255,.34)}

/* grid */
.qin{width:min(1800px,calc(100vw - 24px));margin:0 auto;padding:22px 20px 72px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:clamp(270px,19vw,330px) minmax(0,1fr);gap:18px 26px;align-items:start}

/* sidebar panels */
.score-panel{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr;gap:10px;margin-bottom:0}
.score-box{position:relative;overflow:hidden;background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--white) 88%,var(--warm) 12%);border-radius:20px;padding:16px 12px 13px;text-align:center;box-shadow:var(--sh2);border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);transition:var(--tr)}
.score-box::before{content:'';position:absolute;left:14px;right:14px;top:0;height:4px;border-radius:0 0 999px 999px;background:linear-gradient(90deg,rgba(26,122,109,.16),rgba(62,196,178,.5));opacity:.75}
.score-box.correct{background:linear-gradient(180deg,rgba(255,255,255,.92),rgba(232,245,238,.98))}.score-box.correct::before{background:linear-gradient(90deg,rgba(45,134,89,.2),rgba(45,134,89,.72))}
.score-box.incorrect{background:linear-gradient(180deg,rgba(255,255,255,.92),rgba(253,240,240,.98))}.score-box.incorrect::before{background:linear-gradient(90deg,rgba(196,64,64,.18),rgba(196,64,64,.68))}
.sv{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:34px;font-weight:700;color:var(--navy);line-height:1}
.sl{font-size:9px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:2px;color:var(--mist);font-weight:800;margin-top:7px}
.score-box.correct .sv{color:var(--green)}.score-box.incorrect .sv{color:var(--red)}

.mode-toggle{display:flex;gap:7px;padding:7px;border-radius:22px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.76);backdrop-filter:blur(8px);border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);box-shadow:var(--sh2)}
.mode-btn{flex:1;padding:12px 14px;border-radius:16px;text-align:center;font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:13.5px;font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;border:none;background:transparent;color:var(--slate);transition:all .22s}
.mode-btn:hover{color:var(--navy);background:rgba(18,32,60,.04)}
.mode-btn.active{background:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--navy),#2A3562);color:#fff;box-shadow:0 10px 22px rgba(27,42,74,.18)}
.mode-btn .mode-icon{display:block;font-size:19px;margin-bottom:3px}

.timer-card{position:relative;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;padding:42px 16px 16px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.84);border-radius:22px;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);box-shadow:var(--sh2);transition:var(--tr)}
.timer-card.run{border-color:rgba(26,122,109,.34);box-shadow:0 0 0 4px rgba(26,122,109,.08),var(--sh2)}
.timer-card.warn{border-color:rgba(224,122,95,.4);box-shadow:0 0 0 4px rgba(224,122,95,.08),var(--sh2)}
.timer-lbl{position:absolute;top:13px;left:15px;font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:2px;color:var(--mist);font-weight:800;padding:5px 9px;border-radius:999px;background:rgba(18,32,60,.04)}
.timer-time{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:38px;font-weight:700;color:var(--navy);min-width:110px;text-align:center;letter-spacing:1px}
.timer-card.warn .timer-time{color:var(--coral)}
.tbtn{width:40px;height:40px;border-radius:50%;border:1.8px solid rgba(27,42,74,.24);background:rgba(255,255,255,.9);cursor:pointer;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:15px;transition:var(--tr);color:var(--navy);flex-shrink:0;box-shadow:0 6px 18px rgba(18,32,60,.08)}
.tbtn:hover{background:var(--navy);color:#fff;transform:translateY(-1px) scale(1.04);border-color:var(--navy)}

.tool-panel{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:9px;padding:9px;border-radius:22px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.76);backdrop-filter:blur(8px);border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);box-shadow:var(--sh2)}
.q-tool{flex:1 1 120px;min-width:0;padding:11px 14px;border-radius:14px;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:700;border:1.5px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);background:rgba(255,255,255,.86);cursor:pointer;transition:var(--tr);font-family:var(--font-ui);color:var(--ink2);box-shadow:0 6px 16px rgba(18,32,60,.06);text-align:left}
.q-tool:hover{border-color:rgba(26,122,109,.32);color:var(--teal);transform:translateY(-1px);background:#fff}
.q-tool.active.hl-tool{background:#FFF8B8;border-color:#D3AF2B;color:#6B5C00;box-shadow:0 0 0 3px rgba(211,175,43,.12)}
.q-tool.active.st-tool{background:var(--red-bg);border-color:rgba(196,64,64,.44);color:var(--red);box-shadow:0 0 0 3px rgba(196,64,64,.08)}
.q-tool.lab-tool{flex-basis:100%}
.font-tools{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;flex:1 1 100%;padding:12px 14px;border-radius:14px;border:1.5px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);background:rgba(255,255,255,.86);box-shadow:0 6px 16px rgba(18,32,60,.06)}
.font-tools-label{font-size:12px;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--slate)}
.font-tools-group{display:flex;gap:8px;margin-left:auto}
.font-btn{min-width:42px;padding:9px 12px;border-radius:12px;border:1.5px solid rgba(18,32,60,.1);background:#fff;color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:13px;font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;transition:var(--tr);box-shadow:0 6px 14px rgba(18,32,60,.05)}
.font-btn:hover{transform:translateY(-1px);border-color:rgba(26,122,109,.34);color:var(--teal)}
.font-btn.reset{min-width:54px}

/* sidebar stack */
.sidebar{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px;position:sticky;top:18px}

/* question card — pixel-perfect hub match */
.qcd{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--white) 94%,var(--warm) 6%);border-radius:28px;overflow:hidden;box-shadow:var(--sh3);border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);animation:cardIn .42s cubic-bezier(.4,0,.2,1) both}
@keyframes cardIn{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(14px)}to{opacity:1;transform:translateY(0)}}
.qpg{height:6px;background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--teal) 10%,transparent)}
.qpf{height:100%;background:linear-gradient(90deg,var(--teal),var(--teal-glow));transition:width .5s cubic-bezier(.4,0,.2,1);border-radius:0 999px 999px 0}
.qbd{padding:28px 34px 26px}
.q-review-grid{display:grid;gap:24px}
.q-review-grid.has-feedback{grid-template-columns:minmax(0,.94fr) minmax(0,1.06fr);align-items:start}
.q-feedback{min-width:0;align-self:start}
.q-feedback .exb{margin-top:0;max-height:calc(var(--app-vh) - clamp(180px,24vh,260px));overflow:auto;overscroll-behavior:auto;scrollbar-width:thin;scrollbar-color:rgba(18,32,60,.28) rgba(18,32,60,.08)}
.q-feedback .exb::-webkit-scrollbar{width:10px}
.q-feedback .exb::-webkit-scrollbar-track{background:rgba(18,32,60,.08);border-radius:999px}
.q-feedback .exb::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb{background:rgba(18,32,60,.28);border-radius:999px}
.qnum{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;padding:6px 11px;border-radius:999px;background:rgba(18,32,60,.045);font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:11px;color:var(--mist);letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:14px;font-weight:700}
.qnum.marked{background:var(--gold-bg);color:var(--amber)}
.qstem{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:var(--stem-size);line-height:1.45;margin-bottom:22px;font-weight:400;color:var(--ink);letter-spacing:0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere}
.lab-panel{display:grid;margin:14px 0 18px;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.13);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden;background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--white) 88%,var(--warm) 12%);font-size:.86em;line-height:1.35;white-space:normal;box-shadow:0 5px 16px rgba(18,32,60,.04)}
.lab-head,.lab-row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.35fr) minmax(96px,.65fr) minmax(118px,.8fr);align-items:baseline;gap:18px;padding:9px 14px}
.lab-head{background:rgba(18,32,60,.075);font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.72em;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--mist)}
.lab-row+.lab-row{border-top:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08)}
.lab-row:nth-child(even){background:rgba(18,32,60,.025)}
.lab-name{min-width:0;font-weight:600}
.lab-value,.lab-reference{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:500}
.lab-reference{color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--ink) 68%,var(--mist) 32%);font-size:.92em}
.lab-note{padding:7px 14px;border-top:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);background:rgba(18,32,60,.02);color:var(--mist);font-size:.72em;line-height:1.35}
.qstem mark{
  background:linear-gradient(180deg,transparent 12%,var(--mark-color,#FFFAAD) 12%,var(--mark-color,#FFFAAD) 88%,transparent 88%);
  padding:0;
  margin:0;
  border-radius:.1em;
  color:inherit;
  -webkit-box-decoration-break:clone;
  box-decoration-break:clone;
}
.qstem s{color:var(--mist);text-decoration:line-through}
.ach{display:grid;gap:10px}
.ac{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:14px;padding:15px 18px;border:1.5px solid rgba(18,32,60,.09);border-radius:18px;cursor:pointer;transition:var(--tr);background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--warm) 78%,var(--white) 22%);box-shadow:0 6px 18px rgba(18,32,60,.04)}
.ac:hover:not(.dis){border-color:rgba(26,122,109,.4);background:linear-gradient(180deg,#fff,#F0F7F5);transform:translateY(-1px)}
.ac.sel{border-color:rgba(26,122,109,.5)!important;background:linear-gradient(180deg,#fff,#EFF7F5)!important;transform:translateY(-1px)}
.ac.cor{border-color:rgba(45,134,89,.44)!important;background:linear-gradient(180deg,#fff,#EDF8F1)!important}
.ac.inc{border-color:rgba(196,64,64,.4)!important;background:linear-gradient(180deg,#fff,#FDF2F2)!important}
.ac.dis{cursor:default;transform:none!important}
.cl{width:42px;height:42px;border-radius:50%;border:1.8px solid rgba(18,32,60,.12);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:15px;font-weight:700;flex-shrink:0;transition:var(--tr);background:#fff;box-shadow:0 6px 14px rgba(18,32,60,.05)}
.ac.sel .cl{background:var(--teal)!important;border-color:var(--teal)!important;color:#fff!important}
.ac.cor .cl{background:var(--green)!important;border-color:var(--green)!important;color:#fff!important}
.ac.inc .cl{background:var(--red)!important;border-color:var(--red)!important;color:#fff!important}
.ac-txt{font-size:var(--choice-size);font-weight:400;line-height:1.55;color:var(--ink);padding-top:9px}
.ac-txt mark{
  background:linear-gradient(180deg,transparent 12%,var(--mark-color,#FFFAAD) 12%,var(--mark-color,#FFFAAD) 88%,transparent 88%);
  padding:0;
  margin:0;
  border-radius:.1em;
  color:inherit;
  -webkit-box-decoration-break:clone;
  box-decoration-break:clone;
}
.ac-txt s{color:var(--mist);text-decoration:line-through}
.submit-row{margin-top:24px;text-align:center}
.submit-btn{padding:15px 54px;border-radius:18px;background:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--teal),var(--teal-mid));color:#fff;font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:17px;font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;border:none;transition:var(--tr);box-shadow:0 10px 24px rgba(26,122,109,.24);letter-spacing:.15px}
.submit-btn:hover{transform:translateY(-2px);box-shadow:0 16px 30px rgba(26,122,109,.3)}
.submit-btn:disabled{opacity:.25;cursor:not-allowed;transform:none;box-shadow:none}
.exb{margin-top:26px;border-radius:22px;overflow:hidden;display:none;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);background:#fff}
.exb.vis{display:block;animation:fadeUp .38s cubic-bezier(.4,0,.2,1)}
@keyframes fadeUp{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(8px)}to{opacity:1;transform:translateY(0)}}
.exs{padding:20px 24px}.exs+.exs{border-top:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.06)}
.exs.exc{background:var(--green-bg);border-left:5px solid var(--green)}
.exs.exw{background:var(--red-bg);border-left:5px solid var(--red)}
.exs.exo{background:var(--amber-bg);border-left:5px solid var(--amber)}
.exs.exe{background:var(--teal-light);border-left:5px solid var(--teal)}
.exl{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1.8px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:10px}
.exs.exc .exl{color:var(--green)}.exs.exw .exl{color:var(--red)}.exs.exo .exl{color:var(--amber)}.exs.exe .exl{color:var(--teal)}
.ext{font-size:calc(var(--choice-size) * .97);line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink2)}
.wce{margin-top:10px;padding-top:10px;border-top:1px solid rgba(196,64,64,.12)}
.wce strong{color:var(--red);font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;font-size:12.5px}
.qnv{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:14px;padding:16px 24px 20px;border-top:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);background:linear-gradient(180deg,color-mix(in srgb,var(--warm) 32%,transparent),color-mix(in srgb,var(--cream) 82%,transparent))}
.nb{padding:12px 22px;border:1.8px solid rgba(27,42,74,.2);background:#fff;border-radius:16px;cursor:pointer;font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:15px;font-weight:700;color:var(--navy);transition:var(--tr);box-shadow:0 8px 18px rgba(18,32,60,.06)}
.nb:hover{background:var(--navy);color:#fff;transform:translateY(-1px);border-color:var(--navy)}
.nb.pr{background:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--navy),#2A3562);color:#fff;border-color:transparent}
.nb.pr:hover{background:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--navy2),var(--navy))}
.nb:disabled{opacity:.22;cursor:not-allowed;transform:none!important;box-shadow:none}
.nav-dots{display:flex;gap:5px;flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:center;flex:1}
.qd{width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:rgba(18,32,60,.1);cursor:pointer;border:none;padding:0;transition:var(--tr)}
.qd:hover{background:rgba(18,32,60,.28);transform:scale(1.25)}
.qd.on{background:var(--teal-glow);box-shadow:0 0 0 3px rgba(62,196,178,.22)}
.qd.dc{background:var(--green)}.qd.di{background:var(--red)}
.qd.asel{background:rgba(18,32,60,.46)}
.qd.mk{box-shadow:0 0 0 2px rgba(192,123,26,.28)}

/* NBME / NBOME VIEW */
.nb-wrap{height:var(--app-vh);min-height:var(--app-vh);display:flex;flex-direction:column;overflow:hidden;background:#0d1117}
.nb-util{flex-shrink:0;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:#0d1117;color:#fff}
.nb-util-name{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:var(--ui-title-size);line-height:1;letter-spacing:-.1px;font-weight:700}
.nb-util-actions{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px}
.nb-util-btn{padding:8px 12px;border-radius:10px;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.16);background:rgba(255,255,255,.06);color:#fff;font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:var(--ui-control-size);font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;transition:var(--tr)}
.nb-util-btn:hover{background:rgba(255,255,255,.14);transform:translateY(-1px)}
.nb-app{flex:1;min-height:0;width:100%;display:grid;grid-template-rows:auto minmax(0,1fr) auto;background:#fff;overflow:hidden}
.nb-header,.nb-footer{background:#004976;color:#fff;border-color:#003653;user-select:none}
.nb-header{min-height:78px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:190px 1fr auto;align-items:stretch;border-bottom:3px solid #003653}
.nb-status-box{padding:10px 14px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;border-right:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.28);line-height:1.15}
.nb-status-box strong{font-size:calc(var(--ui-title-size) * .66)}
.nb-status-box span{margin-top:6px;font-size:calc(var(--ui-meta-size) * 1.08);color:rgba(255,255,255,.82)}
.nb-header-center{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;padding:8px 12px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.nb-action-btn,.nb-mode-pill{min-height:52px;padding:6px 11px;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.5);border-radius:3px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:6px;background:transparent;color:#fff;cursor:pointer;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:var(--ui-control-size);font-weight:700;line-height:1.2;transition:background .16s ease,border-color .16s ease}
.nb-action-btn:hover,.nb-mode-pill:hover,.nb-mode-pill.active,.nb-action-btn.active{background:#356f94}
.nb-action-btn:disabled,.nb-mode-pill:disabled{opacity:.45;cursor:default;background:transparent}
.nb-action-btn:disabled:hover,.nb-mode-pill:disabled:hover{background:transparent}
.nb-action-btn.active.hl{background:#f5c84b;border-color:#fff;color:#17212b}
.nb-action-btn.active.st{background:#f9d8de;border-color:#fff;color:#7d1830}
.nb-item-picker{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;white-space:nowrap}
.nb-item-picker label{font-size:calc(var(--ui-meta-size) * 1.02);opacity:.86}
.nb-item-picker select{min-width:68px;padding:7px 5px;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.25);background:#fff;color:#111;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:var(--ui-control-size)}
.nb-nav-controls{display:flex;align-items:stretch}
.nb-nav-btn{min-width:88px;border:0;border-left:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.22);background:transparent;color:#fff;padding:8px 10px;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;cursor:pointer;transition:background .16s ease}
.nb-nav-btn:hover{background:#356f94}
.nb-nav-btn:disabled{opacity:.45;cursor:default;background:transparent}
.nb-nav-btn .icon{display:block;font-size:1.4rem;margin-bottom:4px}
.nb-body{min-height:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:84px minmax(0,1fr);background:#fff;overflow:hidden}
.nb-rail{min-height:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;padding:7px 6px;background:#e8edf1;border-right:1px solid #8b969f;overflow:hidden}
.nb-rail-title{margin:3px 0 1px;color:#35424e;text-align:center;font-size:calc(var(--ui-micro-size) * 1.12);font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.04em}
.nb-rail-scroll{flex:1;min-height:0;overflow-y:auto;overscroll-behavior:contain;padding-right:1px;scrollbar-width:thin;scrollbar-color:#8b969f #dce4ea}
.nb-rail-scroll::-webkit-scrollbar{width:8px}
.nb-rail-scroll::-webkit-scrollbar-track{background:#dce4ea;border-radius:999px}
.nb-rail-scroll::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb{background:#8b969f;border-radius:999px}
.nb-rail-arrow{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;width:100%;height:28px;border:1px solid #8d98a1;border-radius:3px;background:#f7fafc;color:#22313d;font-size:.9rem;font-weight:900;cursor:pointer;transition:background .16s ease,border-color .16s ease,color .16s ease}
.nb-rail-arrow:hover:not(:disabled){background:#d9e5ee;border-color:#647280}
.nb-rail-arrow:disabled{opacity:.38;cursor:default}
.nb-rail-btn{width:100%;height:35px;margin-bottom:5px;position:relative;border:1px solid #8d98a1;border-radius:2px;background:#fff;color:#1d2933;font-size:calc(var(--ui-control-size) * 1.06);font-weight:700;cursor:pointer}
.nb-rail-btn:hover{background:#f4f8fb}
.nb-rail-btn.current{background:#004976;color:#fff;border-color:#004976}
.nb-rail-btn.incorrect{color:var(--red);border-color:#bd6570;background:#fff8f8}
.nb-rail-btn.current.incorrect{color:var(--red);background:#fff8f8;border-color:#a51d2d;box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 2px rgba(165,29,45,.18)}
.nb-rail-btn.answered::after{content:'';position:absolute;right:4px;bottom:4px;width:7px;height:7px;border-radius:50%;background:#257a43;box-shadow:0 0 0 1px #fff}
.nb-rail-btn.incorrect.answered::after{background:#a51d2d}
.nb-rail-btn.marked::before{content:'⚑';position:absolute;left:3px;top:1px;color:#bd7400;font-size:.78rem}
.nb-rail-btn.current.marked::before{color:#ffd75e}
.nb-stage{position:relative;display:flex;flex-direction:column;min-width:0;min-height:0;overflow:hidden;background:#fff}
#nbFinishBanner{flex-shrink:0}
.nb-question-scroll{flex:1;min-height:0;overflow:auto;overscroll-behavior:contain;padding:26px clamp(24px,6vw,92px) 112px}
.nb-question-card,.nb-review-wrap{width:min(980px,100%);margin:0 auto}
.nb-question-card.has-feedback{width:min(1580px,100%)}
.nb-question-layout{display:grid;gap:24px}
.nb-question-layout.has-feedback{grid-template-columns:minmax(0,.95fr) minmax(0,1.05fr);align-items:start}
.nb-question-side{min-width:0;align-self:start}
.nb-question-side .exb{margin-top:0;max-height:calc(var(--app-vh) - clamp(260px,34vh,380px));overflow:auto;overscroll-behavior:auto;scroll-padding-bottom:clamp(88px,12vh,136px);scrollbar-width:thin;scrollbar-color:rgba(18,32,60,.28) rgba(18,32,60,.08)}
.nb-question-side .exb::-webkit-scrollbar{width:10px}
.nb-question-side .exb::-webkit-scrollbar-track{background:rgba(18,32,60,.08);border-radius:999px}
.nb-question-side .exb::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb{background:rgba(18,32,60,.28);border-radius:999px}
.nb-question-side .exb::after{content:'';display:block;height:clamp(88px,12vh,136px);background:transparent}
.nb-question-label{margin-bottom:16px;color:#5b6570;font-weight:700;font-size:calc(var(--ui-meta-size) * 1.16);letter-spacing:.02em}
.nb-question-stem{font-size:calc(var(--stem-size) * .72);line-height:1.58;user-select:text;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere}
.nb-question-stem mark,.nb-answer-copy mark{
  background:linear-gradient(180deg,transparent 16%,var(--mark-color,#fff173) 16%,var(--mark-color,#fff173) 86%,transparent 86%);
  color:inherit;
  padding:0;
  margin:0;
  border-radius:.08em;
  -webkit-box-decoration-break:clone;
  box-decoration-break:clone;
}
.nb-question-stem s,.nb-answer-copy s{color:#717a82;text-decoration:line-through}
.nb-stage-note{margin-top:18px;color:#5b6570;font-size:calc(var(--ui-meta-size) * 1.12);line-height:1.5}
.nb-answers{margin-top:24px;display:grid;gap:11px}
.nb-answer-row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:32px 1fr;align-items:start;gap:9px;padding:8px 9px;border:1px solid transparent;border-radius:3px;cursor:pointer;transition:background .16s ease,border-color .16s ease}
.nb-answer-row:hover{background:#f3f7fa;border-color:#d7e0e7}
.nb-answer-row.selected{background:#d9ebf7;border-color:#7fa9c4}
.nb-answer-row.correct{background:#dff2e5;border-color:#4d9b68}
.nb-answer-row.incorrect{background:#f8dfe2;border-color:#bd6570}
.nb-answer-row.locked{cursor:default}
.nb-answer-glyph{width:24px;height:24px;border:1px solid #8d98a1;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;font-size:calc(var(--ui-control-size) * 1.05);font-weight:700;background:#fff;color:#1d2933;margin-top:1px}
.nb-answer-row.selected .nb-answer-glyph{background:#004976;border-color:#004976;color:#fff}
.nb-answer-row.correct .nb-answer-glyph{background:#257a43;border-color:#257a43;color:#fff}
.nb-answer-row.incorrect .nb-answer-glyph{background:#a51d2d;border-color:#a51d2d;color:#fff}
.nb-answer-copy{font-size:calc(var(--choice-size) * 1.02);line-height:1.52;user-select:text}
.nb-submit-row{margin-top:24px}
.nb-submit-btn{min-width:165px;padding:10px 18px;border:1px solid #003653;border-radius:3px;background:#004976;color:#fff;font-weight:700;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;cursor:pointer}
.nb-submit-btn:disabled{opacity:.55;cursor:default}
.nb-review-wrap h2{color:#004976;font-size:1.55rem;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif}
.nb-review-wrap p{font-size:calc(var(--choice-size) * .95);line-height:1.5;color:#33404a}
.nb-review-summary{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:14px;margin:16px 0 24px}
.nb-summary-chip{min-width:145px;padding:12px 15px;border:1px solid #aeb8c0;border-radius:3px;background:#f3f6f8}
.nb-summary-chip strong{display:block;margin-top:5px;font-size:calc(var(--ui-title-size) * .82);color:#004976}
.nb-review-table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse}
.nb-review-table th,.nb-review-table td{padding:11px;border:1px solid #aeb8c0;text-align:left;font-size:calc(var(--choice-size) * .9)}
.nb-review-table th{background:#004976;color:#fff}
.nb-review-table tr:nth-child(even){background:#f3f6f8}
.nb-review-jump{color:#004976;text-decoration:underline;border:0;background:transparent;font-weight:700;padding:0;cursor:pointer}
.nb-review-actions{display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:18px}
.nb-review-back{padding:10px 16px;border:1px solid #8d99a3;border-radius:3px;background:#f3f5f7;color:#17212b;font-weight:700;cursor:pointer}
.nb-footer{min-height:72px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:auto 1fr auto;align-items:stretch;border-top:3px solid #003653}
.nb-timer-box{min-width:245px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr auto;gap:4px 14px;align-content:center;padding:10px 18px;border-right:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.28);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.nb-timer-box strong{text-align:right}
.nb-timer-box small{grid-column:1 / -1;text-align:center;opacity:.82;font-size:.68rem}
.nb-timer-actions{grid-column:1 / -1;display:flex;gap:8px;justify-content:center;margin-top:2px}
.nb-timer-mini{padding:7px 12px;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.75);border-radius:3px;background:transparent;color:#fff;font-weight:700;cursor:pointer}
.nb-timer-mini:hover{background:#356f94}
.nb-timer-box.warn strong{color:#ffd45b}
.nb-footer-center{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:10px;padding:8px 12px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.nb-score-chip{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.2);border-radius:3px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.08);font-size:calc(var(--ui-control-size) * .98)}
.nb-score-chip span{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:4px;white-space:nowrap}
.nb-score-chip strong{font-size:calc(var(--ui-control-size) * 1.16)}
.nb-font-cluster{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;padding:8px 10px;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.2);border-radius:3px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.08)}
.nb-font-cluster label{font-size:calc(var(--ui-meta-size) * 1.02);font-weight:700}
.nb-font-btn{padding:7px 10px;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.72);border-radius:3px;background:transparent;color:#fff;font-size:var(--ui-control-size);font-weight:700;cursor:pointer}
.nb-font-btn:hover{background:#356f94}
.nb-footer-tool{padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.72);border-radius:3px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.08);color:#fff;font-size:var(--ui-control-size);font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif}
.nb-footer-tool:hover{background:#356f94}
.nb-end-wrap{display:flex;align-items:center;padding:10px 13px}
.nb-end-btn{padding:0 20px;min-height:44px;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.8);border-radius:3px;background:#7d1830;color:#fff;font-size:var(--ui-control-size);font-weight:700;cursor:pointer}
.nb-end-btn:hover:not(:disabled){background:#a82745}
.nb-end-btn:disabled{opacity:.45;cursor:default;background:rgba(255,255,255,.14)}

/* Lab values */
.lab-modal{position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:1200;display:none;align-items:center;justify-content:center;padding:24px;background:rgba(9,17,28,.58);backdrop-filter:blur(9px)}
.lab-modal.open{display:flex}
.lab-shell{width:min(1100px,96vw);max-height:92vh}
.lab-card{display:flex;flex-direction:column;max-height:92vh;border-radius:26px;background:linear-gradient(180deg,rgba(255,255,255,.98),rgba(246,243,236,.98));border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);box-shadow:0 28px 80px rgba(0,0,0,.22);overflow:hidden}
.lab-header{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;justify-content:space-between;gap:18px;padding:22px 24px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08)}
.lab-kicker{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:1.3px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--mist);margin-bottom:8px;font-weight:700}
.lab-title{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:calc(var(--stem-size) * 1.02);line-height:1.08;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 8px;font-weight:700}
.lab-sub{font-size:calc(var(--choice-size) * .92);line-height:1.6;color:var(--slate);max-width:68ch}
.lab-close{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;min-height:44px;padding:0 16px;border-radius:14px;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.12);background:#fff;color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:14px;font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;transition:var(--tr)}
.lab-close:hover{transform:translateY(-1px);border-color:rgba(26,122,109,.34);color:var(--teal)}
.lab-controls{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap;padding:16px 24px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);background:rgba(255,255,255,.72)}
.lab-toggle{display:inline-flex;gap:8px;padding:6px;border-radius:999px;background:rgba(18,32,60,.05)}
.lab-toggle-btn{padding:10px 16px;border:none;border-radius:999px;background:transparent;color:var(--slate);font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:calc(var(--choice-size) * .86);font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;transition:var(--tr)}
.lab-toggle-btn.active{background:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--navy),#2A3562);color:#fff;box-shadow:0 10px 22px rgba(27,42,74,.16)}
.lab-note{font-size:calc(var(--choice-size) * .78);line-height:1.6;color:var(--mist)}
.lab-body{padding:18px 24px 24px;overflow:auto}
.lab-table{width:100%;margin-bottom:18px;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:calc(var(--choice-size) * .92);background:#fff}
.lab-table thead th{padding:9px 10px;background:#dbe6ed;border:1px solid #9eabb4;text-align:left}
.lab-table thead th:first-child{width:48%}
.lab-table tbody td{padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid #c6cdd3;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}
.lab-table tbody tr:nth-child(even):not(.lab-group){background:#f6f8f9}
.lab-table .lab-group td{padding:8px 9px;background:#edf1f4;font-weight:700}
.lab-table .continuation{color:transparent}

/* finish overlay */
.fin{position:relative;background:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--navy),#2A3562);border-radius:24px;padding:22px 24px;text-align:center;color:#fff;box-shadow:var(--sh3);margin:0 auto 14px;max-width:min(780px,100%);transition:padding .2s ease,box-shadow .2s ease}
.fin.min{padding:56px 24px 22px}
.fin-toggle{position:absolute;top:14px;left:14px;display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;min-width:40px;height:34px;padding:0 12px;border-radius:12px;border:1.5px solid rgba(255,255,255,.25);background:rgba(255,255,255,.04);color:rgba(255,255,255,.86);font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:18px;font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;transition:all .18s}
.fin-toggle:hover{background:rgba(255,255,255,.12);color:#fff}
.fin-main{display:grid;justify-items:center}
.fin-pct{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:46px;font-weight:700;color:var(--teal-glow);line-height:1}
.fin-pct-lbl{font-size:11px;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:rgba(255,255,255,.45);margin:4px 0 14px}
.fin-msg{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:24px;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:6px}
.fin-sub{font-size:13px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.6);margin-bottom:18px}
.fin-btns{display:flex;gap:10px;justify-content:center;flex-wrap:wrap}
.fin-btn{padding:10px 16px;border-radius:12px;border:1.5px solid rgba(255,255,255,.25);background:transparent;color:rgba(255,255,255,.8);font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:13px;font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;transition:all .18s}
.fin-btn:hover{background:rgba(255,255,255,.12);color:#fff}

/* final review sheet */
.sum-sheet{margin-top:18px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.96);border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);border-radius:28px;box-shadow:var(--sh3);overflow:hidden;scroll-margin-top:20px}
.sum-head{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;justify-content:space-between;gap:18px;padding:26px 28px 22px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);background:linear-gradient(180deg,color-mix(in srgb,var(--warm) 48%,transparent),#fff)}
.sum-kicker{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1.3px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--mist);margin-bottom:8px}
.sum-title{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:34px;line-height:1.08;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 8px;font-weight:700}
.sum-sub{font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--slate);max-width:66ch}
.sum-actions{display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.sum-btn{padding:11px 16px;border-radius:14px;border:1.5px solid rgba(18,32,60,.12);background:#fff;color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:14px;font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;transition:var(--tr);white-space:nowrap}
.sum-btn:hover{transform:translateY(-1px);border-color:rgba(26,122,109,.34);color:var(--teal)}
.sum-btn.primary{background:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--teal),var(--teal-mid));border-color:transparent;color:#fff;box-shadow:0 10px 24px rgba(26,122,109,.2)}
.sum-btn.primary:hover{color:#fff;box-shadow:0 14px 28px rgba(26,122,109,.26)}
.sum-stats{display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;padding:18px 28px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);background:rgba(246,243,236,.72)}
.sum-stat{min-width:150px;padding:14px 16px;border-radius:18px;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);background:#fff;box-shadow:0 8px 20px rgba(18,32,60,.05)}
.sum-stat-label{font-size:10px;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:1.6px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--mist);margin-bottom:8px}
.sum-stat-value{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:32px;line-height:1;color:var(--navy);font-weight:700}
.sum-list{display:grid;gap:14px;padding:22px 24px 26px}
.sum-item{display:grid;grid-template-columns:150px minmax(0,1fr);gap:18px;padding:20px;border-radius:22px;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.08);background:#fff;box-shadow:0 8px 22px rgba(18,32,60,.05)}
.sum-item.correct{border-color:rgba(45,134,89,.24);background:linear-gradient(180deg,#fff,#f6fcf8)}
.sum-item.incorrect{border-color:rgba(196,64,64,.18);background:linear-gradient(180deg,#fff,#fff8f8)}
.sum-item.unanswered{border-color:rgba(192,123,26,.24);background:linear-gradient(180deg,#fff,#fffaf1)}
.sum-meta{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px}
.sum-num{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;min-height:42px;padding:0 16px;border-radius:999px;background:rgba(18,32,60,.06);font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);width:max-content}
.sum-state{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;min-height:34px;padding:0 14px;border-radius:999px;font-size:12px;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;width:max-content}
.sum-item.correct .sum-state{background:var(--green-bg);color:var(--green)}
.sum-item.incorrect .sum-state{background:var(--red-bg);color:var(--red)}
.sum-item.unanswered .sum-state{background:var(--amber-bg);color:var(--amber)}
.sum-answer{font-size:13px;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--slate)}
.sum-body{display:grid;gap:14px}
.sum-section{padding:16px 18px;border-radius:18px;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.06);background:rgba(246,243,236,.56)}
.sum-label{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:1.7px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--mist);margin-bottom:9px}
.sum-text{font-size:calc(var(--choice-size) * .97);line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink2)}
.sum-view{display:grid;gap:18px}

/* responsive */
/* iPad portrait & narrow laptops — stack feedback next-to layouts before they cramp */
@media(max-width:1180px){
  .q-review-grid.has-feedback,.nb-question-layout.has-feedback{grid-template-columns:1fr}
  .q-feedback .exb,.nb-question-side .exb{max-height:none;overflow:visible}
  .qin{grid-template-columns:clamp(230px,24vw,290px) minmax(0,1fr);gap:16px 18px}
}
@media(max-width:820px){
  .qin{grid-template-columns:1fr;padding:16px 14px 64px}
  .sidebar{position:static;flex-direction:row;flex-wrap:wrap}
  .score-panel{flex:1 1 200px}
  .mode-toggle,.tool-panel{flex:1 1 100%}
  .timer-card{flex:1 1 160px}
  .nb-header{grid-template-columns:1fr}
  .nb-status-box{border-right:none;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.2)}
  .nb-nav-controls{border-top:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.18)}
  .nb-nav-btn{flex:1;min-height:52px}
  .nb-nav-btn .icon{display:inline;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 6px 0 0}
  /* rail becomes a horizontal question strip instead of disappearing */
  .nb-body{grid-template-columns:1fr;grid-template-rows:auto minmax(0,1fr)}
  .nb-rail{flex-direction:row;align-items:center;gap:4px;padding:6px 8px;border-right:none;border-bottom:1px solid #8b969f}
  .nb-rail-title{display:none}
  .nb-rail-arrow{display:none}
  .nb-rail-scroll{flex:1;overflow-y:hidden;overflow-x:auto;padding:2px 0;scrollbar-width:thin}
  #nbRailItems{display:flex;flex-direction:row;gap:5px;width:max-content}
  .nb-rail-btn{width:44px;min-width:44px;height:38px;margin-bottom:0}
  .nb-question-scroll{padding:18px clamp(14px,4vw,32px) 96px}
  .nb-footer{grid-template-columns:1fr}
  .nb-timer-box{border-right:none;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.18)}
  .nb-end-wrap{justify-content:center}
  .setup-ui-toggle{grid-template-columns:1fr}
}
@media(max-width:480px){.qbd{padding:20px 16px}.qstem{font-size:max(21px,var(--stem-size))}.qnv{flex-direction:column;padding:14px 16px 18px}.nb{width:100%}.font-tools{flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch}.font-tools-group{margin-left:0;justify-content:space-between}.sum-head{padding:22px 18px 18px;flex-direction:column}.sum-title{font-size:28px}.sum-stats{padding:16px 18px}.sum-list{padding:18px 16px 22px}.sum-item{grid-template-columns:1fr;padding:16px}.cl{width:36px;height:36px;font-size:13.5px}.ac{gap:10px;padding:13px 14px}}
@media(max-width:560px){.setup-actions{flex-direction:column}.qt-bar{height:auto;flex-wrap:wrap;padding:12px 16px}.nb-util{flex-direction:column;align-items:flex-start}}

/* touch devices — comfortable hit targets everywhere */
@media(pointer:coarse){
  .qt-btn,.nb-util-btn,.nb-action-btn,.nb-mode-pill,.nb-footer-tool,.nb-font-btn,.nb-timer-mini,.font-btn,.q-tool,.paste-tab{min-height:44px}
  .nb-answer-row{padding:12px 11px;gap:11px}
  .nb-rail-btn{min-height:42px}
  .qd{width:14px;height:14px}
  .lab-toggle-btn{min-height:44px}
}

/* big screens — 16" MBP maximized, 4K monitors, TVs: widen the stage and let type scale */
@media(min-width:2000px){
  .qin{width:min(2400px,calc(100vw - 48px));gap:22px 34px;grid-template-columns:clamp(300px,17vw,400px) minmax(0,1fr)}
  .nb-question-card,.nb-review-wrap{width:min(1240px,100%)}
  .nb-question-card.has-feedback{width:min(2000px,100%)}
  .nb-body{grid-template-columns:104px minmax(0,1fr)}
  .nb-rail-btn{height:44px}
  .nb-header{min-height:88px}
  .nb-footer{min-height:82px}
  .qd{width:13px;height:13px}
  .setup-card{width:min(820px,100%)}
}
@media(min-width:3000px){
  .qin{width:min(2900px,calc(100vw - 64px))}
  .nb-question-card,.nb-review-wrap{width:min(1500px,100%)}
  .nb-question-card.has-feedback{width:min(2500px,100%)}
  .nb-body{grid-template-columns:120px minmax(0,1fr)}
}

/* notch / home-indicator safe areas (iPhone, iPad) */
.nb-footer{padding-bottom:env(safe-area-inset-bottom,0)}
.nb-util{padding-left:max(18px,env(safe-area-inset-left,0));padding-right:max(18px,env(safe-area-inset-right,0))}
.qt-bar{padding-left:max(22px,env(safe-area-inset-left,0));padding-right:max(22px,env(safe-area-inset-right,0))}

/* timer display-mode + expiry */
.timer-lbl{cursor:pointer;user-select:none;transition:background .15s}
.timer-lbl:hover{background:rgba(18,32,60,.1)}
.nb-timer-mode{padding:0;border:0;background:transparent;color:inherit;font:inherit;cursor:pointer;text-align:left;text-decoration:underline dotted rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:3px}
.timer-card.expired,.nb-timer-box.expired strong{animation:timerBlink 1s steps(2,start) 6}
.nb-timer-box.expired strong{color:#ff8f8f}
.timer-card.expired{border-color:rgba(196,64,64,.6);box-shadow:0 0 0 4px rgba(196,64,64,.12),var(--sh2)}
@keyframes timerBlink{50%{opacity:.35}}

@media(max-width:600px){.lab-head{display:none}.lab-row{grid-template-columns:1fr auto;gap:2px 12px}.lab-value{text-align:right;white-space:normal}.lab-reference{grid-column:1/-1;text-align:left;white-space:normal}.lab-reference::before{content:'Reference: ';font-weight:700}.lab-note{font-size:.68em}}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce){
  *,*::before,*::after{animation:none !important;transition:none !important}
}

/* toast */
.toast-wrap{position:fixed;bottom:24px;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);z-index:999;pointer-events:none}
.toast{padding:11px 20px;border-radius:999px;font-size:13.5px;font-weight:700;box-shadow:0 8px 28px rgba(0,0,0,.15);animation:toastIn .28s cubic-bezier(.2,.8,.2,1) both;white-space:nowrap}
@keyframes toastIn{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(14px) scale(.94)}to{opacity:1;transform:translateY(0) scale(1)}}
@keyframes toastOut{to{opacity:0;transform:translateY(8px) scale(.95)}}
.toast.ok{background:var(--navy);color:var(--teal-glow)}.toast.err{background:var(--red);color:#fff}

.annotation-menu{position:fixed;z-index:10000;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;padding:10px 12px;background:#242424;border-radius:9px;box-shadow:0 6px 24px #0004}
.annotation-menu[hidden]{display:none}
.annotation-swatch{width:28px;height:28px;border:2px solid transparent;border-radius:50%;cursor:pointer;flex-shrink:0}
.annotation-swatch[aria-pressed="true"]{outline:2px solid white;outline-offset:2px}
.annotation-menu button:focus-visible{outline:2px solid white;outline-offset:3px}
.annotation-strike{border:0;border-left:1px solid #777;background:transparent;color:white;padding:5px 9px;font-size:16px;cursor:pointer;text-decoration:line-through}
.highlight-color-button{width:28px;height:28px;flex-shrink:0;align-self:center;border:2px solid #777;border-radius:50%;background:var(--active-highlight,#fff173);cursor:pointer}


/* Reading themes and study tools */
:root{--reading-accent:#1a7a6d;--reading-selected:#d9ebf7}
:root[data-theme="dark"]{color-scheme:dark;--cream:#141a20;--warm:#202932;--white:#1b232b;--ink:#e4e9ed;--ink2:#cbd4db;--slate:#aebbc5;--mist:#a4b3bf;--rule:#46535f;--teal:#78c9ba;--teal-deep:#9cded1;--teal-light:#223c3a;--green:#89d5a4;--green-bg:#233c30;--red:#ffaaaa;--red-bg:#452c32;--amber:#e7bf7a;--amber-bg:#3d3528;--gold-bg:#3d3528;--reading-accent:#9bd4e8;--reading-selected:#294454}
:root[data-theme="soft"]{color-scheme:light;--cream:#e6dfd1;--warm:#eee7d9;--white:#f3eddf;--ink:#393e35;--ink2:#4c5348;--slate:#60685b;--mist:#697260;--rule:#bbb7a5;--teal:#496e58;--teal-deep:#3e604a;--teal-light:#dfe7d8;--green:#376047;--green-bg:#e0e8d9;--red:#943f3b;--red-bg:#f0dfd4;--amber:#795823;--amber-bg:#ece2cc;--gold-bg:#ece2cc;--reading-accent:#45644d;--reading-selected:#d6dfcc}
:root[data-theme] :is(.nb-app,.nb-body,.nb-stage,.nb-answer-glyph,.nb-rail-btn,.cl,.exb,.sum-sheet,.sum-item,.sum-stat,.lab-card,.lab-table,.mode-toggle,.timer-card,.tool-panel,.font-tools,.score-box,.q-tool,.font-btn,.tbtn,.nb,.ghost-btn,.paste-tab,.lab-close,.sum-btn){background:var(--white);color:var(--ink);border-color:var(--rule)}
:root[data-theme] :is(.nb-rail,.nb-rail-arrow,.nb-summary-chip,.nb-review-back,.sum-head,.sum-stats,.sum-section,.lab-controls,.lab-table thead th,.lab-table .lab-group td,.lab-table tbody tr:nth-child(even):not(.lab-group),.nb-review-table tr:nth-child(even)){background:var(--warm);color:var(--ink);border-color:var(--rule)}
:root[data-theme] :is(.nb-question-label,.nb-stage-note,.nb-rail-title,.nb-review-wrap p){color:var(--slate)}
:root[data-theme] :is(.sv,.timer-time,.sum-num,.sum-title,.sum-stat-value,.lab-title,.nb-review-wrap h2,.nb-summary-chip strong,.nb-review-jump){color:var(--reading-accent)}
:root[data-theme] :is(.inp,.ta):focus{background:var(--white)}
:root[data-theme] :is(.nb-answer-row:hover,.ac:hover:not(.dis)){background:var(--warm);border-color:var(--rule)}
:root[data-theme] :is(.ac.sel,.nb-answer-row.selected,.nb-rail-btn.current){background:var(--reading-selected)!important;color:var(--ink);border-color:var(--reading-accent)!important}
:root[data-theme] :is(.ac.cor,.nb-answer-row.correct,.sum-item.correct,.score-box.correct){background:var(--green-bg)!important;border-color:var(--green)!important}
:root[data-theme] :is(.ac.inc,.nb-answer-row.incorrect,.sum-item.incorrect,.score-box.incorrect,.nb-rail-btn.incorrect){background:var(--red-bg)!important;border-color:var(--red)!important}
:root[data-theme="dark"] .ac:is(.sel,.cor,.inc) .cl{color:#17212b!important}
:root[data-theme] mark{color:#17212b!important}
:root[data-theme] :is(.nb-header,.nb-footer){background:#253c45;border-color:var(--rule)}
:root[data-theme="soft"] :is(.nb-header,.nb-footer){background:#4a5c4b}
:root[data-theme] :is(.nb-review-table td,.lab-table td,.lab-table th){border-color:var(--rule)}
.reading-theme{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;flex-wrap:wrap}
.reading-theme select{padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:8px;background:var(--white);color:var(--ink);font:inherit;max-width:100%}
.setup-body>.reading-theme{margin-bottom:22px}
.tool-panel>.reading-theme{padding:6px;flex-basis:100%}

.study-notes-text{white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere}
/* Compact, theme-matched chrome and movable study windows. */
:root{--chrome:#163a50;--chrome-text:#f3f7f9;--chrome-border:#527184;--tool-accent:#236d68;--tool-on-accent:#fff;--tool-hover:#e4eeeb}
:root[data-theme="dark"]{--chrome:#222e37;--chrome-text:#dce5ea;--chrome-border:#53626d;--tool-accent:#86c8b7;--tool-on-accent:#162b25;--tool-hover:#304139}
:root[data-theme="soft"]{color-scheme:light;--cream:#ccd3ca;--warm:#d5dcd2;--white:#e0e5dc;--ink:#29352f;--ink2:#35443b;--slate:#4c5d52;--mist:#506155;--rule:#9ba99c;--teal:#3c6654;--teal-deep:#315342;--teal-light:#cbdacb;--green:#315d3c;--green-bg:#c7dac4;--red:#853e3e;--red-bg:#e3ceca;--amber:#755322;--amber-bg:#ded5bc;--gold-bg:#ded5bc;--reading-accent:#355e4e;--reading-selected:#c1d4c4;--chrome:#bdc9bc;--chrome-text:#293d31;--chrome-border:#8c9e8e;--tool-accent:#3c6654;--tool-on-accent:#f0f4ed;--tool-hover:#c7d5c7}
.nb-util{min-height:38px;padding:5px 14px;gap:10px;background:var(--chrome);color:var(--chrome-text);border-bottom:1px solid var(--chrome-border)}
.nb-util-name{font-size:16px;line-height:1.25;min-width:0;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;white-space:nowrap}
.nb-util-actions{gap:5px;flex-shrink:0}
.nb-util-btn{padding:5px 9px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;color:inherit;border-color:var(--chrome-border);background:transparent}
:root .nb-header,:root .nb-footer,:root .qt-bar,:root[data-theme] .nb-header,:root[data-theme] .nb-footer,:root[data-theme] .qt-bar{background:var(--chrome);color:var(--chrome-text);border-color:var(--chrome-border)}
.nb-header-center .nb-action-btn,.nb-header-center .nb-mode-pill{min-height:36px;padding:6px 9px}
:root .nb-footer{border-top-width:1px}
:root :is(.nb-action-btn,.nb-mode-pill,.nb-nav-btn,.nb-timer-mini,.nb-font-btn,.nb-footer-tool,.nb-util-btn,.qt-btn){color:var(--chrome-text);border-color:var(--chrome-border)}
:root :is(.nb-action-btn:hover,.nb-mode-pill:hover,.nb-mode-pill.active,.nb-action-btn.active,.nb-nav-btn:hover,.nb-font-btn:hover,.nb-timer-mini:hover){background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--chrome-text) 13%,var(--chrome));color:var(--chrome-text)}
:root .nb-status-box span{color:var(--chrome-text);opacity:.85}
:root .nb-item-picker select{background:var(--white);color:var(--ink);border-color:var(--rule)}
:root[data-theme] :is(.qfig,.exs.exf){background:var(--white);border-color:var(--rule)}
:root[data-theme] .qfig-cap{color:var(--slate)}
:root[data-theme] .sum-item.unanswered{background:var(--amber-bg)}
.study-toolbar{display:flex;align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px}
.study-toolbar .reading-theme{font-size:12px;gap:6px;flex-wrap:nowrap;padding:0}
.study-toolbar .reading-theme select{padding:7px 8px;max-width:158px;font-size:12px;border-radius:7px}
.study-tool-button{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:6px;min-height:33px;padding:7px 10px;border:1px solid var(--chrome-border);border-radius:7px;background:transparent;color:var(--chrome-text);font:600 12px var(--font-ui);cursor:pointer;white-space:nowrap}
.study-tool-button:hover{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--chrome-text) 12%,transparent)}
.study-tool-button[aria-pressed="true"]{background:var(--reading-selected);color:var(--ink);border-color:var(--reading-accent)}
.study-tool-button.has-note::after{content:'';width:6px;height:6px;border-radius:50%;background:currentColor}
.tool-panel>.study-toolbar{flex-basis:100%;padding:6px;color:var(--ink)}
.tool-panel .study-tool-button{color:var(--ink);border-color:var(--rule);background:var(--warm)}
.study-window{position:fixed;inset:74px 22px auto auto;margin:0;width:min(322px,calc(100vw - 24px));max-height:calc(100dvh - 24px);padding:0;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:15px;overflow:auto;background:var(--white);color:var(--ink);box-shadow:0 16px 48px #0004;font-family:var(--font-ui);z-index:1400}
.study-window.notes-window{width:min(410px,calc(100vw - 24px));inset:104px auto auto 24px}
.study-window-head{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:10px;position:sticky;top:0;padding:11px 14px;background:var(--warm);border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);cursor:grab;touch-action:none;user-select:none;z-index:1}
.study-window-head:active{cursor:grabbing}
.study-window-title{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:9px;font-size:14px;font-weight:700}
.study-drag-dots{color:var(--slate);letter-spacing:-3px;font-size:18px;padding-right:4px}
.study-window-close{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;width:28px;height:28px;padding:0;border:0;border-radius:6px;background:transparent;color:var(--ink);font:20px var(--font-ui);cursor:pointer}
.study-window-close:hover{background:var(--tool-hover)}
.study-window-body{padding:14px}
.study-window label{display:block;font-size:12px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:7px;color:var(--slate)}
.calc-display{padding:12px;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:10px;background:var(--warm);margin-bottom:12px}
.calc-history{min-height:19px;font:12px/1.4 var(--font-mono);color:var(--slate);text-align:right;overflow-wrap:anywhere}
.calc-display input{width:100%;border:0;background:transparent;color:var(--ink);font:28px/1.5 var(--font-mono);text-align:right;padding:4px 0;outline:none}
.calc-display:focus-within{outline:2px solid var(--reading-accent);outline-offset:2px}
.calculator-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr));gap:7px}
.calculator-grid button,.calc-parentheses button{border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:9px;background:var(--warm);color:var(--ink);font:500 19px var(--font-ui);min-height:43px;cursor:pointer;box-shadow:0 1px 1px #0001}
.calculator-grid button:hover,.calc-parentheses button:hover{background:var(--tool-hover)}
.calculator-grid .calc-operation{color:var(--reading-accent);background:var(--reading-selected);font-weight:700}
.calculator-grid .calc-equals{background:var(--tool-accent);color:var(--tool-on-accent);border-color:var(--tool-accent);font-weight:700}
.calculator-grid .calc-equals:hover{filter:brightness(.94)}
.calculator-grid .calc-clear{font-size:14px;font-weight:700}
.calc-parentheses{display:flex;gap:6px;margin-bottom:8px;align-items:center}
.calc-parentheses button{min-height:28px;width:36px;font-size:14px;border-radius:6px}
.calc-parentheses span{font-size:11px;color:var(--slate);margin-left:auto}
.calc-result{display:block;min-height:27px;margin-top:10px;font-size:12px;line-height:1.4;color:var(--slate);overflow-wrap:anywhere}
.calc-result[data-error="true"]{color:var(--red)}
.notes-window textarea{width:100%;min-height:210px;max-height:55vh;resize:vertical;padding:12px;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:9px;background:var(--warm);color:var(--ink);font:15px/1.65 var(--font-ui)}
.notes-window .notes-status{margin:8px 0 0;min-height:32px;color:var(--slate);font-size:12px;line-height:1.5}
.study-window :is(button,textarea):focus-visible,.study-window-head:focus-visible,.study-tool-button:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--reading-accent);outline-offset:2px}
@media(max-width:700px){.nb-util{flex-wrap:wrap;padding:5px 9px}.nb-util-name{font-size:14px}.nb-util-actions{gap:4px}.nb-util-btn{font-size:11px;padding:4px 6px}.nb-footer-center{padding:6px;gap:6px}.study-toolbar{gap:5px}.study-window{max-height:calc(100dvh - 16px)}}
@media print{.study-window,.study-toolbar{display:none!important}}


:root{
  /* Key Findings (post-submit vignette highlights) — case-note token palette */
  --vh-coral:#C85A3E;--vh-coral-bg:#F7E4DB;
  --vh-teal:#256B63;--vh-teal-bg:#DFEDE8;
  --vh-gold:#A47A1F;--vh-gold-bg:#F3E7C6;
  --vh-violet:#6B5390;--vh-violet-bg:#E9E2F1;
  --vh-sky:#2F6485;--vh-sky-bg:#DEE9EF;
}
/* ══ KEY FINDINGS — vignette highlights, revealed on submit ══ */
mark.vh{cursor:pointer;padding:0 .08em;border-radius:.2em;text-decoration:none;color:inherit;box-decoration-break:clone;-webkit-box-decoration-break:clone;border-bottom:2px solid transparent;transition:filter .15s;animation:vhFadeIn .35s ease both}
mark.vh:hover{filter:brightness(0.96)}
@keyframes vhFadeIn{from{background-color:transparent;border-bottom-color:transparent}}
mark.vh.vh-cat-finding{background:var(--vh-coral-bg);border-bottom-color:var(--vh-coral)}
mark.vh.vh-cat-mechanism{background:var(--vh-teal-bg);border-bottom-color:var(--vh-teal)}
mark.vh.vh-cat-term{background:var(--vh-gold-bg);border-bottom-color:var(--vh-gold)}
mark.vh.vh-cat-pattern{background:var(--vh-violet-bg);border-bottom-color:var(--vh-violet)}
mark.vh.vh-cat-workup{background:var(--vh-sky-bg);border-bottom-color:var(--vh-sky)}
.vh-legend{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;gap:8px 14px;margin:14px 0 0;padding:10px 14px;border-radius:10px;background:var(--warm);border:1px solid var(--rule);animation:vhFadeIn .35s ease both}
.vh-legend-label{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1.4px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--mist)}
.vh-legend-item{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;font-size:13.5px;font-weight:700;color:var(--ink2);letter-spacing:.02em}
.vh-legend-dot{width:11px;height:11px;border-radius:3px;flex-shrink:0}
.vh-legend-hint{font-size:13.5px;color:var(--slate);font-weight:400}
.vh-tooltip{position:fixed;z-index:900;max-width:380px;padding:14px 17px;border-radius:12px;background:var(--navy);color:#fff;font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:17px;line-height:1.6;box-shadow:0 14px 34px rgba(18,32,60,.28);opacity:0;pointer-events:none;transition:opacity .12s;transform:translateY(2px)}
.vh-tooltip.show{opacity:1}
.vh-tooltip .vht-eyebrow{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1.4px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--teal-glow);margin-bottom:4px}
.vh-tooltip .vht-title{font-weight:700;font-size:18px;margin-bottom:6px}
.vh-tooltip .vht-teaser{color:rgba(255,255,255,.86);font-size:16px}
.vh-panel{position:fixed;z-index:950;background:var(--white);box-shadow:0 -10px 40px rgba(18,32,60,.22);opacity:0;pointer-events:none;transition:transform .28s cubic-bezier(.4,0,.2,1),opacity .2s;display:flex;flex-direction:column;top:0;right:0;bottom:0;width:min(360px,86vw);border-left:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.1);transform:translateX(16px);padding:22px 22px 26px}
.vh-panel.open{opacity:1;pointer-events:auto;transform:translateX(0)}
.vh-panel-close{position:absolute;top:14px;right:14px;width:30px;height:30px;border-radius:50%;border:none;background:rgba(18,32,60,.06);color:var(--navy);font-size:18px;line-height:1;cursor:pointer}
.vh-panel-close:hover{background:rgba(18,32,60,.12)}
.vh-panel-eyebrow{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:13px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1.5px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:6px 0 8px;color:var(--vh-violet)}
.vh-panel-title{font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:24px;font-weight:800;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:12px;padding-right:26px;line-height:1.3}
.vh-panel-body{font-size:18px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ink2);overflow-y:auto}
.vh-panel.vh-panel-finding .vh-panel-eyebrow{color:var(--vh-coral)}.vh-panel.vh-panel-finding{border-top:4px solid var(--vh-coral)}
.vh-panel.vh-panel-mechanism .vh-panel-eyebrow{color:var(--vh-teal)}.vh-panel.vh-panel-mechanism{border-top:4px solid var(--vh-teal)}
.vh-panel.vh-panel-term .vh-panel-eyebrow{color:var(--vh-gold)}.vh-panel.vh-panel-term{border-top:4px solid var(--vh-gold)}
.vh-panel.vh-panel-pattern .vh-panel-eyebrow{color:var(--vh-violet)}.vh-panel.vh-panel-pattern{border-top:4px solid var(--vh-violet)}
.vh-panel.vh-panel-workup .vh-panel-eyebrow{color:var(--vh-sky)}.vh-panel.vh-panel-workup{border-top:4px solid var(--vh-sky)}
@media (max-width:1000px){
  .vh-panel{top:auto;left:0;right:0;bottom:0;width:auto;max-height:62vh;border-left:none;border-top:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.1);border-radius:20px 20px 0 0;transform:translateY(100%)}
  .vh-panel.open{transform:translateY(0)}
}

mark.vh:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--vh-sky);outline-offset:3px}
.vh-panel[aria-hidden="true"]{visibility:hidden}
.exs.exlo{background:var(--gold-bg,#FDF6E3);border-left:5px solid var(--coral,#E07A5F)}
.exs.exlo .exl{color:var(--coral,#E07A5F)}
.exb .exs .ext{white-space:pre-line}
.exs.exlo .ext{white-space:normal}
.lo-row{display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:flex-start}
.lo-row+.lo-row{margin-top:9px;padding-top:9px;border-top:1px solid rgba(224,122,95,.18)}
.lo-chip{flex:0 0 auto;display:inline-block;font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',Consolas,monospace;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.6px;padding:3px 9px;border-radius:999px;background:var(--coral,#E07A5F);color:#fff;text-decoration:none;white-space:nowrap}
.lo-chip:hover{filter:brightness(1.12)}
.lo-text{font-size:.9em;line-height:1.55;color:var(--ink2,#3E4555)}
.lo-sec{display:inline-block;margin-left:7px;font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',Consolas,monospace;font-size:10.5px;color:var(--slate,#6B7689)}
.qfig{margin:18px 0 4px;padding:10px;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.14);border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto}
.qfig img{display:block;width:100%;min-width:520px;height:auto;border-radius:8px}
.qfig.photo{max-width:640px;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto}
.qfig.photo img{min-width:0}
.qfig-cap{margin-top:9px;font-size:.86em;line-height:1.5;color:#5a6a85;font-style:italic}
.qfig.exfig{margin:10px 0 0;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(18,32,60,.12)}
.qfig.exfig img{min-width:420px}
.exs.exf{background:#fff}
@media print{.qfig{break-inside:avoid;page-break-inside:avoid}.qfig img{min-width:0}}
</style>
<script>
(function(){
  var NOTE_AUTH_KEY='une-study-hub-remembered-auth-v1';
  var NOTE_LOGIN_URL='../../../../index.html?login=notes';

  function readCookie(name){
    try{
      var prefix=encodeURIComponent(name)+'=';
      var parts=String(document.cookie||'').split(';');
      for(var i=0;i<parts.length;i++){
        var part=parts[i].trim();
        if(part.indexOf(prefix)===0)return decodeURIComponent(part.slice(prefix.length));
      }
    }catch(err){}
    return null;
  }
  function readAuthPayload(){
    try{
      var stored=localStorage.getItem(NOTE_AUTH_KEY);
      if(stored)return stored;
    }catch(err){}
    return readCookie(NOTE_AUTH_KEY);
  }
  function hasValidNoteAuth(){
    var raw=readAuthPayload();
    if(!raw)return false;
    try{
      var parsed=JSON.parse(raw);
      var email=String(parsed&&parsed.email||'').trim().toLowerCase();
      var expiresAt=Number(parsed&&parsed.expiresAt);
      return /@une\.edu$/.test(email)&&expiresAt&&Date.now()<expiresAt;
    }catch(err){return false}
  }
  function syncNoteAuth(){
    document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-note-auth',hasValidNoteAuth()?'unlocked':'locked');
  }
  window.openNoteLogin=function(){
    var loginWindow=window.open(NOTE_LOGIN_URL,'uneStudyHubLogin');
    if(!loginWindow)window.location.href=NOTE_LOGIN_URL;
  };
  window.refreshNoteAuth=syncNoteAuth;
  window.addEventListener('message',function(event){
    if(event&&event.data&&event.data.type==='une-study-hub-auth-complete')syncNoteAuth();
  });
  window.addEventListener('storage',function(event){
    if(!event||event.key===NOTE_AUTH_KEY)syncNoteAuth();
  });
  window.addEventListener('focus',syncNoteAuth);
  window.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded',syncNoteAuth);
  window.setInterval(syncNoteAuth,1000);
  syncNoteAuth();
})();
</script>
<style>
#noteAuthGate{display:none}
html[data-note-auth="locked"] body{min-height:100vh;overflow:hidden;background:#f4f5f7;color:#1f2937}
html[data-note-auth="locked"] body>*:not(#noteAuthGate){display:none!important}
html[data-note-auth="locked"] #noteAuthGate{position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:2147483647;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;padding:24px;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#f4f5f7,#fff 52%,#eef2f7);font-family:Arial,sans-serif}
.note-auth-card{box-sizing:border-box;width:min(460px,100%);padding:30px;border:1px solid rgba(31,41,55,.14);border-radius:14px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.96);box-shadow:0 22px 70px rgba(31,41,55,.16);text-align:center}
.note-auth-kicker{margin-bottom:12px;color:#a5142a;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase}
.note-auth-card h1{margin:0 0 12px;color:#263241;font-size:clamp(28px,6vw,42px);line-height:1.05}
.note-auth-card p{margin:0 0 22px;color:#556070;font-size:16px;line-height:1.55}
.note-auth-btn{border:0;border-radius:999px;padding:12px 20px;color:#fff;background:#263241;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;cursor:pointer;box-shadow:0 12px 28px rgba(38,50,65,.22)}
.note-auth-btn:hover{background:#a5142a}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="noteAuthGate" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="noteAuthTitle">
  <div class="note-auth-card">
    <div class="note-auth-kicker">2nd Year Study Hub</div>
    <h1 id="noteAuthTitle">Please log in to continue.</h1>
    <p>Open the Study Hub login, enter your approved UNE email, and this page will unlock automatically.</p>
    <button class="note-auth-btn" type="button" onclick="openNoteLogin()">Log in to continue</button>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="toast-wrap" id="toastWrap"></div>

<!-- ══════════ SETUP VIEW ══════════ -->
<div class="view active" id="viewSetup">
<div class="setup-page">
<div class="setup-card">

  <div class="setup-top">
    <div class="setup-emoji">🧪</div>
    <div class="setup-title">Custom <em>Quiz Builder</em></div>
    <div class="setup-sub">Generate a quiz from any AI and run it in the same exam format as the study hub.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="setup-body">
<label class="reading-theme">Theme <select aria-label="Theme" data-reading-theme onchange="setReadingTheme(this.value)"><option value="light">Light</option><option value="dark">Dark</option><option value="soft">Easy on the eyes</option></select></label>

    <div class="how-title">How it works</div>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-text">
          <strong>Set your question count</strong>
          Choose how many questions you want the AI to make. Smaller batches usually give better questions.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-text">
          <strong>Add it to your AI of choice</strong>
          Go to the "Your AI prompt" section below and click Copy prompt. Paste it into Claude for best results, ChatGPT Sol with a Pro account for strong results, or Gemini if needed. Add your notes after MY CONTENT:, then send.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-text">
          <strong>Paste the AI response into a Set tab</strong>
          Copy the entire JSON response from the AI and paste it into Set 1. For multiple batches, use Sets 2-4 too.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-text">
          <strong>Name it, choose the interface, and launch</strong>
          Add an optional quiz name, choose the study-hub or NBME / NBOME interface, then hit Launch Quiz.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="how-visual">
      <img src="assets/copy-generated-question-sets.png" alt="Screenshot showing the AI response copy button below a generated question set.">
      <div class="how-visual-caption">After the AI finishes, use its copy button to grab the full generated response before pasting it below.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="field">
      <label class="lbl">Target question count</label>
      <input class="inp" id="questionCount" type="number" min="1" max="25" step="1" value="15" oninput="handleQuestionCountInput()" onblur="handleQuestionCountBlur()">
      <div class="field-note">Cap: 25 questions. Increasing the number of questions may cause degraded question quality from your AI.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- Prompt to copy -->
    <div class="lbl" style="margin-bottom:8px">Your AI prompt</div>
    <div class="prompt-wrap">
      <button class="copy-prompt-btn" id="copyPromptBtn" onclick="copyPrompt()">📋 Copy prompt</button>
      <div class="prompt-meta">
        <div class="prompt-chip" id="promptCountChip">Target: 15 questions</div>
      </div>
      <div class="prompt-inner" id="promptText"></div>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--mist);margin-bottom:24px;margin-top:6px">Paste your notes or existing questions <em>after</em> this prompt before sending to your AI. The prompt is calibrated on the released NBME Step 1 sample items — full USMLE / COMLEX item-writing guide, distractor architecture, and quality gate.</p>

    <div class="divider"></div>

    <div class="field">
      <label class="lbl">Quiz name (optional)</label>
      <input class="inp" id="quizName" placeholder="e.g. Ferland — Confidence Intervals — Bank 1" oninput="refreshPromptText()">
    </div>
    <div class="field">
      <label class="lbl">Quiz interface</label>
      <div class="setup-ui-toggle">
        <button class="setup-ui-btn active" id="uiModeClassicBtn" type="button" onclick="selectLaunchUI('classic')">
          <span class="setup-ui-name">UNE Study Hub Style</span>
          <span class="setup-ui-meta">The current layout with score cards, sidebar tools, and the original study-hub feel.</span>
        </button>
        <button class="setup-ui-btn" id="uiModeNbmeBtn" type="button" onclick="selectLaunchUI('nbme')">
          <span class="setup-ui-name">NBME / NBOME Style</span>
          <span class="setup-ui-meta">Header navigation, question rail, footer timer, and an exam-stage layout modeled after the simulator.</span>
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="field">
      <label class="lbl">Paste AI responses here</label>
      <div class="field-note">Paste the AI's full copied response into Set 1. If you ask the AI to make questions in smaller groups, paste each extra group into Set 2, Set 3, or Set 4. Launch Quiz will put all filled sets together.</div>
      <div class="paste-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="AI response sets">
        <button class="paste-tab active" id="pasteTab1" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="pastePanel1" onclick="showPasteSetTab(1)">Set 1</button>
        <button class="paste-tab" id="pasteTab2" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="pastePanel2" onclick="showPasteSetTab(2)">Set 2</button>
        <button class="paste-tab" id="pasteTab3" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="pastePanel3" onclick="showPasteSetTab(3)">Set 3</button>
        <button class="paste-tab" id="pasteTab4" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="pastePanel4" onclick="showPasteSetTab(4)">Set 4</button>
      </div>
      <div class="paste-panels">
        <div class="paste-panel active" id="pastePanel1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="pasteTab1">
          <label class="lbl" for="pasteArea1">Set 1</label>
          <div class="field-note">Use this for your first AI batch.</div>
          <textarea class="ta" id="pasteArea1" placeholder="Paste AI response for Set 1…" oninput="syncPasteSetTabs()"></textarea>
        </div>
        <div class="paste-panel" id="pastePanel2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="pasteTab2" hidden>
          <label class="lbl" for="pasteArea2">Set 2</label>
          <div class="field-note">Use this for your second AI batch.</div>
          <textarea class="ta" id="pasteArea2" placeholder="Paste AI response for Set 2…" oninput="syncPasteSetTabs()"></textarea>
        </div>
        <div class="paste-panel" id="pastePanel3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="pasteTab3" hidden>
          <label class="lbl" for="pasteArea3">Set 3</label>
          <div class="field-note">Use this for your third AI batch.</div>
          <textarea class="ta" id="pasteArea3" placeholder="Paste AI response for Set 3…" oninput="syncPasteSetTabs()"></textarea>
        </div>
        <div class="paste-panel" id="pastePanel4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="pasteTab4" hidden>
          <label class="lbl" for="pasteArea4">Set 4</label>
          <div class="field-note">Use this for your fourth AI batch.</div>
          <textarea class="ta" id="pasteArea4" placeholder="Paste AI response for Set 4…" oninput="syncPasteSetTabs()"></textarea>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="setup-actions">
      <button class="launch-btn" onclick="launchQuiz()">🚀 Launch Quiz</button>
      <button class="ghost-btn" onclick="downloadQuiz()">⬇ Download My Quiz</button>
    </div>
    <div class="err-msg" id="errMsg"></div>
  </div>

</div>
</div>
</div>

<!-- ══════════ QUIZ VIEW ══════════ -->
<div class="view" id="viewQuiz">
<div class="quiz-page">

  <!-- top bar -->
  <div class="qt-bar">
    <div class="qt-name" id="qtName">Quiz</div>
    <button class="qt-btn" id="qtClassicUiBtn" onclick="switchQuizUI('nbme')">⇄ NBME UI</button>
    <button class="qt-btn" id="qtMarkBtn" onclick="toggleMark()">⚑ Mark</button>
    <button class="qt-btn" id="qtSubmitBtn" onclick="submitExam()">🧾 Submit Exam</button>
    <button class="qt-btn" onclick="downloadQuiz()">⬇ Download Quiz</button>
    <button class="qt-btn" onclick="resetQuiz()">↺ Reset</button>
    <button class="qt-btn" onclick="exitQuiz()">✕ Close</button>
  </div>

  <!-- grid -->
  <div class="qin">

    <!-- LEFT SIDEBAR -->
    <div class="sidebar">

      <!-- score boxes -->
      <div class="score-panel">
        <div class="score-box correct"><div class="sv" id="sC">0</div><div class="sl">Correct</div></div>
        <div class="score-box incorrect"><div class="sv" id="sI">0</div><div class="sl">Incorrect</div></div>
        <div class="score-box"><div class="sv" id="sP">—</div><div class="sl">Score</div></div>
      </div>

      <!-- mode toggle -->
      <div class="mode-toggle">
        <button class="mode-btn active" id="modeLearn" onclick="setMode('learn')"><span class="mode-icon">📚</span>Learn Mode</button>
        <button class="mode-btn" id="modeExam" onclick="setMode('exam')"><span class="mode-icon">📝</span>Exam Mode</button>
      </div>

      <!-- timer -->
      <div class="timer-card" id="timerCard">
        <div class="timer-lbl" id="timerModeLbl" onclick="toggleTimerDisplay()" title="Click to switch between elapsed and remaining time">TIMER · ELAPSED</div>
        <div class="timer-time" id="timerTime">00:00</div>
        <button class="tbtn" id="timerPlayBtn" onclick="toggleTimer()">▶</button>
        <button class="tbtn" onclick="resetTimer()">↺</button>
      </div>

      <!-- tools -->
      <div class="tool-panel">
<div class="study-toolbar"><label class="reading-theme">Theme <select aria-label="Theme" data-reading-theme onchange="setReadingTheme(this.value)"><option value="light">Light</option><option value="dark">Dark</option><option value="soft">Easy on the eyes</option></select></label><button class="study-tool-button" type="button" data-study-tool="studyCalculator" aria-pressed="false" aria-controls="studyCalculator" onclick="openCalculator()">Calculator</button><button class="study-tool-button" type="button" data-study-tool="studyNotes" aria-pressed="false" aria-controls="studyNotes" onclick="openNotes()">Notes</button></div>

        <button class="q-tool hl-tool" id="hlTool" onclick="toggleTool('hl')">🖊 Highlight</button>
        <button class="q-tool st-tool" id="stTool" onclick="toggleTool('st')">✂ Strikethrough</button>
        <button class="q-tool" onclick="clearMarks()">✖ Clear Marks</button>
        <button class="q-tool lab-tool" onclick="openLabs()">🧪 Lab Values</button>
        <div class="font-tools">
          <span class="font-tools-label">Text Size</span>
          <div class="font-tools-group">
            <button class="font-btn" onclick="adjustFontSize(-1)" aria-label="Decrease question and answer text size">A-</button>
            <button class="font-btn reset" onclick="resetFontSize()" aria-label="Reset question and answer text size">A</button>
            <button class="font-btn" onclick="adjustFontSize(1)" aria-label="Increase question and answer text size">A+</button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <!-- RIGHT: QUESTION -->
    <div>
      <div id="finBanner"></div>
      <div id="qArea"></div>
    </div>

  </div>
</div>
</div>

<!-- ══════════ NBME / NBOME VIEW ══════════ -->
<div class="view" id="viewQuizNbme">
<div class="nb-wrap">
  <div class="nb-util">
    <div class="nb-util-name" id="nbQuizTitle">Quiz</div>
    <div class="nb-util-actions">

      <button class="nb-util-btn" onclick="toggleFullscreen()" id="nbFsBtn">⛶ Fullscreen</button>
      <button class="nb-util-btn" onclick="switchQuizUI('classic')">UNE UI</button>
      <button class="nb-util-btn" onclick="downloadQuiz()">Download</button>
      <button class="nb-util-btn" onclick="resetQuiz()">Reset</button>
      <button class="nb-util-btn" onclick="exitQuiz()">Close</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="nb-app">
    <header class="nb-header">
      <div class="nb-status-box">
        <strong id="nbItemIndicator">Question: 1 of 1</strong>
        <span id="nbStatusMeta">Answered 0 of 1</span>
      </div>

      <div class="nb-header-center">
        <button class="nb-action-btn" id="nbMarkBtn" type="button" onclick="toggleMark()">⚑ Mark</button>
        <button class="nb-action-btn" id="nbHighlightBtn" type="button" onclick="toggleTool('hl')">Highlight</button>
        <button class="nb-action-btn" id="nbStrikeBtn" type="button" onclick="toggleTool('st')">Strikeout</button>
        <button class="nb-action-btn" type="button" onclick="clearMarks()">Clear</button>
<div class="study-toolbar"><label class="reading-theme">Theme <select aria-label="Theme" data-reading-theme onchange="setReadingTheme(this.value)"><option value="light">Light</option><option value="dark">Dark</option><option value="soft">Easy on the eyes</option></select></label><button class="study-tool-button" type="button" data-study-tool="studyCalculator" aria-pressed="false" aria-controls="studyCalculator" onclick="openCalculator()">Calculator</button><button class="study-tool-button" type="button" data-study-tool="studyNotes" aria-pressed="false" aria-controls="studyNotes" onclick="openNotes()">Notes</button></div>
        <button class="nb-mode-pill active" id="nbModeLearn" type="button" onclick="setMode('learn')">Learn</button>
        <button class="nb-mode-pill" id="nbModeExam" type="button" onclick="setMode('exam')">Testing</button>
        <button class="nb-action-btn" id="nbSummaryBtn" type="button" onclick="toggleSummaryPanel()" disabled>See Score &amp; Summary</button>
        <div class="nb-item-picker">
          <label for="nbItemSelect">Question</label>
          <select id="nbItemSelect" aria-label="Jump to a question" onchange="goQ(parseInt(this.value,10))"></select>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="nb-nav-controls">
        <button class="nb-nav-btn" id="nbPrevBtn" type="button" onclick="prevQ()">
          <span class="icon">◀</span><span>Previous</span>
        </button>
        <button class="nb-nav-btn" id="nbNextBtn" type="button" onclick="nextQ()">
          <span class="icon">▶</span><span>Next</span>
        </button>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main class="nb-body">
      <aside class="nb-rail" aria-label="Question navigator">
        <div class="nb-rail-title">Questions</div>
        <button class="nb-rail-arrow" id="nbRailUpBtn" type="button" aria-label="Scroll question list up" onclick="scrollNbmeRail(-1)">▲</button>
        <div class="nb-rail-scroll" id="nbRailScroll" onscroll="syncNbmeRailArrows()">
          <div id="nbRailItems"></div>
        </div>
        <button class="nb-rail-arrow" id="nbRailDownBtn" type="button" aria-label="Scroll question list down" onclick="scrollNbmeRail(1)">▼</button>
      </aside>

      <section class="nb-stage">
        <div id="nbFinishBanner"></div>
        <div class="nb-question-scroll">
          <div id="nbContent"></div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer class="nb-footer">
      <div class="nb-timer-box" id="nbTimerCard">
        <button class="nb-timer-mode" id="nbTimerModeLbl" type="button" onclick="toggleTimerDisplay()" title="Click to switch between elapsed and remaining time">Elapsed Time:</button><strong id="nbTimerTime">00:00</strong>
        <span>Status:</span><strong id="nbTimerStatus">Paused</strong>
        <small id="nbTimerNote">Same timer as the original interface</small>
        <div class="nb-timer-actions">
          <button class="nb-timer-mini" id="nbTimerToggle" type="button" onclick="toggleTimer()">Start</button>
          <button class="nb-timer-mini" type="button" onclick="resetTimer()">Reset</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="nb-footer-center">
        <div class="nb-score-chip">
          <span>Correct <strong id="nbScoreCorrect">0</strong></span>
          <span>Incorrect <strong id="nbScoreIncorrect">0</strong></span>
          <span>Score <strong id="nbScorePercent">—</strong></span>
        </div>
        <button class="nb-footer-tool" type="button" onclick="openLabs()">▤ Lab Values</button>
        <div class="nb-font-cluster">
          <label>Text</label>
          <button class="nb-font-btn" type="button" onclick="adjustFontSize(-1)">A-</button>
          <button class="nb-font-btn" type="button" onclick="resetFontSize()">A</button>
          <button class="nb-font-btn" type="button" onclick="adjustFontSize(1)">A+</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="nb-end-wrap">
        <button class="nb-end-btn" id="nbReviewBtn" type="button" onclick="handleNbmeEndAction()">Testing Mode Only</button>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="lab-modal" id="labModal" onclick="if(event.target===this)closeLabs()">
  <div class="lab-shell">
    <div class="lab-card">
      <div class="lab-header">
        <div>
          <div class="lab-kicker">Reference Table</div>
          <h2 class="lab-title">Laboratory Values</h2>
          <div class="lab-sub">Shared lab reference for both quiz interfaces, imported from the NBME-style simulator so the values stay consistent.</div>
        </div>
        <button class="lab-close" type="button" onclick="closeLabs()">Close</button>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-controls">
        <div class="lab-toggle">
          <button class="lab-toggle-btn active" id="labUnitsUs" type="button" onclick="setLabUnits('us')">U.S. Units</button>
          <button class="lab-toggle-btn" id="labUnitsSi" type="button" onclick="setLabUnits('si')">SI Units</button>
        </div>
        <div class="lab-note">Switch units without leaving the question.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-body">
        <div id="labTables"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>


<dialog class="study-window" id="studyCalculator" aria-labelledby="studyCalculatorTitle"><header class="study-window-head" tabindex="0" aria-label="Move calculator. Drag or use arrow keys."><span class="study-window-title"><span class="study-drag-dots" aria-hidden="true">⠿</span><span id="studyCalculatorTitle">Calculator</span></span><button class="study-window-close" type="button" aria-label="Close calculator" onclick="closeStudyWindow('studyCalculator')">×</button></header><div class="study-window-body"><label for="calculatorInput">Expression</label><div class="calc-display"><div class="calc-history" id="calculatorHistory"></div><input id="calculatorInput" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="0" maxlength="300"></div><div class="calc-parentheses"><button type="button" onclick="calculatorKey('(')">(</button><button type="button" onclick="calculatorKey(')')">)</button><span>% divides by 100</span></div><div class="calculator-grid"><button type="button" class="calc-clear" aria-label="Clear calculator" onclick="calculatorKey('C')">AC</button><button type="button" class="calc-clear" aria-label="Backspace" onclick="calculatorKey('⌫')">⌫</button><button type="button" class="calc-operation"  onclick="calculatorKey('%')">%</button><button type="button" class="calc-operation"  onclick="calculatorKey('÷')">÷</button><button type="button" class=""  onclick="calculatorKey('7')">7</button><button type="button" class=""  onclick="calculatorKey('8')">8</button><button type="button" class=""  onclick="calculatorKey('9')">9</button><button type="button" class="calc-operation"  onclick="calculatorKey('×')">×</button><button type="button" class=""  onclick="calculatorKey('4')">4</button><button type="button" class=""  onclick="calculatorKey('5')">5</button><button type="button" class=""  onclick="calculatorKey('6')">6</button><button type="button" class="calc-operation"  onclick="calculatorKey('−')">−</button><button type="button" class=""  onclick="calculatorKey('1')">1</button><button type="button" class=""  onclick="calculatorKey('2')">2</button><button type="button" class=""  onclick="calculatorKey('3')">3</button><button type="button" class="calc-operation"  onclick="calculatorKey('+')">+</button><button type="button" class="" aria-label="Change sign" onclick="calculatorKey('±')">±</button><button type="button" class=""  onclick="calculatorKey('0')">0</button><button type="button" class=""  onclick="calculatorKey('.')">.</button><button type="button" class="calc-equals"  onclick="calculatorKey('=')">=</button></div><output class="calc-result" id="calculatorResult" aria-live="polite">Enter = calculate · Esc = close</output></div></dialog>
<dialog class="study-window notes-window" id="studyNotes" aria-labelledby="studyNotesTitle"><header class="study-window-head" tabindex="0" aria-label="Move notes. Drag or use arrow keys."><span class="study-window-title"><span class="study-drag-dots" aria-hidden="true">⠿</span><span id="studyNotesTitle">Notes</span></span><button class="study-window-close" type="button" aria-label="Close notes" onclick="closeStudyWindow('studyNotes')">×</button></header><div class="study-window-body"><label for="studyNotesInput" id="studyNotesLabel">Current question</label><textarea id="studyNotesInput" placeholder="Your reasoning, reminders, and takeaways…" oninput="saveQuestionNote(Number(this.dataset.question),this.value)"></textarea><p class="notes-status" id="studyNotesStatus" role="status">Saved with your quiz progress · Included in summary exports.</p></div></dialog>

<script>

// Themes are shared across quizzes; notes belong to the saved question order.
var questionNotes={};
function escapeStudyText(value){return String(value==null?'':value).replace(/[&<>"']/g,function(c){return {'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c];});}
function setReadingTheme(theme){
  if(['light','dark','soft'].indexOf(theme)<0)theme='light';
  if(theme==='light')document.documentElement.removeAttribute('data-theme');
  else document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme',theme);
  document.querySelectorAll('[data-reading-theme]').forEach(function(el){el.value=theme;});
  try{localStorage.setItem('quiz-builder-reading-theme',theme);}catch(e){}
}
function exportSummarySheet(){
  var html='<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>'+escapeStudyText(getQuizTitle())+' — Summary</title><style>'+getSummaryPrintStyles()+'</style></head><body>'+buildReviewSheetHTML(true)+'</body></html>';
  triggerQuizDownload(new Blob([html],{type:'text/html;charset=utf-8'}),buildDownloadFileName(getQuizTitle()).replace(/\.html$/i,' Summary.html'));
}
function calculateExpression(source){
  var text=source.replace(/\s+/g,'').replace(/×/g,'*').replace(/÷/g,'/').replace(/−/g,'-');
  if(!text||text.length>300)throw Error('Enter an expression (up to 300 characters).');
  var pos=0;
  function primary(){
    if(text[pos]==='+'){pos++;return primary();}
    if(text[pos]==='-'){pos++;return -primary();}
    var value;
    if(text[pos]==='('){pos++;value=sum();if(text[pos++]!==')')throw Error('Check your parentheses.');}
    else{var m=text.slice(pos).match(/^(?:\d+(?:\.\d*)?|\.\d+)(?:e[+-]?\d+)?/i);if(!m)throw Error('Enter numbers and arithmetic operators only.');pos+=m[0].length;value=Number(m[0]);}
    while(text[pos]==='%'){pos++;value/=100;}
    return value;
  }
  function product(){var value=primary();while(text[pos]==='*'||text[pos]==='/'){var op=text[pos++],right=primary();if(op==='/'&&right===0)throw Error('Cannot divide by zero.');value=op==='*'?value*right:value/right;}return value;}
  function sum(){var value=product();while(text[pos]==='+'||text[pos]==='-'){var op=text[pos++],right=product();value=op==='+'?value+right:value-right;}return value;}
  var value=sum();if(pos!==text.length)throw Error('Check the expression.');if(!Number.isFinite(value))throw Error('Result is outside the supported range.');return Number(value.toPrecision(12));
}
var studyWindowZ=1400, studyWindowOpeners={}, calculatorFinished=false;
function openStudyWindow(id){
  var win=document.getElementById(id);
  studyWindowOpeners[id]=document.activeElement;
  if(!win.open)win.show();
  win.style.zIndex=++studyWindowZ;
  clampStudyWindow(win);
  syncStudyTools();
}
function closeStudyWindow(id){
  document.getElementById(id).close();
  syncStudyTools();
  var opener=studyWindowOpeners[id];
  if(opener&&opener.isConnected&&opener.getClientRects().length)opener.focus();
}
function openCalculator(){openStudyWindow('studyCalculator');document.getElementById('calculatorInput').focus();}
function closeCalculator(){closeStudyWindow('studyCalculator');}
function openNotes(){
  if(!questions.length)return;
  syncStudyTools();openStudyWindow('studyNotes');document.getElementById('studyNotesInput').focus();
}
function syncStudyTools(){
  var input=document.getElementById('studyNotesInput');
  if(!input)return;
  var value=questionNotes[CQ]||'';
  // Do not reset selection or the caret while the user is typing.
  if(input.value!==value)input.value=value;
  input.dataset.question=String(CQ);
  document.getElementById('studyNotesLabel').textContent='Question '+(CQ+1)+' of '+questions.length;
  document.querySelectorAll('[data-study-tool]').forEach(function(btn){
    var id=btn.dataset.studyTool;
    btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(document.getElementById(id).open));
    if(id==='studyNotes')btn.classList.toggle('has-note',!!value);
  });
}
function saveQuestionNote(index,value){
  if(value)questionNotes[index]=value;else delete questionNotes[index];
  var saved=saveSession();
  document.getElementById('studyNotesStatus').textContent=saved?'Saved in this browser · Included in summary exports.':'Browser storage unavailable. Export your summary to keep these notes.';
  syncStudyTools();
  if(summaryVisible){if(uiMode==='nbme')renderNbmeView();else renderClassicView();}
}
function clampStudyWindow(win,left,top){
  var rect=win.getBoundingClientRect(),edge=8;
  left=left==null?rect.left:left;top=top==null?rect.top:top;
  win.style.left=Math.max(edge,Math.min(left,window.innerWidth-rect.width-edge))+'px';
  win.style.top=Math.max(edge,Math.min(top,window.innerHeight-rect.height-edge))+'px';
  win.style.right='auto';win.style.bottom='auto';
}
function initStudyWindows(){
  document.querySelectorAll('.study-window').forEach(function(win){
    var head=win.querySelector('.study-window-head'),drag=null;
    win.addEventListener('pointerdown',function(){win.style.zIndex=++studyWindowZ;});
    head.addEventListener('pointerdown',function(event){
      if(event.button!==0||event.target.closest('button'))return;
      var rect=win.getBoundingClientRect();
      drag={x:event.clientX,y:event.clientY,left:rect.left,top:rect.top};
      head.setPointerCapture(event.pointerId);event.preventDefault();
    });
    head.addEventListener('pointermove',function(event){
      if(drag)clampStudyWindow(win,drag.left+event.clientX-drag.x,drag.top+event.clientY-drag.y);
    });
    function endDrag(){drag=null;}
    head.addEventListener('pointerup',endDrag);head.addEventListener('pointercancel',endDrag);head.addEventListener('lostpointercapture',endDrag);
    head.addEventListener('keydown',function(event){
      if(event.target!==head||!/^Arrow/.test(event.key))return;
      event.preventDefault();event.stopPropagation();
      var rect=win.getBoundingClientRect(),step=event.shiftKey?40:10;
      clampStudyWindow(win,rect.left+(event.key==='ArrowRight'?step:event.key==='ArrowLeft'?-step:0),rect.top+(event.key==='ArrowDown'?step:event.key==='ArrowUp'?-step:0));
    });
    win.addEventListener('keydown',function(event){
      event.stopPropagation();
      if(event.key==='Escape'){event.preventDefault();closeStudyWindow(win.id);}
      if(win.id==='studyCalculator'&&event.key==='Enter'&&event.target.tagName==='INPUT'){event.preventDefault();calculatorKey('=');}
    });
    win.addEventListener('close',syncStudyTools);
    if(window.ResizeObserver)new ResizeObserver(function(){if(win.open)clampStudyWindow(win);}).observe(win);
  });
  window.addEventListener('resize',function(){document.querySelectorAll('.study-window[open]').forEach(function(win){clampStudyWindow(win);});});
  document.getElementById('calculatorInput').addEventListener('input',function(){calculatorFinished=false;document.getElementById('calculatorResult').textContent='Enter = calculate · Esc = close';});
}
function calculatorKey(key){
  var input=document.getElementById('calculatorInput'),result=document.getElementById('calculatorResult'),history=document.getElementById('calculatorHistory');
  result.removeAttribute('data-error');
  if(key==='='){
    try{var expression=input.value,value=calculateExpression(expression);history.textContent=expression+' =';input.value=String(value);result.textContent='Result: '+value;calculatorFinished=true;}
    catch(e){result.textContent=e.message;result.dataset.error='true';calculatorFinished=false;}
  }else if(key==='C'){
    input.value='';history.textContent='';result.textContent='Enter = calculate · Esc = close';calculatorFinished=false;
  }else if(key==='±'){
    if(input.value)input.value=input.value.startsWith('-(')&&input.value.endsWith(')')?input.value.slice(2,-1):'-('+input.value+')';
    calculatorFinished=false;
  }else{
    if(calculatorFinished&&/^[0-9.(]$/.test(key))input.value='';
    var start=input.selectionStart==null?input.value.length:input.selectionStart,end=input.selectionEnd==null?start:input.selectionEnd;
    if(key==='⌫')input.setRangeText('',start===end?Math.max(0,start-1):start,end,'end');
    else if(input.value.length-(end-start)+key.length<=300)input.setRangeText(key,start,end,'end');
    calculatorFinished=false;
  }
  input.focus();
}

// ── STATE ──
var questions=[],questionBank=[],answers={},selected={},marked={},stemMarkup={},choiceMarkup={},NC=0,NI=0,CQ=0;
var quizMode='learn';
var uiMode='classic';
var examSubmitted=false;
var reviewOpen=false;
var summaryVisible=false;
var finishBannerMinimized=false;
var toolMode='';
var highlightColor='#fff173', annotationRange=null, annotationRoot=null, suppressChoiceUntil=0;
var labUnitMode='us';
var timerSec=0,timerRunning=false,timerInterval=null;
var fontStep=0;
var LETTERS=['A','B','C','D','E','F','G','H'];
var MAX_ANSWER_CHOICES=8;
var PRELOADED_QUIZ_NAME="Jeevs Edition — Hardest Exam (Final 100)";
var PRELOADED_QUESTIONS_JSON="[{\"stem\": \"A 26-year-old woman at 23 weeks' gestation comes to the emergency department due to 2 days of decreased fetal movement. She and her partner were each previously told they have mildly microcytic red cell indices with normal iron studies. Ultrasonography shows fetal ascites, bilateral pleural effusions, scalp edema, and absent cardiac activity. At autopsy, the fetal liver is markedly enlarged, and sections show numerous clusters of nucleated erythroid precursors lying between the hepatic cords. The marrow cavities of the long bones are only sparsely cellular. Hemoglobin analysis of fetal blood shows that nearly all of the hemoglobin is a tetramer of four identical non-alpha chains. In a healthy fetus of this gestational age, which of the following is the principal site of blood cell production?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Liver\", \"B. Marrow of the axial skeleton\", \"C. Marrow of the long bones\", \"D. Spleen\", \"E. Thymus\", \"F. Yolk sac\"], \"correct\": 0, \"explanation\": \"Hydrops fetalis from alpha thalassemia with all four alpha genes deleted. Both parents carry alpha thalassemia trait (microcytosis with normal iron studies). With no alpha genes, the fetus makes excess gamma chains → gamma-chain tetramers (hemoglobin Barts) → extreme oxygen affinity → oxygen is bound and never released → profound tissue hypoxia → heart failure and hydrops.\\n\\nThe liver finding is a trap only if you forget the timeline. The lifetime sequence is:\\n• Embryo → yolk sac (the first, primitive wave)\\n• Fetus → LIVER (dominant mid-gestation), with a contribution from the spleen\\n• Late fetus onward → bone marrow\\n• Adult → the axial skeleton (vertebrae, ribs, sternum, pelvis); the long bones turn to fat\\n\\nSo clusters of erythroid precursors in the liver at 23 weeks are expected, and hypoxia would only exaggerate them. In a CHILD or ADULT the same finding would mean extramedullary hematopoiesis — the body reopening a site it abandoned under extreme demand (thalassemia major, severe chronic hemolysis, marrow fibrosis).\\n\\nEducational objective: Hematopoiesis moves from the yolk sac (embryo) to the liver and spleen (fetus) to the bone marrow (late fetus onward), and in adults retreats to the axial skeleton. Liver hematopoiesis is normal mid-gestation but, after birth, signals extramedullary hematopoiesis from severe marrow demand.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"1\": \"This is the ADULT pattern — vertebrae, ribs, sternum and pelvis — after the marrow of the long bones converts to fat. It is the reason a marrow biopsy is taken from the posterior iliac crest, not from a fetus's hematopoietic sites.\", \"2\": \"Bone marrow takes over in the LATE fetus and is the dominant site by birth, and in childhood the long bones are still red. At 23 weeks the long-bone marrow is only beginning to populate, as this autopsy shows.\", \"3\": \"The fetal spleen contributes to blood formation, but it is the secondary site. The liver is dominant through mid-gestation.\", \"4\": \"The thymus educates T cells that have already been made — it is a site of lymphocyte maturation, not a primary site of hematopoiesis at any age.\", \"5\": \"The yolk sac makes the first, primitive wave of blood in the EMBRYO (the first weeks after conception, making embryonic hemoglobins). By the second trimester it has been replaced by the liver.\"}, \"eli5\": \"Before you are born, your liver is the blood factory. Later the job moves into your bones, and in grown-ups only the bones in the middle of the body keep doing it. If a grown-up's liver starts making blood again, the body is in serious trouble.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_hx_fetal_hemato\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Sites of hematopoiesis across development (yolk sac → liver and spleen → bone marrow) plotted against the globin chains being made at each stage — note gamma chains dominating through fetal life, which is why an alpha-less fetus forms gamma tetramers.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 81, \"t\": \"Understand the embryologic progression of hematopoiesis and the hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) from early embryonic life through adulthood.\", \"a\": \"s1-hematopoiesis\", \"s\": \"§1\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"t\": \"Categorize the adult stem cell hierarchy and identify the primary sites of adult hematopoiesis.\", \"a\": \"s1-hematopoiesis\", \"s\": \"§1\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"An 8-year-old boy is brought to the office by his mother due to 3 weeks of fatigue, easy bruising, and bleeding gums when he brushes his teeth. Two months ago, he was hospitalized for acute hepatitis; viral serologies for hepatitis A, B, and C were negative, and his liver enzymes have since normalized. He takes no medications. He appears well and is at the 50th percentile for height. There are scattered petechiae on the legs. Both thumbs and forearms are normal, and there are no skin pigment changes. There is no lymphadenopathy or hepatosplenomegaly.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  6.9 g/dL (N=11.5–15.5 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  21% (N=35%–45%)\\n  Mean corpuscular volume  94 µm3 (N=77–95 µm3)\\n  Leukocyte count  1,700/mm3 (N=4,500–13,500/mm3)\\n  Platelet count  11,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\\n  Reticulocyte count  0.2% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\\nPeripheral smear shows no blasts. A posterior iliac crest core biopsy is shown. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of this patient's cytopenias?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Antibody destruction of circulating platelets\", \"B. Immune destruction of hematopoietic stem cells\", \"C. Ineffective hematopoiesis from vitamin deficiency\", \"D. Inherited defect in DNA cross-link repair\", \"E. Marrow replacement by lymphoblasts\", \"F. Splenic sequestration of blood cells\"], \"correct\": 1, \"explanation\": \"Acquired aplastic anemia, here following seronegative hepatitis. A well-appearing child with pancytopenia, a low reticulocyte count, no organomegaly and an acellular marrow is the aplastic picture in the pediatric differential; the sick-appearing child with splenomegaly is the leukemic one.\\n\\nMechanism: a trigger (hepatitis, drugs, toxins, viruses, or most often nothing identifiable) → T cell–mediated attack on the self-renewing hematopoietic stem cells → the stem cell pool collapses → every lineage fails together → fat fills the empty marrow space.\\n\\nWhy the biopsy matters: cellularity is a core biopsy reading, not an aspirate reading, because only the core keeps the architecture — trabeculae and the ratio of cells to fat. Normal cellularity is roughly 100 minus the patient's age, so an 8-year-old should be about 90% cellular. A marrow that is mostly fat is hypocellular.\\n\\nThe inherited mimics are separated by what you can see on examination: Fanconi anemia (short stature, café-au-lait macules, absent thumbs; confirm with a chromosome breakage assay), Diamond-Blackfan anemia (red cells only), Shwachman-Diamond syndrome (pancreatic insufficiency, neutropenia) and thrombocytopenia with absent radii (thumbs present). Definitive treatment for severe aplastic anemia in a child is a matched sibling marrow transplant.\\n\\nEducational objective: Aplastic anemia is immune-mediated loss of hematopoietic stem cells, producing pancytopenia, reticulocytopenia and a fatty, hypocellular marrow on core biopsy. Leukemia and megaloblastic anemia also cause cytopenias but leave the marrow hypercellular.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"This is immune thrombocytopenia: an ISOLATED low platelet count with a normal or increased number of megakaryocytes. It does not lower the hemoglobin, the white count and the reticulocytes, and it does not empty the marrow.\", \"2\": \"Megaloblastic anemia gives a HYPERcellular marrow (precursors are made, then die inside it) and a macrocytic MCV. This marrow is nearly all fat.\", \"3\": \"This is Fanconi anemia, which also gives pancytopenia with an empty marrow in a well-appearing child — but announces itself outside the blood with short stature, café-au-lait macules and absent or hypoplastic thumbs. This boy has none of those.\", \"4\": \"Acute lymphoblastic leukemia produces a sick-appearing child, often with splenomegaly and bone pain, a PACKED marrow and blasts. The biopsy here is empty.\", \"5\": \"Hypersplenism lowers all three lineages, but it needs a big spleen, and the marrow is normal or hypercellular as it compensates. There is no splenomegaly here.\"}, \"eli5\": \"The boy's blood factory has lost the special seed cells that make every kind of blood cell, so all of his counts dropped at once. The picture of his bone shows mostly fat where busy blood cells should be.\", \"image\": \"fig_hx_aplastic\", \"imageCaption\": \"Core biopsy of bone marrow (hematoxylin and eosin). Pink bony trabeculae border a marrow space occupied almost entirely by large empty fat cells, with only a single small cluster of hematopoietic cells.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 65, \"t\": \"Identify causes of pancytopenia and bone marrow failure in children.\", \"a\": \"s46-pediatric\", \"s\": \"§51\"}, {\"n\": 29, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette with a presentation of anemia, differentiate clinical presentations based upon laboratory + bone marrow findings and correlate with the underlying pathophysiologic mechanisms.\", \"a\": \"s2-marrow\", \"s\": \"§2\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 64-year-old man comes to the office for follow-up of end-stage kidney disease due to diabetic nephropathy. He has received hemodialysis 3 times weekly for 2 years and reports progressive fatigue and exertional dyspnea. He does not use alcohol. There is no splenomegaly. Stool is negative for occult blood.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  8.6 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  26% (N=41%–53%)\\n  Mean corpuscular volume  88 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\\n  Reticulocyte count  0.4% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\\n  Ferritin  310 ng/mL (N=20–250 ng/mL)\\n  Transferrin saturation  29% (N=20%–50%)\\n  Lactate dehydrogenase  160 U/L (N=45–200 U/L)\\n  Erythropoietin  11 mU/mL (N=4–26 mU/mL)\\nThe peripheral smear shows normocytic, normochromic erythrocytes. Which of the following is the most likely primary mechanism of this patient's anemia?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Hepcidin-mediated iron sequestration\", \"B. Immune destruction of stem cells\", \"C. Impaired DNA synthesis in precursors\", \"D. Inadequate erythropoietin production\", \"E. Marrow replacement by fibrosis\", \"F. Peripheral red cell destruction\"], \"correct\": 3, \"explanation\": \"Anemia of chronic kidney disease. The loop: the kidney samples oxygen delivery → when delivery falls it releases erythropoietin → erythropoietin drives committed erythroid progenitors to divide and mature → reticulocytes enter the blood.\\n\\nIn kidney failure the sensor and the factory foreman are the same failed organ. A normocytic anemia with a LOW reticulocyte count and replete iron (ferritin 310, saturation 29%) means the marrow has the raw material but is never given the order.\\n\\nThe trap is the erythropoietin value: 11 mU/mL sits inside the reference interval, but for a hemoglobin of 8.6 g/dL it should be many times higher. An 'inappropriately normal' erythropoietin in the face of anemia is a production failure — the same logic as a 'normal' reticulocyte count during severe anemia.\\n\\nEach lineage has its own dispatcher, and each has become a drug class: erythropoietin (red cells; epoetin, darbepoetin), thrombopoietin (platelets), granulocyte and granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factors (myeloid cells; filgrastim, sargramostim), and growth hormone with insulin-like growth factor (lymphoid cells). An erythropoiesis-stimulating agent works only if iron stores are repleted first.\\n\\nEducational objective: Erythropoietin is made by the kidney in response to reduced oxygen delivery, so chronic kidney disease causes a hypoproliferative normocytic anemia with low reticulocytes. An erythropoietin level within the reference range is inappropriately low when the patient is significantly anemic.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"This is anemia of chronic disease: iron is locked in macrophages, so serum iron and the TRANSFERRIN SATURATION fall. His saturation is a healthy 29%, so iron is reaching the marrow.\", \"1\": \"Aplastic anemia fails ALL lineages. Only the red cells are affected here.\", \"2\": \"Megaloblastic anemia is macrocytic (MCV over 100) with hypersegmented neutrophils and a raised lactate dehydrogenase. His MCV is 88 and his LDH is normal.\", \"4\": \"Primary myelofibrosis gives teardrop cells, a leukoerythroblastic smear and massive splenomegaly from extramedullary hematopoiesis. None are present.\", \"5\": \"Hemolysis drives the reticulocyte count UP, with a raised LDH. His reticulocytes are low and his LDH is normal — a production problem.\"}, \"eli5\": \"The kidney is the boss that tells the bone marrow to make red blood cells. When the kidney breaks, the boss stops shouting, so the factory slows down even though it has all its supplies.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_erythropoiesis\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Erythropoiesis from committed progenitor to reticulocyte — erythropoietin acts on the early committed erythroid cells.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 83, \"t\": \"Using your knowledge of adult stem cell hierarchy, identify common functions and pathophysiology of hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs), multipotent regulators and myeloid + lymphoid lineage.\", \"a\": \"s1-hematopoiesis\", \"s\": \"§1\"}, {\"n\": 29, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette with a presentation of anemia, differentiate clinical presentations based upon laboratory + bone marrow findings and correlate with the underlying pathophysiologic mechanisms.\", \"a\": \"s3-anemia-eval\", \"s\": \"§3\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 57-year-old woman comes to the office due to 4 months of fatigue and a sore, smooth tongue. She has hypothyroidism treated with levothyroxine. She does not drink alcohol. Her sclerae are mildly icteric. There is no splenomegaly.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  7.8 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  23% (N=36%–46%)\\n  Mean corpuscular volume  118 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\\n  Leukocyte count  3,600/mm3 (N=4,500–11,000/mm3)\\n  Platelet count  128,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\\n  Reticulocyte count  0.6% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\\n  Bilirubin, total  2.1 mg/dL (N=0.1–1.0 mg/dL)\\n  Lactate dehydrogenase  1,850 U/L (N=45–200 U/L)\\nDirect antiglobulin test is negative. A bone marrow aspirate is shown. Which of the following best explains the combination of this marrow appearance and the reticulocyte count?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Cytokine suppression of erythroid proliferation\", \"B. Intramedullary death of erythroid precursors\", \"C. Marrow infiltration by myeloid blasts\", \"D. Peripheral destruction of mature erythrocytes\", \"E. Viral lysis of erythroid progenitors\"], \"correct\": 1, \"explanation\": \"Megaloblastic anemia (autoimmune thyroid disease plus glossitis points to pernicious anemia). The marrow is hypercellular yet the reticulocyte count is low — the signature of INEFFECTIVE erythropoiesis.\\n\\nMechanism: B12 or folate deficiency → too little thymidine → DNA synthesis stalls while cytoplasmic hemoglobin synthesis continues (nuclear–cytoplasmic asynchrony) → large precursors with fine, open chromatin (megaloblasts) accumulate → many die inside the marrow before release → few reticulocytes reach the blood, and the dying cells release lactate dehydrogenase and indirect bilirubin.\\n\\nThat is why this patient looks 'hemolytic' on chemistry (very high LDH, mild jaundice) but 'hypoproliferative' on the reticulocyte count. The defect hits every dividing cell, so the white count and platelets are also mildly low, and granulocyte precursors are giant.\\n\\nIneffective hematopoiesis is also the mechanism in thalassemia, myelodysplastic syndrome, sideroblastic anemia and severe iron deficiency.\\n\\nEducational objective: Ineffective hematopoiesis means precursors are made in excess but die within the marrow, giving a hypercellular marrow with a low reticulocyte count, raised lactate dehydrogenase and indirect bilirubin. Megaloblastic anemia is the classic example; true hemolysis raises the reticulocyte count instead.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"This is one of the four mechanisms of anemia of chronic disease, which is normocytic or microcytic with a normal lactate dehydrogenase and a normal or hypocellular marrow — not a macrocytic anemia with an LDH of 1,850.\", \"2\": \"Acute leukemia also fills the marrow, but with uniform blasts and usually circulating blasts on the smear. These precursors are maturing, enlarged cells of several lineages.\", \"3\": \"Hemolysis also raises LDH and indirect bilirubin, but an intact marrow answers with a HIGH reticulocyte count. Hers is 0.6% at a hematocrit of 23% — a corrected count of about 0.3%.\", \"4\": \"Parvovirus B19 halts erythropoiesis and leaves the marrow DEVOID of red cell precursors apart from occasional giant pronormoblasts. It does not create a crowded marrow or lower all three lineages.\"}, \"eli5\": \"Her bone marrow is working very hard and is full of cells, but the cells cannot finish growing because they are missing a vitamin, so most of them break apart before they ever leave. That is why the factory looks busy but very few new cells reach the blood.\", \"image\": \"fig_megaloblastic_marrow\", \"imageCaption\": \"Bone marrow aspirate (high power). The field is highly cellular. Many precursor cells are enlarged, with nuclei showing finely dispersed, open chromatin, and several granulocytic cells are larger than expected.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_b12_tree\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The lecture's decision tree for megaloblastic anemia.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 29, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette with a presentation of anemia, differentiate clinical presentations based upon laboratory + bone marrow findings and correlate with the underlying pathophysiologic mechanisms.\", \"a\": \"s5-b12-folate\", \"s\": \"§5\"}, {\"n\": 20, \"t\": \"Differentiate clinical causes of nutritional anemias (iron, B12 + folate deficiency) by clinical presentation, etiologies, laboratory studies, and diagnostics.\", \"a\": \"s5-b12-folate\", \"s\": \"§5\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 58-year-old woman comes to the office due to 5 months of fatigue and numbness in both feet. She has vitiligo and Hashimoto thyroiditis. She eats a mixed diet and does not drink alcohol. Examination shows decreased vibration and position sense in both great toes and a positive Romberg sign.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  9.1 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  27% (N=36%–46%)\\n  Mean corpuscular volume  114 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\\n  Methylmalonic acid  1,240 nmol/L (N=70–270 nmol/L)\\n  Homocysteine  38 µmol/L (N=5–15 µmol/L)\\nAnti-parietal cell antibodies are positive. The peripheral smear is shown. After replacement therapy is begun, this patient requires ongoing surveillance for which of the following?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Colonic adenocarcinoma\", \"B. Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma\", \"C. Esophageal adenocarcinoma\", \"D. Gastric adenocarcinoma\", \"E. Hepatocellular carcinoma\"], \"correct\": 3, \"explanation\": \"Pernicious anemia. Autoimmunity (vitiligo, Hashimoto thyroiditis) → anti-parietal cell antibodies → destruction of the gastric body mucosa → loss of parietal cells → no intrinsic factor (and no acid) → no B12–intrinsic factor complex to absorb at the terminal ileum → B12 deficiency however much the patient eats.\\n\\nThe B12 deficiency is confirmed by the RAISED methylmalonic acid (normal in folate deficiency; homocysteine rises in both). The dorsal column signs (vibration, position sense, Romberg) are B12's second job — myelin maintenance — failing; folate has no such role.\\n\\nPernicious anemia is three problems, not one:\\n1. Megaloblastic anemia (macro-ovalocytes, hypersegmented neutrophils)\\n2. Atrophic gastritis with intestinal metaplasia → increased risk of GASTRIC CARCINOMA → ongoing surveillance\\n3. Myelin degeneration of the dorsal and lateral columns (subacute combined degeneration)\\n\\nB12 must be given by a route that does not need intrinsic factor: intramuscular injection, or high-dose oral therapy absorbed by passive diffusion. Anti-parietal cell antibodies are more sensitive (85%–90%); anti-intrinsic factor antibodies are more specific (75%).\\n\\nEducational objective: Pernicious anemia is autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells, causing intrinsic factor deficiency, B12 malabsorption and atrophic gastritis with intestinal metaplasia. Beyond B12 replacement, patients need surveillance for gastric carcinoma.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"Colonic cancer is the lesion hunted for in an adult man or postmenopausal woman with IRON deficiency — a microcytic anemia from occult bleeding. This is a macrocytic anemia from failed absorption.\", \"1\": \"This is the malignancy of long-standing celiac disease, itself a cause of iron and B12 malabsorption. Celiac disease is marked by anti-tissue transglutaminase antibodies, not anti-parietal cell antibodies.\", \"2\": \"This arises from Barrett metaplasia in chronic acid reflux. Pernicious anemia produces the opposite state — a stomach that can no longer make acid.\", \"4\": \"This is the cancer of cirrhosis, including cirrhosis from hemochromatosis. Nothing in this autoimmune gastric disease damages the liver.\"}, \"eli5\": \"Her immune system attacked the part of her stomach that helps her soak up vitamin B12. That damaged stomach lining can slowly turn into cancer, so doctors keep checking it for years.\", \"image\": \"fig_hx_hyperseg\", \"imageCaption\": \"Peripheral blood smear (Wright-Giemsa). Several neutrophils have nuclei divided into six or more lobes; the surrounding erythrocytes are large and many are oval.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 20, \"t\": \"Differentiate clinical causes of nutritional anemias (iron, B12 + folate deficiency) by clinical presentation, etiologies, laboratory studies, and diagnostics.\", \"a\": \"s5-b12-folate\", \"s\": \"§5\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A newborn boy is evaluated in the nursery due to the lesion shown. He was born at 39 weeks' gestation to a 29-year-old woman whose pregnancy was unplanned. She had no chronic medical conditions and took no medications before conception. When a home pregnancy test became positive at 9 weeks' gestation, she began a daily prenatal vitamin containing 0.8 mg of folic acid and took it for the rest of the pregnancy. Her serum folate at the first prenatal visit was within normal limits. The lesion is covered only by a thin membrane through which neural tissue is visible, and the infant does not move his lower extremities. For folic acid to have reduced this infant's risk of this malformation, when should supplementation have begun?\", \"choices\": [\"A. At a positive pregnancy test\", \"B. At the first trimester ultrasound examination\", \"C. At the start of the second trimester\", \"D. Before conception in any woman able to conceive\", \"E. Only if the maternal diet lacks green vegetables\"], \"correct\": 3, \"explanation\": \"Myelomeningocele — a neural tube defect in which the caudal neural tube failed to close, leaving exposed spinal cord and meninges over the lumbosacral spine (hence the paralyzed legs).\\n\\nWhy folate: folate is required for DNA synthesis, and the neural tube is one of the fastest-dividing tissues in the early embryo. Folic acid supplementation prevents neural tube defects — spina bifida and anencephaly — which is why prenatal vitamins contain more folic acid than a general multivitamin.\\n\\nWhy timing is the whole point: the neural tube closes at about 4 WEEKS after conception → often before a period is even recognized as missed → a supplement started at a positive test (here, 9 weeks) arrives after the event it was meant to protect. The recommendation is therefore to supplement before conception, in all women who could become pregnant, and to continue because pregnancy is itself a state of increased folate requirement (as are infancy, chronic hemolysis and dialysis).\\n\\nEducational objective: Folic acid prevents neural tube defects, but the neural tube closes around week 4 of gestation, frequently before pregnancy is recognized. Supplementation must therefore begin before conception in every woman of childbearing potential, not after a positive pregnancy test.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"This is what the mother did, and it was too late. By the time most pregnancies are recognized, the neural tube has already closed or failed to close.\", \"1\": \"Dating ultrasound usually happens at 8–13 weeks — after the 4-week window in which the neural tube closes.\", \"2\": \"Folate needs do rise throughout pregnancy, but a supplement started at 13 weeks cannot influence an event that finished at 4 weeks.\", \"4\": \"Supplementation is recommended for ALL women of childbearing potential, regardless of diet, because intake cannot be assumed to cover the higher requirement at the moment it matters. A normal serum folate later in pregnancy does not reassure about week 4.\"}, \"eli5\": \"The baby's spine closes very early, about a month into pregnancy, before most moms even know they are pregnant. The vitamin that helps it close only works if mom is already taking it before the baby starts growing.\", \"image\": \"fig_hx_mmc\", \"imageCaption\": \"Newborn photographed prone. An oval defect over the lumbosacral spine (arrowhead) exposes red neural tissue covered by a thin membrane, with a purplish border of surrounding skin.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 18, \"t\": \"Describe why folic acid supplementation is needed during pregnancy.\", \"a\": \"s5-b12-folate\", \"s\": \"§5\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 67-year-old man comes to the office due to 3 months of fatigue and shortness of breath when climbing stairs. He has no abdominal pain and has not noticed black or bloody stools. He eats a varied diet that includes red meat. He takes no medications. A guaiac-based fecal occult blood test 6 months ago was negative. His conjunctivae are pale. Abdominal and rectal examinations are normal.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  9.2 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  28% (N=41%–53%)\\n  Mean corpuscular volume  71 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\\n  Red cell distribution width  18.6% (N=11.5%–14.5%)\\n  Platelet count  462,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\\n  Iron  28 µg/dL (N=65–175 µg/dL)\\n  Total iron-binding capacity  468 µg/dL (N=250–400 µg/dL)\\n  Ferritin  6 ng/mL (N=20–250 ng/mL)\\nThe peripheral smear is shown. Further evaluation of this patient is most likely to reveal which of the following?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Adenocarcinoma of the cecum\", \"B. Atrophy of duodenal villi\", \"C. Loss of two alpha-globin genes\", \"D. Mitochondrial iron in erythroblasts\", \"E. Parietal cell loss in the stomach\"], \"correct\": 0, \"explanation\": \"Iron deficiency anemia: microcytic, hypochromic cells with pencil forms, a high RDW, reactive thrombocytosis, low ferritin (the earliest and most useful marker), low serum iron, and a HIGH total iron-binding capacity (empty transferrin 'cups', so the capacity to bind more is high).\\n\\nIron deficiency is a symptom, not a diagnosis — always ask where the iron went. In an adult man or a postmenopausal woman, it is gastrointestinal blood loss until proven otherwise, because colorectal adenocarcinoma can present with iron deficiency and nothing else. Right-sided (cecal, ascending) tumors are bulky, exophytic and ulcerated → slow, silent bleeding into liquid stool → iron deficiency without visible blood or change in bowel habit. Left-sided tumors more often present with obstruction or visible blood.\\n\\nThe negative fecal occult blood test is the decoy: bleeding is intermittent, and a single negative guaiac test does not exclude a tumor. The next step is bidirectional endoscopy (colonoscopy plus upper endoscopy), with iron replacement given alongside — treat the iron and find the leak. In a premenopausal woman with menorrhagia, by contrast, the gastrointestinal search is not automatic.\\n\\nEducational objective: Iron deficiency anemia in an adult man or postmenopausal woman is presumed to be from gastrointestinal blood loss until proven otherwise and requires endoscopic evaluation, even with a negative fecal occult blood test. Right-sided colon cancer classically presents as isolated iron deficiency.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"1\": \"Celiac disease causes iron deficiency by malabsorption, but it is the answer in a YOUNGER patient, or in iron deficiency that fails oral therapy — usually with diarrhea or dermatitis herpetiformis. In a 67-year-old man, blood loss comes first.\", \"2\": \"Alpha thalassemia trait is microcytic with NORMAL iron studies and a high red cell count. This patient's ferritin is 6 and TIBC is high — genuine iron deficiency.\", \"3\": \"Ringed sideroblasts mark sideroblastic anemia, where iron is HIGH and cannot be incorporated into heme. Here iron is absent.\", \"4\": \"Pernicious anemia gives a MACROcytic anemia with hypersegmented neutrophils. Achlorhydria can impair iron absorption, but it is not the expected finding in this patient.\"}, \"eli5\": \"This man's blood cells are small and pale because he has run out of iron. Grown men don't usually lose iron, so doctors look for a hidden slow bleed, and a tumor in the gut is the main worry.\", \"image\": \"fig_hx_ida_smear\", \"imageCaption\": \"Peripheral blood smear. The erythrocytes are small and pale, with central pallor occupying well over one third of each cell; there is variation in size and several elongated forms. A small lymphocyte is present for size comparison.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_hx_colon_exophytic\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Resected colon with a bulky, ulcerated, exophytic adenocarcinoma (diamond) — the kind of right-sided tumor that bleeds slowly and silently.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 49, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette, identify the important next steps in the differential diagnosis of iron deficiency anemia.\", \"a\": \"s4-iron\", \"s\": \"§4\"}, {\"n\": 20, \"t\": \"Differentiate clinical causes of nutritional anemias (iron, B12 + folate deficiency) by clinical presentation, etiologies, laboratory studies, and diagnostics.\", \"a\": \"s4-iron\", \"s\": \"§4\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 62-year-old woman comes to the office due to fatigue. She has had rheumatoid arthritis for 15 years, with persistently swollen metacarpophalangeal joints despite treatment. She has no gastrointestinal symptoms and is postmenopausal. Colonoscopy a year ago was normal.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  9.8 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  29% (N=36%–46%)\\n  Mean corpuscular volume  81 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\\n  Reticulocyte count  0.6% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\\n  Iron  34 µg/dL (N=50–170 µg/dL)\\n  Total iron-binding capacity  198 µg/dL (N=250–400 µg/dL)\\n  Ferritin  390 ng/mL (N=10–120 ng/mL)\\n  C-reactive protein  4.8 mg/dL (N=less than 0.5 mg/dL)\\nBecause of mild thrombocytosis, a bone marrow aspirate is obtained; a Prussian blue stain is shown. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of this patient's anemia?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Hepcidin-mediated ferroportin degradation\", \"B. Impaired duodenal iron absorption\", \"C. Lead inhibition of ferrochelatase\", \"D. Mitochondrial iron trapping in erythroblasts\", \"E. Occult gastrointestinal blood loss\", \"F. Reduced alpha-globin synthesis\"], \"correct\": 0, \"explanation\": \"Anemia of chronic disease (anemia of inflammation) from active rheumatoid arthritis.\\n\\nMechanism: inflammation → interleukin-6 → hepatic hepcidin synthesis ↑ → hepcidin degrades ferroportin, the only exit iron has from macrophages and enterocytes → recycled and dietary iron is trapped → serum iron falls although body iron is normal or high → the marrow is iron-starved. Three further hits add to it: cytokines blunt erythropoietin release, suppress erythroid proliferation directly, and augment hemophagocytosis.\\n\\nReading the iron row:\\n• Ferritin HIGH — iron is stored, and ferritin is also an acute phase reactant\\n• TIBC LOW — transferrin is a negative acute phase reactant, so there are fewer 'cups'\\n• Serum iron low — none is circulating (low in iron deficiency too, so it discriminates nothing)\\n• Marrow iron INCREASED, inside macrophages — the stain shown\\n\\nFerritin and TIBC move in opposite directions in the two diseases, and that pair is what separates them. Oral iron does not help — it is pushed at a locked door. Treatment is control of the underlying disease.\\n\\nEducational objective: In anemia of chronic disease, interleukin-6–driven hepcidin degrades ferroportin and traps iron in macrophages, giving low serum iron, low TIBC, normal-to-high ferritin and abundant marrow macrophage iron. Iron deficiency shows the reverse ferritin and TIBC pattern with absent marrow iron.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"1\": \"Malabsorption (celiac disease, bypass surgery, acid suppression) empties iron STORES — ferritin would be low, TIBC high and the marrow stain blank.\", \"2\": \"Lead poisoning is microcytic with basophilic stippling and raised zinc protoporphyrin. It does not produce a low TIBC with iron-laden macrophages in a patient with active inflammation.\", \"3\": \"Sideroblastic anemia puts iron in a RING around the nucleus of erythroid precursors and raises serum iron. Here the iron is in macrophages and serum iron is low.\", \"4\": \"Chronic bleeding is the classic cause of iron deficiency: low ferritin, HIGH TIBC and no stainable marrow iron — the mirror image of this patient.\", \"5\": \"Thalassemia trait is microcytic with entirely NORMAL iron studies and a high red cell count; it does not lower serum iron or TIBC.\"}, \"eli5\": \"Her body has plenty of iron, but the swelling in her joints tells her body to lock the iron away in storage cells. The blood factory can't reach it, so it makes fewer red cells.\", \"image\": \"fig_hx_prussian\", \"imageCaption\": \"Bone marrow aspirate, Prussian blue stain. A large macrophage in the center contains abundant coarse blue-staining granules; the surrounding hematopoietic cells are counterstained pink-red.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_hepcidin\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Hepcidin and iron regulation — hepcidin blocks ferroportin on enterocytes and macrophages.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 53, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette, interpret laboratory and diagnostic studies as well as molecular genetics to distinguish various etiologies of anemia.\", \"a\": \"s6-aocd\", \"s\": \"§6\"}, {\"n\": 19, \"t\": \"Differentiate clinical causes of anemias by clinical presentation, etiologies, laboratory studies, and diagnostics.\", \"a\": \"s6-aocd\", \"s\": \"§6\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 51-year-old woman comes to the office due to 6 months of fatigue and tingling in her feet. Seven years ago she underwent Roux-en-Y gastric bypass surgery and lost 55 kg (121 lb). She stopped taking her prescribed supplements 3 years ago. She has not had a menstrual period in 2 years. Vibration sense is decreased at both ankles.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  9.4 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  28% (N=36%–46%)\\n  Mean corpuscular volume  90 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\\n  Red cell distribution width  22.4% (N=11.5%–14.5%)\\n  Reticulocyte count  0.5% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\\n  Ferritin  5 ng/mL (N=10–120 ng/mL)\\n  Vitamin B12  118 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL)\\n  Creatinine  0.8 mg/dL (N=0.6–1.2 mg/dL)\\nWhich of the following best explains the mean corpuscular volume in this patient?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Averaging of microcytic and macrocytic cells\", \"B. Early iron depletion before microcytosis develops\", \"C. Erythropoietin deficiency with normocytic cells\", \"D. Red cell agglutination artifact in the analyzer\", \"E. Reticulocytosis offsetting microcytic erythrocytes\"], \"correct\": 0, \"explanation\": \"Combined iron and B12 deficiency after gastric bypass. The operation bypasses the duodenum (where iron is absorbed) and the acid- and intrinsic factor–producing stomach (needed for B12), so both deficiencies develop together and require lifelong monitoring.\\n\\nWhy the MCV is normal: the MCV is an AVERAGE. Iron deficiency → small cells (extra divisions before maturation). B12 deficiency → large cells (nucleus cannot divide) → the two populations average out to a 'normal' 90 µm3.\\n\\nTwo clues expose it:\\n• A very high RDW — two populations of different size\\n• A dimorphic smear — small pale cells alongside large oval ones\\n\\nManagement follows the etiology: replace BOTH — iron (often intravenously, since the duodenum is bypassed) and B12 by a route that does not need intrinsic factor. Giving folate alone would risk masking the B12 deficiency while the neuropathy progresses.\\n\\nEducational objective: A normal MCV does not exclude nutritional anemia, because coexisting microcytic and macrocytic deficiencies average to a normocytic value. A markedly raised RDW and a dimorphic smear reveal the combination, which is common after bariatric surgery and in malnutrition.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"1\": \"Early iron deficiency can be normocytic, but it does not widen the RDW to 22% and does not explain a B12 of 118 with neuropathy. Two deficiencies are present.\", \"2\": \"This is the anemia of chronic kidney disease. Her creatinine is normal, and it would not produce a markedly raised RDW.\", \"3\": \"Cold agglutinins falsely RAISE the MCV and lower the red cell count, with a hemoglobin that does not match three times the hematocrit. Her indices are internally consistent.\", \"4\": \"Reticulocytes are large and can lift the MCV — but only when the marrow is responding. Her reticulocyte count is low.\"}, \"eli5\": \"The machine reports the average size of her blood cells. Half are too small and half are too big, so the average looks normal even though both kinds are wrong.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_anemia_algorithm\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The lecture's anemia algorithm — split on MCV first, then on the reticulocyte count.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 55, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette, use clinical features and indices including the MCV to distinguish etiologies and mechanisms of anemia and generate a differential diagnosis (DDx).\", \"a\": \"s3-anemia-eval\", \"s\": \"§3\"}, {\"n\": 42, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette, generate a differential diagnosis of anemia based upon clinical features and labs. Formulate a management plan based upon the identified etiology.\", \"a\": \"s3-anemia-eval\", \"s\": \"§3\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 3-year-old girl is brought to the office by her father due to 2 months of irritability, intermittent abdominal pain, and constipation. She no longer uses several words she had learned. The family moved 4 months ago into a house built in 1938 that is being renovated, and she often chews on windowsills. Abdominal examination is benign.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  9.6 g/dL (N=11.0–14.0 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  29% (N=33%–42%)\\n  Mean corpuscular volume  69 µm3 (N=70–86 µm3)\\n  Ferritin  46 ng/mL (N=10–120 ng/mL)\\n  Transferrin saturation  24% (N=20%–50%)\\n  Zinc protoporphyrin  180 µg/dL (N=less than 35 µg/dL)\\nA peripheral smear is shown. Which of the following is most likely to be found on further evaluation of this patient?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Dense metaphyseal lines on radiographs\", \"B. Elevated hemoglobin A2 fraction\", \"C. Elevated urine porphobilinogen\", \"D. Hypersegmented neutrophils on the blood smear\", \"E. Increased red cell osmotic fragility\", \"F. Positive direct antiglobulin test\"], \"correct\": 0, \"explanation\": \"Lead poisoning: an old house under renovation, pica, colic, constipation, developmental regression (encephalopathy), a microcytic anemia with coarse basophilic stippling, and a raised zinc protoporphyrin with NORMAL iron studies.\\n\\nMechanism: lead inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase →\\n• ALA dehydratase block → δ-aminolevulinic acid accumulates, porphobilinogen stays normal (the discriminator from acute porphyria)\\n• Ferrochelatase block → iron cannot enter protoporphyrin → zinc is inserted instead → zinc protoporphyrin rises (a screen reflecting the prior 3 months)\\n• Impaired ribosomal RNA degradation → aggregated ribosomes → basophilic stippling\\n\\nLead also deposits where bone is actively growing, producing dense transverse lines at the ends of long bones on radiographs — part of the 'Burton lines' family (gingival lead lines plus dense lines at the growing ends of long bones). Other signs: facial pallor (earliest), wrist or foot drop, and saturnine gout. Confirm with a blood lead level (over 10 µg/dL is unsafe).\\n\\nEducational objective: Lead inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase, producing a microcytic anemia with basophilic stippling, raised zinc protoporphyrin, raised urine ALA with normal porphobilinogen, gingival lines, and dense lines at the growing ends of long bones.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"1\": \"Beta-thalassemia trait is also microcytic and can also show stippling — but it does not raise zinc protoporphyrin, and it does not cause colic or developmental regression.\", \"2\": \"Porphobilinogen rises in acute intermittent porphyria, where the block is at PBG deaminase. Lead blocks the step BEFORE porphobilinogen is made, so it stays normal.\", \"3\": \"These mark megaloblastic anemia, which is macrocytic. This anemia is microcytic.\", \"4\": \"This is the confirmatory finding in hereditary spherocytosis, a normocytic hemolytic anemia with spherocytes rather than stippled microcytes.\", \"5\": \"This identifies antibody-coated red cells in immune hemolysis. Lead injures heme synthesis, not the red cell surface.\"}, \"eli5\": \"Lead from old paint jams the machine that builds the iron part of blood, so the red cells come out small and speckled. Lead also sticks in growing bones, which shows up as bright white bands on an x-ray.\", \"image\": \"fig_hx_stipple_wright\", \"imageCaption\": \"Peripheral blood smear (Wright stain). The erythrocyte marked by the arrowhead contains numerous fine, dark blue-purple granules scattered evenly through its cytoplasm. A small lymphocyte is present at lower left.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_porph_lead\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Lead inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase within the heme synthesis pathway.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 35, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette, describe the pathophysiology, clinical features including labs and diagnostics and differential diagnosis (DDx) of porphyria including lead poisoning.\", \"a\": \"s6b-porphyria\", \"s\": \"§7\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 54-year-old man comes to the office due to 4 months of blisters on the backs of his hands that appear after he works in his garden and heal with scarring. He has also noticed new, darker hair growing over his cheeks and temples, and his urine has become tea-colored. He has chronic hepatitis C infection and drinks 6 beers daily. He has no abdominal pain, weakness, or mood change. His left hand is shown. Serum aminotransferases are twice the upper limit of normal and a positive urine porphyrin screen. Evaluation for which of the following coexisting conditions is most likely to be revealing in this patient?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Acute intermittent porphyria\", \"B. Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency\", \"C. Hereditary hemochromatosis\", \"D. Systemic lupus erythematosus\", \"E. Wilson disease\"], \"correct\": 2, \"explanation\": \"Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT) — the most common porphyria overall, from deficient uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase.\\n\\nThe recognition set: blistering photosensitivity on sun-exposed skin with erosions, scarring and milia; hypertrichosis of the cheeks and temples; and tea-colored urine from uroporphyrins spilling out of the liver. The associations are the precipitants in this man: hepatitis C, alcohol, iron overload, and end-stage kidney disease.\\n\\nWhy hemochromatosis: PCT is fundamentally an IRON-related disease. Hepatic iron → oxidative generation of an inhibitor of uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase → porphyrins accumulate → photosensitizing porphyrins reach the skin. Heterozygous hemochromatosis gene mutations are found in about two-thirds of patients — a classic 'one patient, two diseases in two systems' board question. Check iron studies.\\n\\nTreatment: direct-acting antivirals to cure the hepatitis C, phlebotomy to remove iron, hydroxychloroquine for skin symptoms, and sun avoidance until porphyrins normalize.\\n\\nEducational objective: Porphyria cutanea tarda presents with blistering photosensitivity, hypertrichosis and tea-colored urine, and is precipitated by hepatitis C, alcohol and iron overload. Heterozygous hemochromatosis mutations are present in about two-thirds of patients, so iron studies are required and phlebotomy is a core treatment.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"This is the NEUROVISCERAL porphyria — attacks of abdominal pain, neuropathy and psychiatric change with urine that darkens on standing — and it has no skin findings. The two porphyria branches are separate diseases with separate tests.\", \"1\": \"This causes liver disease and emphysema, but it has no connection to the heme pathway or to blistering photosensitivity.\", \"3\": \"Lupus is the photosensitive disease most people think of first, which is exactly why this patient could be misdiagnosed. Blistering with milia, hypertrichosis and tea-colored urine pull the diagnosis away from lupus.\", \"4\": \"Wilson disease causes liver and neuropsychiatric disease in YOUNG patients through copper accumulation. It does not cause a photocutaneous porphyria.\"}, \"eli5\": \"His liver is holding too much iron, and that iron jams one step of the machine that makes the red part of blood. The half-made pieces build up, travel to the skin, and blister when sunlight hits them.\", \"image\": \"fig_hx_pct\", \"imageCaption\": \"Dorsum of the left hand. A tense fluid-filled bulla sits on the index finger, with hemorrhagic crusted erosions over the knuckles and several small white papules and scaly scars on the back of the hand.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_porph_pathway\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Heme biosynthesis with the enzyme defect of each porphyria marked — porphyria cutanea tarda sits at uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 35, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette, describe the pathophysiology, clinical features including labs and diagnostics and differential diagnosis (DDx) of porphyria including lead poisoning.\", \"a\": \"s6b-porphyria\", \"s\": \"§7\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 66-year-old man comes to the office due to 6 weeks of fatigue and intermittently dark urine. Eight years ago he underwent mechanical aortic valve replacement, and he takes warfarin. Echocardiography shows a new moderate paravalvular leak. There is no splenomegaly. The plasma of a centrifuged blood sample is pink.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  9.0 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  27% (N=41%–53%)\\n  Mean corpuscular volume  92 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\\n  Reticulocyte count  6.8% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\\n  Lactate dehydrogenase  890 U/L (N=45–200 U/L)\\n  Haptoglobin less than  10 mg/dL (N=41–165 mg/dL)\\n  Bilirubin, total  2.4 mg/dL (N=0.1–1.0 mg/dL)\\nDirect antiglobulin test is negative. A peripheral smear is shown. Which of the following is most likely to be found in this patient?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Absent hemoglobin A on electrophoresis\", \"B. Heinz bodies on supravital stain\", \"C. Hemosiderin in urine sediment\", \"D. Increased osmotic fragility\", \"E. Palpable splenic enlargement\"], \"correct\": 2, \"explanation\": \"Intravascular hemolysis from a leaking mechanical valve — a macroangiopathic cause of fragmentation hemolysis.\\n\\nChain: turbulent jet → shear force → red cells torn apart inside the vessel (schistocytes, helmet cells) → free hemoglobin enters plasma (pink plasma = hemoglobinemia) → haptoglobin, the plasma 'mop', is consumed (undetectable) → once haptoglobin is saturated, free hemoglobin is filtered by the glomerulus (hemoglobinuria: dipstick positive for blood with no red cells on microscopy) → proximal tubular cells take up the hemoglobin, store its iron as hemosiderin and shed into the urine → urine hemosiderin, which persists for weeks after an episode.\\n\\nIntravascular versus extravascular:\\n• Intravascular — pink plasma, absent haptoglobin, hemoglobinuria and hemosiderinuria, schistocytes, normal spleen, HIGH kidney risk (free heme is nephrotoxic)\\n• Extravascular — yellow plasma, mildly low haptoglobin, no hemoglobinuria, spherocytes, splenomegaly\\n• Both — raised LDH, raised unconjugated bilirubin, raised reticulocytes\\n\\nLong-standing loss of iron in the urine can also make these patients iron deficient.\\n\\nEducational objective: Intravascular hemolysis releases free hemoglobin into plasma, producing hemoglobinemia, undetectable haptoglobin, hemoglobinuria and urine hemosiderin, with a risk of acute kidney injury. Extravascular hemolysis instead produces spherocytes and splenomegaly without free plasma hemoglobin.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"No hemoglobin A band is the diagnostic finding in homozygous sickle cell disease, which would have presented in childhood with sickle cells on the smear.\", \"1\": \"Heinz bodies are precipitated oxidized hemoglobin in G6PD deficiency after an oxidant exposure, removed by splenic macrophages to leave bite cells.\", \"3\": \"This belongs to hereditary spherocytosis — a lifelong EXTRAVASCULAR hemolysis with splenomegaly and spherocytes, not fragments.\", \"4\": \"Splenomegaly is the sign of EXTRAVASCULAR hemolysis, where the spleen does the destroying. These cells are being shredded inside the circulation.\"}, \"eli5\": \"His new heart valve is chopping red cells to pieces inside his blood vessels. The spilled red stuff leaks into his pee, and the kidney keeps some of its iron and sheds it, which the lab can find.\", \"image\": \"fig_hx_schisto\", \"imageCaption\": \"Peripheral blood smear. Among normal-appearing erythrocytes are numerous small, irregular red cell fragments — triangular, helmet-shaped and spiculated forms. A lymphocyte is present at right.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_maha\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The lecture's microangiopathic and mechanical hemolysis slide.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 25, \"t\": \"Distinguish between various causes of hemolytic anemia and characterize by site of hemolysis (intravascular versus extravascular), pathophysiology, laboratory testing and diagnostics.\", \"a\": \"s7-hemolysis\", \"s\": \"§8\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 15-year-old girl undergoes cholecystectomy due to recurrent right upper quadrant pain after meals. The opened gallbladder is shown. She has had episodes of yellow eyes since early childhood, usually during viral illnesses. Her father had his spleen removed as a teenager. The spleen tip is palpable 4 cm below the left costal margin.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  10.2 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  27% (N=36%–46%)\\n  Mean corpuscular volume  84 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\\n  Mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration  37.8% (N=31%–36%)\\n  Reticulocyte count  9.5% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\\n  Bilirubin, total  3.1 mg/dL (N=0.1–1.0 mg/dL)\\n  Bilirubin, direct  0.2 mg/dL (N=0.0–0.3 mg/dL)\\nDirect antiglobulin test is negative. This patient's underlying condition is most likely caused by a defect in which of the following?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Ankyrin\", \"B. Beta-globin chain\", \"C. CD55 and CD59\", \"D. Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase\", \"E. Pyruvate kinase\"], \"correct\": 0, \"explanation\": \"Hereditary spherocytosis. The gallbladder contains black pigment (calcium bilirubinate) stones — in a teenager, almost never ordinary gallbladder disease, and a signal of chronic hemolysis.\\n\\nThe chronic extravascular quartet is complete: anemia, jaundice (unconjugated — direct fraction normal), a big spleen, and pigment gallstones. Autosomal dominant inheritance shows in the father's teenage splenectomy.\\n\\nMechanism: defective cytoskeletal protein (ankyrin most commonly; also band 3, beta-spectrin, alpha-spectrin, protein 4.2) → the lattice cannot hold the biconcave shape → membrane is lost → the cell becomes a sphere with the least surface area for its volume → it cannot deform through the splenic cords → splenic macrophages remove it (extravascular hemolysis) → chronic bilirubin load → pigment stones.\\n\\nSupporting findings: a HIGH MCHC (10.2 ÷ 27 = 37.8%; the cell is small for its hemoglobin), increased osmotic fragility, a high reticulocyte count, and — the value that excludes the look-alike — a NEGATIVE direct antiglobulin test (warm autoimmune hemolysis also makes spherocytes but is antibody-coated). Viral illnesses can trigger hemolytic crises (reticulocytes high) or, with parvovirus B19, aplastic crises (reticulocytes low).\\n\\nEducational objective: Hereditary spherocytosis is an autosomal dominant defect of red cell cytoskeletal proteins such as ankyrin and spectrin, causing extravascular hemolysis with splenomegaly, a high MCHC, increased osmotic fragility and early pigment gallstones. A negative direct antiglobulin test separates it from warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"1\": \"Beta-globin mutations cause sickle cell disease and beta thalassemia. Thalassemia gives a LOW MCV and MCHC; sickle cell disease gives sickle cells and eventual loss, not enlargement, of the spleen.\", \"2\": \"Loss of these complement regulators is paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria — an acquired INTRAVASCULAR hemolysis with hemoglobinuria and thrombosis, not a dominantly inherited disease with splenomegaly.\", \"3\": \"G6PD deficiency is X-linked and episodic, triggered by oxidants, with bite cells and a normal MCHC. It does not give a raised MCHC or pass father to daughter as a dominant trait with splenectomy.\", \"4\": \"Pyruvate kinase deficiency is an autosomal recessive glycolytic defect causing chronic hemolysis without spherocytes or a raised MCHC.\"}, \"eli5\": \"Her red blood cells are missing a piece of their inside scaffolding, so they become little balls instead of flexible discs. The spleen keeps grabbing and breaking them, and all that broken blood turns into black stones in her gallbladder.\", \"image\": \"fig_hx_pigment_stones\", \"imageCaption\": \"Opened gallbladder with a 4 cm scale bar. The lumen is packed with numerous small, hard, jet-black granular stones; the mucosa is green and bile-stained.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_hx_spherocytes\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Peripheral smear from the same disease: small, dense erythrocytes lacking central pallor (arrow) among normal biconcave cells.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 28, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette of a patient with hemolytic anemia, characterize clinical presentations, labs and diagnostics (including peripheral smear + hemoglobin electrophoresis findings), therapies and long-term complications.\", \"a\": \"s8-membrane-enzyme\", \"s\": \"§9\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 24-year-old man comes to the emergency department due to 1 day of dark urine and yellow eyes. Three days ago he began trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for a urinary tract infection. His family emigrated from Sardinia. He had a similar episode as a child after eating broad beans. There is scleral icterus; the spleen is not palpable.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  8.1 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  24% (N=41%–53%)\\n  Mean corpuscular volume  94 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\\n  Reticulocyte count  7.2% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\\n  Lactate dehydrogenase  760 U/L (N=45–200 U/L)\\n  Haptoglobin  12 mg/dL (N=41–165 mg/dL)\\nDirect antiglobulin test is negative. A peripheral smear is shown. Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of this patient's hemolysis?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Antibody-mediated red cell opsonization\", \"B. Complement-mediated lysis of unprotected cells\", \"C. Cytoskeletal loss of membrane surface\", \"D. Deficient ATP generation through glycolysis\", \"E. Impaired regeneration of reduced glutathione\", \"F. Shear injury from platelet-rich microthrombi\"], \"correct\": 4, \"explanation\": \"G6PD deficiency (Mediterranean variant, WHO class II), with hemolysis triggered by a sulfonamide, after a childhood episode of favism.\\n\\nThe antioxidant chain: glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase → NADPH (the red cell's ONLY meaningful source) → glutathione reductase regenerates reduced glutathione → reduced glutathione neutralizes hydrogen peroxide. Break the first link → peroxide accumulates → hemoglobin is oxidized → it denatures and precipitates as Heinz bodies on the inner membrane → splenic macrophages bite out the inclusion (bite and blister cells) → hemolysis, largely intravascular during a crisis.\\n\\nWhy it is episodic: the variant enzyme is UNSTABLE. Old cells run out of activity; young cells are protected. A red cell has no nucleus and no mitochondria, so it cannot make replacement enzyme or find another NADPH source.\\n\\nTriggers: infections (the commonest), drugs (primaquine, chloroquine, sulfonamides, nitrofurantoin, dapsone) and fava beans. The enzyme assay can be falsely NORMAL during the crisis, because depleted old cells are gone and reticulocytes carry high enzyme levels — repeat it 2–3 months later or send a genetic test.\\n\\nEducational objective: G6PD deficiency limits NADPH production, so red cells cannot regenerate reduced glutathione to detoxify peroxide; oxidant stress (infection, sulfonamides, antimalarials, fava beans) precipitates hemoglobin as Heinz bodies, producing bite cells and episodic hemolysis with a negative direct antiglobulin test.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"Drug-induced IMMUNE hemolysis also follows a new drug, but the cells are antibody-coated and the direct antiglobulin test is positive. Here it is negative.\", \"1\": \"This is paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (loss of CD55 and CD59) — chronic intravascular hemolysis with thrombosis, not an episode triggered by a sulfonamide.\", \"2\": \"Hereditary spherocytosis gives chronic hemolysis with spherocytes, splenomegaly and a high MCHC, not bite cells after an oxidant drug.\", \"3\": \"Pyruvate kinase deficiency is chronic, not oxidant-triggered, and does not produce bite cells or Heinz bodies.\", \"5\": \"Thrombotic microangiopathies fragment cells into schistocytes and lower the platelet count. These cells have had a smooth bite removed, not been torn.\"}, \"eli5\": \"His red cells are missing the helper that cleans up harmful 'rust' chemicals. The new medicine made a lot of that rust, so the cells got damaged, the spleen took bites out of them, and many burst.\", \"image\": \"fig_bite_cells\", \"imageCaption\": \"Peripheral blood smear. Several erythrocytes have a smooth semicircular defect at the edge, as though a portion had been removed, and appear smaller and more densely stained than the surrounding cells.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_g6pd_pathway\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"G6PD generates NADPH, which keeps glutathione reduced so it can neutralize peroxide.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 28, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette of a patient with hemolytic anemia, characterize clinical presentations, labs and diagnostics (including peripheral smear + hemoglobin electrophoresis findings), therapies and long-term complications.\", \"a\": \"s8-membrane-enzyme\", \"s\": \"§9\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 2-year-old boy is brought to the office due to progressive pallor and poor weight gain. He was born at term and was well until about 6 months of age. His parents are first cousins of Cypriot descent. Examination shows frontal bossing, prominent maxillae, and a liver and spleen palpable 3 cm and 6 cm below the costal margins, respectively. A lateral skull radiograph is shown.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  6.1 g/dL (N=11.0–14.0 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  20% (N=33%–42%)\\n  Mean corpuscular volume  61 µm3 (N=70–86 µm3)\\n  Reticulocyte count  6.5% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\\n  Hemoglobin F  88% (N=less than 2%)\\n  Hemoglobin A2  5.4% (N=1.5%–3.5%)\\nHemoglobin A is not detected. Which of the following is the primary mechanism of this patient's anemia?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Formation of beta-chain tetramers\", \"B. Formation of gamma-chain tetramers\", \"C. Impaired heme synthesis in mitochondria\", \"D. Oxidative denaturation of hemoglobin\", \"E. Polymerization of deoxygenated hemoglobin\", \"F. Precipitation of unpaired alpha chains\"], \"correct\": 5, \"explanation\": \"Beta thalassemia major (homozygous beta-zero): no hemoglobin A, very high hemoglobin F, raised hemoglobin A2.\\n\\nWhy it hurts: too few beta chains → a relative EXCESS of normal alpha chains with nothing to pair with → free alpha chains aggregate into insoluble inclusions → (1) erythroid precursors die in the marrow (ineffective erythropoiesis) and (2) the survivors are removed by the spleen (extravascular hemolysis). Severity tracks the chain you have too MUCH of — free alpha chains are more damaging than free beta chains, which at least form hemoglobin H.\\n\\nWhy onset at 6 months: fetal hemoglobin (two alpha, two gamma) needs no beta chains, so the newborn is well; the anemia appears as the globin switch turns gamma off and beta fails to take over.\\n\\nWhy the skull: ineffective erythropoiesis drives massive marrow expansion → the diploic space widens with vertical trabeculae ('hair-on-end') → frontal bossing and maxillary prominence; extramedullary hematopoiesis enlarges the liver and spleen.\\n\\nManagement: lifelong transfusion every 3–4 weeks with iron chelation (the body cannot excrete iron), folic acid, genetic counseling (25% risk each pregnancy), and evaluation for transplant or gene therapy. Never give iron.\\n\\nEducational objective: In beta thalassemia, the relative excess of alpha-globin chains precipitates, killing erythroid precursors in the marrow and shortening red cell survival. Symptoms begin at 3–6 months as fetal hemoglobin declines, and marrow expansion produces bony deformities of the skull and face.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"Beta-chain tetramers are hemoglobin H, which forms in ALPHA thalassemia with one working alpha gene — the opposite chain imbalance.\", \"1\": \"Gamma-chain tetramers are hemoglobin Barts, formed in a fetus with NO alpha genes, causing hydrops fetalis. This child has abundant alpha chains.\", \"2\": \"This is sideroblastic anemia, with ringed sideroblasts and high iron — a heme defect, not a globin defect, and it would not alter the hemoglobin fractions.\", \"3\": \"This is the G6PD mechanism — episodic, trigger-driven hemolysis with bite cells, not lifelong transfusion-level anemia with a globin switch failure.\", \"4\": \"This is sickle cell disease, a QUALITATIVE beta-chain defect with sickle cells and hemoglobin S on electrophoresis.\"}, \"eli5\": \"His body can't make one of the two building blocks of hemoglobin, so the other building block piles up with no partner and clumps inside the cells, killing them. His bone marrow grows bigger and bigger trying to keep up, which changes the shape of his skull.\", \"image\": \"fig_hair_on_end\", \"imageCaption\": \"Lateral radiograph of the skull in a child. The space between the inner and outer tables of the cranial vault is widened, and fine striations run perpendicular to the skull surface.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_electrophoresis\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Hemoglobin electrophoresis patterns — beta thalassemia shows an enlarged hemoglobin F band because beta chains are missing.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 28, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette of a patient with hemolytic anemia, characterize clinical presentations, labs and diagnostics (including peripheral smear + hemoglobin electrophoresis findings), therapies and long-term complications.\", \"a\": \"s9-globin\", \"s\": \"§10\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 34-year-old man is brought to the emergency department due to 12 hours of fever, confusion, and a rapidly spreading rash. Four years ago he underwent emergency splenectomy for a shattered spleen after a motorcycle collision; he did not attend follow-up visits. Temperature is 39.8 C (103.6 F) and blood pressure is 76/40 mm Hg. Over the next day, the rash progresses to the changes shown in the extremities. Platelet count is 31,000/mm3, fibrinogen is 70 mg/dL, and prothrombin time is prolonged. Blood cultures grow gram-positive, lancet-shaped diplococci that are optochin-sensitive. Which of the following is the most important virulence factor of the causative organism in this patient?\", \"choices\": [\"A. IgA protease\", \"B. Lipooligosaccharide endotoxin\", \"C. M protein\", \"D. Polysaccharide capsule\", \"E. Protein A\"], \"correct\": 3, \"explanation\": \"Overwhelming post-splenectomy infection with Streptococcus pneumoniae, complicated by disseminated intravascular coagulation and purpura fulminans with peripheral gangrene.\\n\\nWhy the spleen matters: encapsulated bacteria resist direct phagocytosis → clearance depends on opsonization plus the splenic red pulp filter (its cords and sinusoids are the body's 'oil filter') and on marginal zone B cells that make antibody against polysaccharide antigens → without a spleen, opsonized encapsulated bacteria circulate unchecked → sepsis progresses in HOURS, not days.\\n\\nThe organisms that matter: Streptococcus pneumoniae (dominant, by a wide margin), Neisseria meningitidis and Haemophilus influenzae type b. Functional asplenia (sickle cell autosplenectomy) carries the same risk, and Howell-Jolly bodies on the smear announce it.\\n\\nPrevention is three-part: vaccinate against all three (before an elective splenectomy; after an emergency one — which this man never received), daily penicillin prophylaxis in children, and education that any fever is an emergency needing immediate empiric antibiotics without waiting for cultures.\\n\\nEducational objective: The spleen clears opsonized encapsulated bacteria from the blood, so anatomic or functional asplenia predisposes to fulminant sepsis from Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis and Haemophilus influenzae type b, whose polysaccharide capsules are their key virulence factor. Vaccination, pediatric penicillin prophylaxis and fever education prevent it.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"IgA protease helps pneumococci, meningococci and Haemophilus colonize mucosa. It does not explain why the organism overwhelms a patient WITHOUT A SPLEEN once it is in the blood.\", \"1\": \"This drives the fulminant sepsis of Neisseria meningitidis — also an encapsulated post-splenectomy pathogen — but meningococci are gram-negative diplococci.\", \"2\": \"M protein is the antiphagocytic factor of Streptococcus pyogenes, a beta-hemolytic chain-forming coccus that is not optochin-sensitive.\", \"4\": \"Protein A binds the Fc portion of IgG for Staphylococcus aureus, which grows in clusters and is not a characteristic post-splenectomy pathogen.\"}, \"eli5\": \"Some germs wear a slippery coat that makes them hard to grab. The spleen is the body's best net for catching them, so without a spleen those germs can take over the blood in just a few hours.\", \"image\": \"fig_opsi\", \"imageCaption\": \"Four panels of the patient's extremities over the following days: confluent purple-black discoloration of the hand and forearm and of the foot and leg, progressing to dry, blackened, necrotic digits with a sharply demarcated border of sloughing skin.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_postsplen_uw\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Why asplenia predisposes to encapsulated-organism sepsis.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 51, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette, identify the pathophysiology of post splenectomy complications including the serious complication of post-splenectomy infection and/or sepsis.\", \"a\": \"s10-spleen\", \"s\": \"§11\"}, {\"n\": 73, \"t\": \"Identify the primary mechanism of splenic injury, including acute/traumatic and chronic/pathologic etiologies, key anatomy and physiology of the spleen, and complications including splenic rupture, abscess/infarct, pseudoaneurysm, adjacent injury, thrombosis, and overwhelming post-splenectomy infection.\", \"a\": \"s52-splenic-trauma\", \"s\": \"§55\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 6-year-old girl with homozygous sickle cell disease is brought to the office for a routine health maintenance examination. She takes hydroxyurea and folic acid, and her vaccinations are up to date. She has had two vaso-occlusive pain episodes this year. Neurologic examination is normal. Screening transcranial Doppler ultrasonography shows a time-averaged mean maximum velocity of 228 cm/sec in the left middle cerebral artery (normal: less than 170 cm/sec), confirmed on repeat study. A peripheral smear is shown. Which of the following is the most appropriate intervention to reduce this patient's risk of the most likely serious complication?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Chronic red cell transfusion\", \"B. Continued daily penicillin prophylaxis\", \"C. Elective laparoscopic splenectomy\", \"D. Iron chelation with deferasirox\", \"E. Long-term warfarin anticoagulation\", \"F. Low-dose daily aspirin therapy\"], \"correct\": 0, \"explanation\": \"Sickle cell disease with an abnormal transcranial Doppler — high flow velocity through a narrowed middle cerebral artery → very high risk of ischemic stroke.\\n\\nMechanism: deoxygenated hemoglobin S polymerizes (valine for glutamic acid exposes a hydrophobic patch) → rigid sickled cells → vaso-occlusion plus hemolysis-driven vasculopathy (free hemoglobin scavenges nitric oxide) → progressive narrowing of large cerebral arteries.\\n\\nEvery sickle cell therapy attacks the same variable — the proportion and concentration of hemoglobin S. Chronic transfusion adds hemoglobin A, and randomized trial evidence shows that keeping hemoglobin S BELOW 30% significantly decreases stroke risk; this is done every 3–4 weeks, often by exchange transfusion so the patient is not made hyperviscous. Its costs are iron overload (→ chelation), alloimmunization (→ extended antigen-matched units), and infectious risk. Hydroxyurea (raises hemoglobin F), penicillin, vaccination and folic acid continue alongside it.\\n\\nEducational objective: Children with sickle cell disease and an abnormal transcranial Doppler velocity are at high risk of stroke and should receive chronic red cell transfusion to keep hemoglobin S below 30%. Transfusion carries the long-term costs of iron overload and alloimmunization.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"1\": \"Penicillin prevents pneumococcal sepsis in functionally asplenic young children. It does nothing about the cerebral vasculopathy the Doppler has detected.\", \"2\": \"Splenectomy is for recurrent splenic sequestration crises or massive splenomegaly — not for stroke risk, and it would worsen her infection risk.\", \"3\": \"Chelation treats iron overload that develops AFTER years of transfusion. It is a consequence of the correct answer, not a substitute for it.\", \"4\": \"Sickle cell stroke is driven by sickling vasculopathy of large cerebral arteries, not by a cardioembolic or venous clot. Anticoagulation adds bleeding risk without addressing hemoglobin S.\", \"5\": \"Aspirin is the secondary-prevention agent for atherosclerotic stroke in adults. It does not lower the hemoglobin S fraction, which is what drives sickle cell stroke.\"}, \"eli5\": \"Her sticky, bent blood cells are slowly narrowing a big blood vessel in her brain. Giving her regular healthy blood dilutes the bent cells so the vessel is less likely to block and cause a stroke.\", \"image\": \"fig_hx_sickle\", \"imageCaption\": \"Peripheral blood smear. Many erythrocytes are elongated, crescent-shaped and pointed at both ends (arrow); a few oval and round cells are present.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_sickle_complications\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The lecture's summary of sickle cell complications.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 7, \"t\": \"Describe management approaches for sickle cell disease as it pertains to prevention and complications.\", \"a\": \"s9-globin\", \"s\": \"§10\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 4-year-old boy with homozygous sickle cell disease is brought to the emergency department due to 2 days of lethargy and pallor. One week ago he had a low-grade fever and a red rash on both cheeks. He has not had pain and has not received a transfusion in the past year. His baseline hemoglobin is 8.2 g/dL. The spleen is not palpable.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  3.9 g/dL (N=11.0–14.0 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  12% (N=33%–42%)\\n  Mean corpuscular volume  84 µm3 (N=70–86 µm3)\\n  Leukocyte count  7,800/mm3 (N=5,500–15,500/mm3)\\n  Platelet count  290,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\\n  Reticulocyte count  0.1% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\\nThe peripheral smear shows sickled cells without hypersegmented neutrophils. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's current condition?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Alloantibody-mediated delayed hemolysis\", \"B. Folate depletion from high cell turnover\", \"C. Oxidant injury to hemoglobin\", \"D. Splenic pooling of sickled cells\", \"E. Vaso-occlusion in bone marrow\", \"F. Viral infection of erythroid precursors\"], \"correct\": 5, \"explanation\": \"Transient aplastic crisis from parvovirus B19 (the 'slapped cheek' rash of erythema infectiosum a week earlier).\\n\\nMechanism: parvovirus B19 uses the erythrocyte P antigen to enter erythroid progenitors → lytic infection → red cell production stops for about a week → harmless in a healthy person, whose red cells live 120 days → catastrophic in chronic hemolysis, where cells live only 10–20 days and survival depends on a marrow running at maximum → hemoglobin halves, and the reticulocyte count falls to almost zero.\\n\\nThree crises, three reticulocyte counts:\\n• Aplastic → reticulocytes LOW (production stopped)\\n• Sequestration → reticulocytes HIGH, spleen suddenly enormous, often low platelets (cells are alive but trapped)\\n• Vaso-occlusive → hemoglobin near baseline; the problem is pain and ischemia\\n\\nThe same crisis occurs in hereditary spherocytosis and other chronic hemolytic anemias. Management is transfusion and supportive care until the marrow recovers; the patient is contagious, so isolate him from pregnant staff and immunocompromised patients.\\n\\nEducational objective: Parvovirus B19 infects erythroid precursors and halts red cell production, causing an aplastic crisis with a precipitous hemoglobin fall and reticulocytopenia in patients with chronic hemolysis. Splenic sequestration also drops the hemoglobin but with reticulocytosis and a suddenly enlarged spleen.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"A delayed hemolytic transfusion reaction appears 2–10 days after a transfusion, with a positive direct antiglobulin test and a HIGH reticulocyte count. He has not been transfused.\", \"1\": \"Folate deficiency also lowers the reticulocyte count in chronic hemolysis, but it develops gradually and produces macrocytosis and hypersegmented neutrophils, which are absent.\", \"2\": \"Oxidant hemolysis (G6PD deficiency) would raise the reticulocyte count and produce bite cells.\", \"3\": \"Splenic sequestration also crashes the hemoglobin, but the spleen is suddenly ENORMOUS, the reticulocytes are HIGH, and platelets are often low. His spleen is not palpable.\", \"4\": \"Vaso-occlusive crises present with severe PAIN, and the hemoglobin is usually near baseline. He has no pain and has lost more than half his red cell mass.\"}, \"eli5\": \"A virus shut down his blood factory for a week. Most kids wouldn't notice, but his red cells die very fast, so without new ones his blood count fell dangerously low.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_parvo_pronormoblast\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Parvovirus B19 in the marrow — a giant pronormoblast with intranuclear inclusions, and an absence of maturing red cell precursors.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 7, \"t\": \"Describe management approaches for sickle cell disease as it pertains to prevention and complications.\", \"a\": \"s46-pediatric\", \"s\": \"§51\"}, {\"n\": 28, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette of a patient with hemolytic anemia, characterize clinical presentations, labs and diagnostics (including peripheral smear + hemoglobin electrophoresis findings), therapies and long-term complications.\", \"a\": \"s9-globin\", \"s\": \"§10\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 31-year-old woman at 36 weeks' gestation is brought to the emergency department due to severe abdominal pain and heavy vaginal bleeding. Placental abruption is diagnosed, and emergency cesarean delivery is performed. During surgery, she oozes from the incision, intravenous catheter sites, and gums. She has already received 4 units of red blood cells and 4 units of plasma.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  7.6 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  23% (N=36%–46%)\\n  Platelet count  64,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\\n  Prothrombin time  24 sec (N=11–15 sec)\\n  Partial thromboplastin time  58 sec (N=25–40 sec)\\n  Fibrinogen  78 mg/dL (N=200–400 mg/dL)\\n  D-dimer  6,400 ng/mL (N=less than 250 ng/mL)\\nA peripheral smear shows scattered red cell fragments. Which of the following blood products is most appropriate to correct this patient's most critical hemostatic deficit?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Albumin\", \"B. Cryoprecipitate\", \"C. Factor VIII concentrate\", \"D. Four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate\", \"E. Plasma\", \"F. Platelets\"], \"correct\": 1, \"explanation\": \"Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) triggered by placental abruption — blood clotting and breaking down clot at the same time: consumption of platelets and factors (prolonged PT and aPTT, low platelets), a very LOW fibrinogen, a HIGH D-dimer, and schistocytes from microthrombi.\\n\\nWhy fibrinogen first: the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways converge on thrombin converting fibrinogen to fibrin, the protein that actually forms and cross-links the clot. Obstetric hemorrhage drops patients into a low-fibrinogen state very quickly.\\n\\nCryoprecipitate: thaw frozen plasma in the refrigerator (1–6 C) → a cold-insoluble precipitate forms → it is concentrated fibrinogen plus factor VIII, von Willebrand factor, factor XIII and fibronectin → the fibrinogen of many units of plasma in a fraction of the volume. It is indicated for low fibrinogen, DIC, obstetric hemorrhage, and periodically during massive transfusion.\\n\\nProduct → deficit: red cells → oxygen-carrying capacity; platelets → thrombocytopenia (not immune thrombocytopenia or dengue); plasma → multiple factor deficiency with bleeding; cryoprecipitate → fibrinogen; 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate plus vitamin K → warfarin reversal; factor VIII concentrate → hemophilia A; albumin → volume only. The underlying cause (delivery) is treated alongside.\\n\\nEducational objective: Cryoprecipitate is the concentrated source of fibrinogen (with factor VIII, von Willebrand factor and factor XIII) and is the product of choice for hypofibrinogenemia in DIC and obstetric hemorrhage, where plasma cannot deliver enough fibrinogen in a tolerable volume.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"Albumin is a pathogen-inactivated colloid for volume expansion. It contains NO coagulation factors and does nothing for hemostasis.\", \"2\": \"A specific factor concentrate is the answer for an ISOLATED single-factor deficiency such as hemophilia A. This is a consumptive, multi-factor coagulopathy.\", \"3\": \"This contains factors II, VII, IX and X and is the product of choice for urgent WARFARIN reversal. It provides no fibrinogen.\", \"4\": \"Plasma carries fibrinogen only at physiologic concentration, so correcting a level of 78 mg/dL would take a volume she cannot tolerate — and she has already had 4 units.\", \"5\": \"Her platelet count is low but above the thresholds that would be the limiting problem. Without fibrinogen, platelets have no fibrin mesh to anchor.\"}, \"eli5\": \"Her blood used up all of its 'glue' protein by making tiny clots everywhere, so now she can't stop bleeding. Cryo is a small bag packed with that glue, so it replaces a lot of it quickly.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_dic\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The lecture's DIC mechanism slide — simultaneous consumption of clotting factors and fibrinolysis.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 74, \"t\": \"Identify which blood product should be used to correct specific coagulopathies / bleeding disorders.\", \"a\": \"s11-products\", \"s\": \"§12\"}, {\"n\": 31, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette, classify the different blood products and the clinical indications for each product, supported by evidence (if available).\", \"a\": \"s11-products\", \"s\": \"§12\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 58-year-old woman receiving warfarin for atrial fibrillation is admitted to the hospital due to a hip fracture. Before surgery, she receives 2 units of plasma. Forty minutes into the second unit, she develops acute dyspnea. Temperature is 38.4 C (101.1 F), blood pressure is 84/50 mm Hg, and oxygen saturation is 80% on room air. Jugular venous pressure is not elevated. Chest radiography shows new bilateral diffuse infiltrates with a normal cardiac silhouette, and serum brain natriuretic peptide is normal. Gram stain and culture of the remaining plasma show no organisms. Despite mechanical ventilation, she dies 3 days later. A section of her lung at autopsy is shown. The implicated plasma came from a woman with 4 prior pregnancies. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of this patient's reaction?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Bacterial endotoxin from a contaminated unit\", \"B. Donor anti-HLA antibodies against recipient leukocytes\", \"C. Donor lymphocytes engrafting in recipient tissue\", \"D. Hydrostatic pulmonary edema from excess infused volume\", \"E. Recipient anti-IgA antibodies against donor plasma proteins\", \"F. Recipient IgM antibodies against donor red cells\"], \"correct\": 1, \"explanation\": \"Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI) — hypoxemia with bilateral infiltrates within 6 hours of a plasma-containing product, with hypotension, fever, flat neck veins and a normal brain natriuretic peptide.\\n\\nMechanism: pregnancy exposes a woman to fetal cells bearing paternal tissue antigens → she forms anti-HLA antibodies (14%–20% of women who have been pregnant; more with each pregnancy) → they do her no harm → she donates → her plasma carries the antibodies to the recipient → they bind recipient leukocytes → leukoagglutination and neutrophil activation in the pulmonary capillaries → capillary leak → noncardiogenic pulmonary edema → diffuse alveolar damage with hyaline membranes (the autopsy section), the acute respiratory distress syndrome picture.\\n\\nTRALI versus TACO — both are hypoxic with a white-out:\\n• TRALI — immune capillary injury; blood pressure LOW, jugular venous pressure normal, diuresis does not help\\n• TACO — too much volume too fast; blood pressure HIGH, jugular venous pressure raised, diuresis helps\\n\\nPrevention is a public health success: blood centers test female donors for anti-HLA antibodies and avoid making high-plasma-volume products from those who have them. Management is supportive.\\n\\nEducational objective: TRALI is caused by donor anti-HLA (anti-leukocyte) antibodies, most often from multiparous female donors, that activate recipient neutrophils in the pulmonary capillaries, producing hypoxemia, hypotension and bilateral infiltrates within 6 hours. Circulatory overload instead causes hypertension, raised jugular venous pressure and responds to diuresis.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"A septic reaction also brings fever and hypotension, most often from a room-temperature PLATELET unit. Plasma is stored frozen, and the unit culture here is sterile.\", \"2\": \"Transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease appears 1–6 WEEKS later with pancytopenia, rash, diarrhea and liver dysfunction; it is prevented by irradiation.\", \"3\": \"Circulatory overload (TACO) gives HYPERtension, a raised jugular venous pressure and a raised brain natriuretic peptide, and it responds to diuresis. She is hypotensive with flat neck veins.\", \"4\": \"Anaphylaxis in an IgA-deficient recipient begins within minutes, after a tiny volume, with urticaria, throat tightness and wheeze rather than fever and noncardiogenic pulmonary edema.\", \"5\": \"ABO incompatibility produces acute INTRAVASCULAR hemolysis — flank pain, pink plasma, hemoglobinuria and a positive direct antiglobulin test. Plasma carries no donor red cells to attack.\"}, \"eli5\": \"The blood donor's body had made tiny 'attack' proteins during her past pregnancies. When they went into this patient, they made her white blood cells clump in her lungs, and the lungs flooded with fluid.\", \"image\": \"fig_hx_hyaline\", \"imageCaption\": \"Lung, hematoxylin and eosin. Alveolar walls are thickened and congested; many alveolar spaces are lined by bands of homogeneous, glassy pink material, and there is scattered intra-alveolar hemorrhage. A bronchiole and a congested vessel run down the left side.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_tx_reactions\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The transfusion reaction differential side by side.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 61, \"t\": \"Identify adverse events in transfusion medicine and classify by clinical presentation and pathophysiology.\", \"a\": \"s13-reactions\", \"s\": \"§14\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 44-year-old woman comes to the emergency department due to a painful, darkening lesion on her left thigh. Four days ago she was diagnosed with a proximal deep vein thrombosis and started on warfarin; no parenteral anticoagulant was given. Her brother had an unprovoked pulmonary embolism at age 30. Her platelet count is 268,000/mm3 and the international normalized ratio is 3.1. The lesion is shown. Biopsy of the affected skin shows fibrin thrombi occluding dermal and subcutaneous venules with hemorrhagic necrosis of the overlying tissue and no vasculitis. Which of the following best explains this patient's lesion?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Antibodies against a heparin-platelet factor 4 complex\", \"B. Calcium phosphate deposition in small vessels\", \"C. Faster decline of protein C than prothrombin\", \"D. Immune complex deposition in venule walls\", \"E. Mutant factor V resisting inactivation by protein C\"], \"correct\": 2, \"explanation\": \"Warfarin-induced skin necrosis, from starting warfarin without bridging.\\n\\nWarfarin inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase → the liver cannot carboxylate factors II, VII, IX and X, or the natural anticoagulants protein C and protein S. Those proteins do not disappear at the same rate — each falls at the pace of its own half-life:\\n• Factor VII ~4–6 hours (shortest) — this is what moves the INR first\\n• Protein C ~8 hours — the natural ANTICOAGULANT\\n• Factor IX ~24 hours\\n• Factor X ~48–72 hours\\n• Factor II (prothrombin) ~60 hours (longest)\\n\\nSo in the first days the body loses protein C before it loses prothrombin → a transient HYPERcoagulable window → thrombosis of dermal and subcutaneous venules, classically over fatty areas (thigh, breast, buttock) → hemorrhagic skin necrosis. The risk is greatest in those with underlying protein C deficiency or another thrombophilia, and after a large loading dose.\\n\\nThe same arithmetic explains the other trap: an INR of 2.3 on day 2 reflects loss of factor VII, not true anticoagulation, because prothrombin is still present. Both problems have one fix — bridge with heparin or enoxaparin for at least 5 days AND until the INR has been therapeutic for 24 hours.\\n\\nEducational objective: Warfarin depletes protein C faster than prothrombin, creating a transient hypercoagulable state in the first days of therapy that can cause skin necrosis over fatty areas. Bridging with a heparin until the INR has been therapeutic for 24 hours, for at least 5 days, prevents both this and inadequate early anticoagulation.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"This is heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, which also causes thrombosis with skin necrosis — but it requires heparin exposure, and the platelet count falls by more than half. She received no heparin and her platelets are normal.\", \"1\": \"This is calciphylaxis, which produces very similar painful necrotic skin lesions — but in patients with end-stage kidney disease and disordered calcium and phosphate, not 4 days after starting a drug.\", \"3\": \"This is leukocytoclastic vasculitis, which shows fibrinoid necrosis of vessel walls with a neutrophilic infiltrate and nuclear dust. The biopsy explicitly shows bland thrombi without vasculitis.\", \"4\": \"Factor V Leiden explains why she and her brother clot in the first place, and it amplifies this complication — but on its own it does not produce skin necrosis in the first days of a new drug.\"}, \"eli5\": \"Warfarin switches off both the body's clot-makers and its clot-brakes, and the brakes wear off first. For a few days she was actually more likely to clot, so tiny vessels in her skin blocked and the skin died.\", \"image\": \"fig_warfarin_necrosis\", \"imageCaption\": \"Photograph of the lateral thigh. There is a well-demarcated area of black, necrotic tissue with a friable, hyperemic border and surrounding erythema.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_warf_onset\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Clotting factor half-lives — protein C falls well before prothrombin, which is the whole reason bridging exists.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 26, \"t\": \"Explain why the onset of warfarin’s action is relatively slow and the need for bridging with another anticoagulant (heparin or enoxaparin) based on its mechanism of action.\", \"a\": \"s14-anticoag\", \"s\": \"§15\"}, {\"n\": 48, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette, identify the basic mechanisms of the most common acquired and hereditary bleeding disorders. Demonstrate an understanding of the basic mechanisms of thrombosis including those related to anticoagulation therapy.\", \"a\": \"s14-anticoag\", \"s\": \"§15\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 71-year-old man comes to the emergency department due to 2 days of painless gross hematuria and new bruises over both forearms. He has atrial fibrillation and has taken warfarin for 3 years, with an international normalized ratio consistently between 2.2 and 2.6 at monthly checks. Five days ago he was prescribed fluconazole for esophageal candidiasis. He takes no other new medications, has not changed his diet, and does not drink alcohol. Vital signs are stable.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  11.4 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  34% (N=41%–53%)\\n  Platelet count  232,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\\n  Prothrombin time  52 sec (N=11–15 sec)\\n  International normalized ratio  6.9 (N=0.9–1.1)\\nWhich of the following best explains this patient's laboratory findings?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Additive inhibition of platelet aggregation\", \"B. Decreased hepatic metabolism of warfarin\", \"C. Increased hepatic clearance by enzyme induction\", \"D. Loss of gut flora that synthesize vitamin K\", \"E. Reduced dietary intake of vitamin K\", \"F. Variant VKORC1 conferring target sensitivity\"], \"correct\": 1, \"explanation\": \"A pharmacokinetic drug-drug interaction. Fluconazole inhibits CYP2C9, the enzyme that clears warfarin → warfarin accumulates → the vitamin K-dependent factors II, VII, IX and X fall further → INR rises → bleeding.\\n\\nThe warfarin interaction list is the highest-yield in hematology, and it sorts by mechanism:\\n• ↑ INR by CYP2C9 INHIBITION — amiodarone, metronidazole, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, fluconazole, ciprofloxacin, fluvoxamine\\n• ↓ INR by enzyme INDUCTION — rifampin, carbamazepine, phenytoin, St John's wort\\n• ↑ bleeding at an UNCHANGED INR — aspirin, other antiplatelets, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (pharmacodynamic)\\n• ↑ INR from LESS SUBSTRATE — any antibiotic that kills vitamin K-producing gut flora\\n• Food and drink — a sudden increase in leafy greens lowers the INR (consistency matters more than avoidance); an acute alcohol binge raises it, while chronic use induces and lowers it\\n\\nManagement here: hold warfarin, give vitamin K, and for serious or life-threatening bleeding add 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate — which corrects the INR immediately, while vitamin K restores his own ability to make factors so the INR does not rebound as the concentrate clears.\\n\\nEducational objective: Azole antifungals, amiodarone, metronidazole and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole inhibit CYP2C9 and raise the INR in patients on warfarin, whereas rifampin and other inducers lower it. Antiplatelet drugs increase bleeding without changing the INR.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"This is how aspirin and other nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs raise bleeding risk with warfarin — a pharmacodynamic interaction that leaves the INR UNCHANGED. His INR has nearly tripled.\", \"2\": \"Rifampin, carbamazepine, phenytoin and St John's wort induce metabolism, which LOWERS the INR and risks clotting. That is the opposite direction.\", \"3\": \"This is the pharmacodynamic half of the antibiotic interaction and does raise the INR — but fluconazole is an antifungal and does not eliminate the vitamin K-producing bacterial flora.\", \"4\": \"A sudden fall in leafy green intake raises the INR, and a sudden rise lowers it. His diet is explicitly unchanged.\", \"5\": \"Polymorphisms in VKORC1 and CYP2C9 explain why dose requirements differ so much between patients — but they are present from the start and would not let him run a stable INR for 3 years before suddenly changing.\"}, \"eli5\": \"The new antifungal pill blocks the liver enzyme that normally chews up his blood thinner, so the thinner piled up and his blood stopped clotting at all.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_warf_reversal\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Stepwise reversal of warfarin anticoagulation.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 8, \"t\": \"Describe several examples of pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic drug interactions with warfarin and the need for dosage modifications based on pharmacogenomics, drug/drug, drug/food and drug/drink interactions.\", \"a\": \"s14-anticoag\", \"s\": \"§15\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 68-year-old man is brought to the emergency department due to sudden right-sided weakness and difficulty speaking that began 90 minutes ago. He takes low-dose aspirin and a statin. Blood pressure is 168/92 mm Hg. Noncontrast head computed tomography shows no hemorrhage, and intravenous alteplase is started. Two hours later he develops a severe headache, vomiting, and a declining level of consciousness, and he dies the following day. A coronal section of the brain at autopsy is shown. Which of the following drug actions is most directly responsible for this complication?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Blockade of platelet adenosine diphosphate receptors\", \"B. Conversion of plasminogen to plasmin\", \"C. Inhibition of platelet cyclooxygenase-1\", \"D. Inhibition of vitamin K epoxide reductase\", \"E. Potentiation of antithrombin activity\"], \"correct\": 1, \"explanation\": \"Intracranial hemorrhage after thrombolysis — the feared complication of the -plase drugs, and the reason their contraindication list exists.\\n\\nMechanism: alteplase (recombinant tissue plasminogen activator) converts plasminogen → plasmin → plasmin digests the fibrin mesh holding a clot together (fibrin degradation products are measured as the D-dimer). These agents are only RELATIVELY fibrin-selective, so hemostatic plugs elsewhere are lysed too — systemic bleeding is the defining risk, and bleeding into the brain is the one you cannot take back.\\n\\nIf catastrophic bleeding occurs: stop the infusion, image the head, give CRYOPRECIPITATE to replace the fibrinogen plasmin has consumed, and give an ANTIFIBRINOLYTIC — tranexamic acid or aminocaproic acid — which blocks plasminogen from becoming plasmin, the exact mirror image of the drug.\\n\\nThe contraindications all follow from 'you are about to dissolve every clot in the body': prior intracranial hemorrhage, ischemic stroke within 3 months, intracranial neoplasm or vascular malformation, suspected aortic dissection, active internal bleeding or bleeding diathesis, significant closed head trauma within 3 months, and severe uncontrolled hypertension.\\n\\nEducational objective: Thrombolytics (alteplase, reteplase, tenecteplase) convert plasminogen to plasmin, which degrades fibrin; intracranial hemorrhage is the feared complication. Treatment is to stop the drug, replace fibrinogen with cryoprecipitate, and give tranexamic or aminocaproic acid to block further plasmin generation.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"This is clopidogrel and the other P2Y12 blockers. They raise bleeding risk modestly but do not dissolve established fibrin, and he was not taking one.\", \"2\": \"This is his aspirin, which irreversibly acetylates cyclooxygenase-1 and blocks thromboxane A2 for the platelet's 7–10 day life. It contributes to bleeding risk but cannot lyse an intracranial clot within 2 hours.\", \"3\": \"This is warfarin's mechanism, producing bleeding through a multiple factor deficiency over days. He is not on warfarin, and the event was immediate.\", \"4\": \"This is how the heparins work. Heparin-associated bleeding is possible, but he received a plasminogen activator, which is far more likely to cause intracranial hemorrhage.\"}, \"eli5\": \"The clot-busting drug switches on the body's demolition crew to chew up a clot in a brain artery. The crew does not only work where you want it to, so it also tore open a vessel and the brain bled.\", \"image\": \"fig_hx_ich_gross\", \"imageCaption\": \"Coronal section of the brain at autopsy. A large, dark, blood-filled cavity (diamond) occupies the deep gray matter of one hemisphere, destroying the surrounding tissue and shifting the midline.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_fibrinolysis\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The fibrinolysis diagram — plasminogen activators on one side, tranexamic and aminocaproic acid blocking the same step on the other.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 62, \"t\": \"Identify and describe the fibrinolytic drugs, mechanism of action, pharmacokinetics, and adverse effects.\", \"a\": \"s15-antiplatelet\", \"s\": \"§16\"}, {\"n\": 12, \"t\": \"Describe the methods of reversal of drug toxicities caused by anticoagulants and thrombolytics.\", \"a\": \"s15-antiplatelet\", \"s\": \"§16\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 30-year-old woman, gravida 2 para 1, at 9 weeks' gestation comes to the office due to 3 days of left calf pain and swelling. She has no chronic medical conditions and takes only a prenatal vitamin. The left calf is 4 cm larger in circumference than the right and is tender along the deep venous course. Compression ultrasonography shows a noncompressible left popliteal vein with thrombus extending into the femoral vein. Serum creatinine is 0.7 mg/dL (N=0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Which of the following is the most appropriate anticoagulant for this patient?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Apixaban\", \"B. Dabigatran\", \"C. Enoxaparin\", \"D. Fondaparinux\", \"E. Warfarin\"], \"correct\": 2, \"explanation\": \"Acute proximal deep vein thrombosis in the first trimester. Pregnancy is a hypercoagulable state, and the anticoagulant choice is decided by what crosses the placenta.\\n\\nHeparins — unfractionated and low-molecular-weight — are large, highly charged molecules that do NOT cross the placenta, so they are the anticoagulants of pregnancy. Low-molecular-weight heparin is preferred for an outpatient: predictable dosing, no routine monitoring (check an anti-factor Xa level in pregnancy, obesity or renal impairment), subcutaneous administration, and a lower risk of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia than unfractionated heparin.\\n\\nWarfarin is small, crosses the placenta, and is teratogenic. Direct oral anticoagulants are avoided because safety is not established.\\n\\nThe rest of the agent-selection logic, for contrast: severe renal impairment → unfractionated heparin (not renally cleared); a need to stop quickly → unfractionated heparin (short half-life plus full reversal with protamine); heparin-induced thrombocytopenia → argatroban, bivalirudin or fondaparinux, never a heparin and never warfarin alone; mechanical heart valve → warfarin; routine outpatient venous thromboembolism or atrial fibrillation → a direct oral anticoagulant, usually apixaban.\\n\\nEducational objective: Heparins do not cross the placenta and are the anticoagulants of choice in pregnancy, with low-molecular-weight heparin preferred for outpatient therapy. Warfarin is teratogenic and direct oral anticoagulants lack established pregnancy safety data.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"Direct oral factor Xa inhibitors are convenient and need no monitoring, but safety in pregnancy is not established, so they are avoided.\", \"1\": \"The same problem as the -xabans: a direct oral anticoagulant with no established pregnancy safety data. It is also the one reversed by idarucizumab, which is irrelevant here.\", \"3\": \"This synthetic pentasaccharide is the agent of choice in heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, but pregnancy data are limited and it has NO effective reversal agent — protamine does not work.\", \"4\": \"Warfarin is teratogenic (fetal warfarin syndrome) and is contraindicated in pregnancy. It is small enough to cross the placenta, which is precisely the difference from the heparins.\"}, \"eli5\": \"Her blood thinner has to be one that cannot cross into the baby. Heparin shots are too big to get across; warfarin pills are small enough and can hurt the baby.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 69, \"t\": \"Identify the clinical uses including pregnancy use, contraindications, and warnings/precautions for anticoagulants, thrombolytics, and antiplatelet drugs.\", \"a\": \"s14-anticoag\", \"s\": \"§15\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 58-year-old man comes to the emergency department due to 40 minutes of crushing substernal chest pain. Three weeks ago he underwent percutaneous coronary intervention with a drug-eluting stent to the left anterior descending artery and was discharged on aspirin 81 mg and clopidogrel daily, which he has taken without missing a dose. Two weeks ago his primary physician added omeprazole for dyspepsia. ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads V2 through V4. Platelet count is 244,000/mm3. Coronary angiography shows a fresh occlusive thrombus within the previously placed stent. Which of the following best explains this patient's presentation?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Antibody-mediated activation of platelets\", \"B. Decreased formation of the active metabolite\", \"C. Increased platelet turnover restoring cyclooxygenase\", \"D. Loss of prostacyclin from a high aspirin dose\", \"E. Resistance of factor V to activated protein C\"], \"correct\": 1, \"explanation\": \"Stent thrombosis from a pharmacokinetic drug interaction.\\n\\nClopidogrel is a PRODRUG. It requires hepatic CYP2C19 to be converted to its active metabolite, which then irreversibly blocks the platelet P2Y12 adenosine diphosphate receptor. Omeprazole inhibits CYP2C19 → less active metabolite → inadequate platelet inhibition → thrombosis inside a stent whose struts are not yet covered by endothelium.\\n\\nWhat to do instead: pantoprazole, which does not meaningfully inhibit CYP2C19, or switch to ticagrelor, which is not a prodrug (but causes dyspnea and bradyarrhythmias, and requires maintenance aspirin ≤ 100 mg/day). Genetic CYP2C19 poor metabolizers have the same problem without any interacting drug.\\n\\nThe class in one line each: clopidogrel needs activation; prasugrel is more potent and is contraindicated after stroke or transient ischemic attack; ticagrelor is reversible and not a prodrug; cangrelor is intravenous with onset and offset in minutes. And the framing rule — arterial 'white clots' are platelet-rich and treated with antiplatelet drugs, while venous 'red clots' are fibrin-rich and treated with anticoagulants.\\n\\nEducational objective: Clopidogrel is a prodrug activated by CYP2C19, so CYP2C19 inhibitors such as omeprazole blunt its antiplatelet effect and can precipitate stent thrombosis. Pantoprazole or a non-prodrug P2Y12 inhibitor such as ticagrelor avoids the interaction.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia does cause arterial and venous thrombosis, but it requires heparin exposure within the preceding days to weeks and produces a platelet count that has fallen by more than half. His count is normal.\", \"2\": \"New platelets restore thromboxane production only if aspirin is stopped, because aspirin inhibits cyclooxygenase-1 irreversibly for each platelet's life. He has taken his aspirin.\", \"3\": \"This is the aspirin dose paradox: higher doses also inhibit endothelial cyclooxygenase-2 and the prostacyclin it makes. He is on 81 mg, the antiplatelet dose.\", \"4\": \"Factor V Leiden is a venous thrombophilia — deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism. Stent thrombosis is a platelet-rich arterial event.\"}, \"eli5\": \"His anti-clot pill only works after the liver switches it on. The heartburn pill blocked that switch, so his platelets stayed sticky and a clot formed inside the new stent.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_kp_antiplatelet\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The antiplatelet summary — mechanism, activation requirement and interactions by agent.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 71, \"t\": \"Identify the major classes and names of the antiplatelet drugs and describe their mechanisms of action, adverse effects, and drug/drug interactions.\", \"a\": \"s15-antiplatelet\", \"s\": \"§16\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 2-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department 3 hours after being found with an open bottle of her mother's ferrous sulfate tablets. She has vomited five times, twice with blood, and has had two episodes of bloody diarrhea. She is lethargic. Blood pressure is 74/40 mm Hg and pulse is 168/min.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Sodium  138 mEq/L (N=136–146 mEq/L)\\n  Bicarbonate  11 mEq/L (N=22–28 mEq/L)\\n  Glucose  186 mg/dL (N=70–100 mg/dL)\\n  Anion gap  24 mEq/L (N=8–12 mEq/L)\\nAbdominal radiography shows multiple radiopaque tablets in the stomach. Which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Activated charcoal\", \"B. Deferasirox\", \"C. Deferiprone\", \"D. Deferoxamine\", \"E. Dimercaprol\", \"F. Succimer\"], \"correct\": 3, \"explanation\": \"Acute iron poisoning — one of the leading causes of poisoning death in young children, and classically an accidental ingestion of an adult's iron tablets.\\n\\nMechanism and staging: free iron is directly corrosive to gastrointestinal mucosa → vomiting, hematemesis and bloody diarrhea from hemorrhagic gastritis, with hypovolemia from fluid loss. Absorbed iron then uncouples oxidative phosphorylation and impairs mitochondrial function → anion-gap metabolic acidosis, shock, and later hepatic necrosis.\\n\\nThe antidote is DEFEROXAMINE, given parenterally: it chelates free iron and is excreted in the urine, classically turning it a reddish 'vin rosé' colour. Supportive care and, in massive ingestion, whole bowel irrigation accompany it. Radiopaque tablets on an abdominal film confirm a substantial ingestion and identify tablets still available for removal.\\n\\nKeep the three chelators straight: deferasirox (oral, once daily; renal, hepatic and gastrointestinal bleeding warnings), deferiprone (oral, three times daily; agranulocytosis), and deferoxamine (parenteral, 8–12 hour infusions, ocular and auditory toxicity) — which is also the acute antidote.\\n\\nEducational objective: Acute iron overdose causes hemorrhagic gastroenteritis, shock and an anion-gap metabolic acidosis, and is treated with parenteral deferoxamine. The oral chelators deferasirox and deferiprone are for chronic transfusional iron overload.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"Charcoal adsorbs many ingested drugs but binds iron poorly, so it does not help here. Whole bowel irrigation is the decontamination option in a massive ingestion.\", \"1\": \"An ORAL chelator for chronic transfusional iron overload, dosed once daily and carrying boxed warnings for renal failure, hepatic failure and gastrointestinal hemorrhage. It is not the acute antidote, and this child cannot keep anything down.\", \"2\": \"Also an oral chelator for chronic overload, given three times daily, with a boxed warning for agranulocytosis requiring weekly white cell counts.\", \"4\": \"A chelator for arsenic, gold and severe lead poisoning. Lead would give a microcytic anemia with basophilic stippling, not radiopaque tablets and hemorrhagic gastritis.\", \"5\": \"The oral chelator used for childhood lead poisoning. Wrong metal — and there is no history of exposure to lead paint or pica here.\"}, \"eli5\": \"The iron pills burned the inside of her stomach and then poisoned her cells. The medicine given grabs onto the loose iron and carries it out in her pee.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_iron_toxicity\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Reversal of acute iron toxicity and chronic iron overload — the three chelators side by side.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 9, \"t\": \"Describe the adverse effects of iron therapy and the reversal agents for acute iron toxicity and chronic iron overload.\", \"a\": \"s16-anemia-drugs\", \"s\": \"§17\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 66-year-old man comes to the office for follow-up of anemia of chronic kidney disease. He has received hemodialysis for 3 years and epoetin alfa for the past 6 weeks. He reports a dull headache for several days. His hemoglobin was 9.2 g/dL two weeks ago and is 10.9 g/dL today. Ferritin is 420 ng/mL (N=20–250 ng/mL) and transferrin saturation is 32% (N=20%–50%). Blood pressure is 176/102 mm Hg, compared with 138/84 mm Hg before treatment was started. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Add intravenous iron sucrose\", \"B. Continue the current dose unchanged\", \"C. Decrease the epoetin alfa dose\", \"D. Increase the dose to target 12 g/dL\", \"E. Switch to oral vadadustat\", \"F. Transfuse one unit of red blood cells\"], \"correct\": 2, \"explanation\": \"Two numbers govern erythropoiesis-stimulating agent dosing, and this patient has broken both.\\n\\n• The CEILING — every boxed warning turns on the same threshold: targeting a hemoglobin above 11 g/dL causes harm with no benefit (increased death, myocardial infarction, stroke, venous thromboembolism and vascular access thrombosis).\\n• The RATE — if the hemoglobin rises more than 1 g/dL in any 2-week period, decrease the dose. His rose 1.7 g/dL.\\n\\nWhy the rate matters: hypertension is the most common adverse effect, occurring in up to a third of treated patients, and a rapid rise in red cell mass drives it. Hypertensive encephalopathy has been reported, and seizures are a recognised risk in chronic kidney disease. Control blood pressure before starting, and note that subcutaneous dosing raises blood pressure less and reaches target at 15%–30% lower doses than intravenous.\\n\\nMechanism for context: these agents stimulate division and differentiation of committed erythroid progenitors and push reticulocytes into the blood — reticulocytes rise first, then hematocrit and hemoglobin. They are approved for anemia of chronic kidney disease and chemotherapy-induced anemia, only when the goal is avoiding transfusion, and only after iron stores are checked and repleted.\\n\\nEducational objective: Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents should be dosed to the lowest level that avoids transfusion; targeting a hemoglobin above 11 g/dL increases cardiovascular events and death, and a rise of more than 1 g/dL in 2 weeks requires a dose reduction. Hypertension is the most common adverse effect.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"Functional iron deficiency is the commonest reason an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent appears not to work — but his ferritin and transferrin saturation show replete stores, and his hemoglobin is rising too fast, not too slowly.\", \"1\": \"This ignores both boxed-warning rules at once: the rate of rise and the developing hypertension, which is the most common adverse effect of the class and has progressed to symptoms.\", \"3\": \"Targeting hemoglobin above 11 g/dL increases death, serious cardiovascular events and stroke in chronic kidney disease, with no additional benefit. The goal is the lowest dose that avoids transfusion.\", \"4\": \"The hypoxia-inducible factor prolyl hydroxylase inhibitor is an oral alternative for dialysis patients, but it carries its own boxed warning for thrombotic events and is contraindicated in uncontrolled hypertension — exactly what he now has.\", \"5\": \"Transfusion is for symptomatic anemia, usually below a hemoglobin of 7 g/dL. His hemoglobin is 10.9 and climbing.\"}, \"eli5\": \"The medicine tells his marrow to build red cells, and it worked too fast. Thicker blood pushed his blood pressure up, so the dose comes down rather than up.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_esa_ae\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Adverse effects of erythropoiesis-stimulating agents.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 70, \"t\": \"Identify the erythropoiesis-stimulating agents (ESAs) and hypoxia-inducible factor prolyl hydroxylase inhibitor (HIF-PHI) and describe their mechanisms of action, route of administration, clinical use, pregnancy use, contraindications, warnings/precautions, adverse effects, and when the medications should be initiated.\", \"a\": \"s16-anemia-drugs\", \"s\": \"§17\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 62-year-old woman comes to the emergency department due to 4 days of mouth pain and inability to eat. She has rheumatoid arthritis treated with methotrexate 15 mg once weekly. Two weeks ago she misread a new prescription label and has been taking 15 mg daily since. Examination shows confluent oral ulceration.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  8.9 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  27% (N=36%–46%)\\n  Mean corpuscular volume  109 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\\n  Leukocyte count  1,300/mm3 (N=4,500–11,000/mm3)\\n  Platelet count  48,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\\n  Creatinine  1.1 mg/dL (N=0.6–1.2 mg/dL)\\nThe peripheral smear shows macro-ovalocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils. In addition to stopping the drug and providing supportive care, which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Cyanocobalamin\", \"B. Epoetin alfa\", \"C. Filgrastim\", \"D. Folic acid\", \"E. Leucovorin\"], \"correct\": 4, \"explanation\": \"Methotrexate toxicity from a dosing error — weekly therapy taken daily. Methotrexate inhibits DIHYDROFOLATE REDUCTASE → no tetrahydrofolate → thymidine synthesis fails → DNA synthesis stops in the fastest-dividing tissues.\\n\\nThat single mechanism produces the whole picture: the marrow empties (pancytopenia with a MACROCYTIC anemia, macro-ovalocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils — the same megaloblastic morphology as B12 or folate deficiency, because the biochemical lesion is the same), the gut mucosa ulcerates (her stomatitis), and hair is lost.\\n\\nLeucovorin (folinic acid) is already PAST the blocked step: it is converted directly to active folate forms without needing dihydrofolate reductase, restoring the folate pool. That is what 'leucovorin rescue' means — you are not reversing methotrexate, you are handing the cell an activated folate it can still use. Giving plain folic acid instead is useless.\\n\\nSupportive measures matter too: hydration and urinary alkalinization promote methotrexate excretion (it is renally cleared, so kidney impairment prolongs exposure), and glucarpidase is available for severe toxicity with renal failure.\\n\\nEducational objective: Methotrexate inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, producing megaloblastic pancytopenia and mucositis. The antidote is leucovorin (folinic acid), which bypasses the blocked enzyme; folic acid is ineffective because it requires that same enzyme for activation.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"Vitamin B12 replacement treats deficiency from pernicious anemia, ileal disease, metformin or proton pump inhibitors. Her folate pathway is blocked by a drug; her B12 supply is not the problem.\", \"1\": \"An erythropoiesis-stimulating agent shouts a build order at a marrow that cannot synthesize DNA. It also takes weeks to work and does nothing for her mucositis or her low platelets and neutrophils.\", \"2\": \"Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor can shorten neutropenia and may be used adjunctively, but it addresses one lineage only and does not restore the blocked folate pathway causing all three cytopenias and the mucositis.\", \"3\": \"This is the trap. Folic acid still requires dihydrofolate reductase to be converted to its active form — and that is precisely the enzyme methotrexate has blocked. You are handing the cell a substrate it cannot activate.\"}, \"eli5\": \"Her arthritis drug blocks the machine that turns the vitamin folate into its usable form. Giving more raw vitamin does nothing, so doctors give the already-usable form instead.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_folate\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The folate pathway — methotrexate blocks dihydrofolate reductase; folinic acid enters beyond that step.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 72, \"t\": \"Identify the most likely and least likely drug treatment(s) for anemia based on a clinical case and describe the mechanisms of action and adverse effects of the drug treatment(s).\", \"a\": \"s16-anemia-drugs\", \"s\": \"§17\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 78-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department from a nursing home due to 1 day of confusion. She has a productive cough that began 3 days ago. Temperature is 35.6 C (96.1 F), blood pressure is 94/52 mm Hg, pulse is 118/min, and respirations are 26/min. She is disoriented to place and time. Crackles are heard at the right lung base. Chest radiography shows a right lower lobe infiltrate. Her leukocyte count is 3,200/mm3 with 14% band forms, platelet count is 88,000/mm3, creatinine is 1.9 mg/dL (baseline 1.0 mg/dL), and serum lactate is 4.6 mmol/L (N=0.5–2.2 mmol/L). Arterial blood gas analysis in this patient would most likely show which of the following?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Metabolic acidosis with respiratory acidosis\", \"B. Metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis\", \"C. Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory acidosis\", \"D. Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory alkalosis\", \"E. Normal acid-base status with hypoxemia\", \"F. Pure respiratory alkalosis\"], \"correct\": 1, \"explanation\": \"Sepsis from community-acquired pneumonia — life-threatening organ dysfunction from a dysregulated host response to infection. Her qSOFA is 3 of 3 (respiratory rate ≥ 22, systolic blood pressure ≤ 100 mm Hg, altered mentation), and organ dysfunction is documented in several systems.\\n\\nThe acid-base pattern is the classic simultaneous pairing:\\n• METABOLIC ACIDOSIS — hypoperfusion → anaerobic metabolism → lactate\\n• RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS — tachypnea, which is both an early direct effect of inflammatory mediators and the compensation for the acidosis\\n\\nTwo traps in the laboratory data are deliberate. Her temperature is LOW and her white count is LOW: the criteria are bidirectional — hypothermia counts as much as fever, and leukopenia (< 4,000/µL) or a normal count with more than 10% immature forms counts as much as leukocytosis (> 12,000/µL). A 'normal-looking' number can still be an abnormal response.\\n\\nThe rest of the panel maps organ by organ: thrombocytopenia (consumption), rising creatinine and oliguria (renal), bilirubin > 4 mg/dL (hepatic), PaO2/FiO2 < 300 (pulmonary), hyperglycemia (stress response). Procalcitonin is the one marker that points at a bacterial cause; cultures are negative in 30%–50% of cases. Management is bundled because the dysfunction is time-dependent: bactericidal antibiotics, intravenous fluids, lactate measurement, perfusion reassessment, vasopressors if needed, plus oxygen and source control.\\n\\nEducational objective: Sepsis classically produces a metabolic acidosis from lactate accumulation together with a respiratory alkalosis from tachypnea. Hypothermia and leukopenia satisfy the systemic inflammatory criteria as fully as fever and leukocytosis.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"This combination appears when a patient can no longer sustain the compensatory hyperventilation — exhaustion, an obstructive lung disease with carbon dioxide retention, or opioid sedation. She is breathing 26 times a minute.\", \"2\": \"This is chronic carbon dioxide retention with renal compensation, or vomiting in a hypoventilating patient. It does not fit a lactate of 4.6.\", \"3\": \"Seen with protracted vomiting in a hyperventilating patient — for example pregnancy or liver failure. Lactic acid production makes an alkalosis impossible here.\", \"4\": \"Hypoxemia alone occurs in an uncomplicated pneumonia, but tissue hypoperfusion with a lactate of 4.6 mmol/L has already moved her beyond that.\", \"5\": \"Early sepsis, anxiety and pulmonary embolism can all give an isolated respiratory alkalosis. Once lactate accumulates from hypoperfusion, a metabolic acidosis is present as well.\"}, \"eli5\": \"Her body is not getting enough oxygen to its tissues, so they make acid. She breathes fast to blow off carbon dioxide, which pushes the blood the other way at the same time.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_sepsis_labs\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The laboratory features of sepsis — most rows measure severity; only procalcitonin points at a cause.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 1, \"t\": \"Briefly explain common screens for sepsis, including SIRS, SOFA, qSOFA, NEWS2 and MEWS, and the laboratory findings associated with sepsis.\", \"a\": \"s17-sepsis\", \"s\": \"§18\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 63-year-old man is admitted to the intensive care unit due to 2 days of fever, myalgia and worsening dyspnea during a winter influenza outbreak. Temperature is 39.1 C (102.4 F), blood pressure is 82/48 mm Hg despite 3 L of intravenous fluid, and oxygen saturation is 88% on high-flow oxygen. Chest radiography shows bilateral infiltrates. Serum lactate is 5.2 mmol/L (N=0.5–2.2 mmol/L) and creatinine is 2.4 mg/dL (N=0.6–1.2 mg/dL). A nasopharyngeal swab is positive for influenza A by polymerase chain reaction. Blood, urine and sputum cultures show no growth at 48 hours, procalcitonin is within normal limits, and serum beta-D-glucan is negative. Which of the following most likely initiated this patient's dysregulated host response?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Beta-glucans within a fungal cell wall\", \"B. Lipopolysaccharide on a bacterial outer membrane\", \"C. Lipoteichoic acid in a bacterial cell wall\", \"D. Molecules released from damaged host cells\", \"E. Superantigen cross-linking of T-cell receptors\"], \"correct\": 3, \"explanation\": \"Viral sepsis — influenza A causing organ dysfunction with sterile cultures and a normal procalcitonin.\\n\\nSepsis is triggered through two families of molecules:\\n• PAMPs — pathogen-associated molecular patterns, carried by the ORGANISM. Gram-negative: lipopolysaccharide (the most prominent trigger overall). Gram-positive: lipoteichoic acid and peptidoglycan. Fungal: beta-glucans, mannans, chitin (all polysaccharides). Also superantigens, bacterial DNA and RNA.\\n• DAMPs — damage-associated molecular patterns, released from the HOST'S OWN damaged tissue. This is how a virus triggers sepsis: virus-damaged cells spill intracellular contents, which set off a cascade of pro-inflammatory cytokines (interleukin-1, interleukin-6).\\n\\nViruses are the exception in the table and therefore the likely question. The damage in sepsis comes from the host response rather than the pathogen directly, which is why no single organism defines sepsis, why cultures are negative in 30%–50% of cases, and why a negative culture never excludes it. Persistent inflammation → endothelial dysfunction and ischemic tissue injury → sequential organ failure → necrosis, necroptosis, pyroptosis and autophagy.\\n\\nEducational objective: Bacteria and fungi trigger sepsis through pathogen-associated molecular patterns (lipopolysaccharide, lipoteichoic acid, beta-glucans), whereas viruses trigger it through damage-associated molecular patterns released from injured host cells. Sterile cultures and a normal procalcitonin do not exclude sepsis.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"Beta-glucans, mannans and chitin are the fungal pathogen-associated molecular patterns, and Candida is the usual organism. His beta-D-glucan is negative.\", \"1\": \"Endotoxin is the most prominent trigger overall — but it belongs to GRAM-NEGATIVE bacteria such as Escherichia coli, Klebsiella and Pseudomonas. Cultures are sterile and procalcitonin, the bacterial marker, is normal.\", \"2\": \"Lipoteichoic acid and peptidoglycan are the GRAM-POSITIVE patterns — Staphylococcus aureus, streptococci, enterococci. Again, the cultures are negative.\", \"4\": \"Toxic shock syndrome toxins and streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin bypass normal antigen presentation to activate T cells en masse. They are bacterial products, and nothing here suggests a staphylococcal or streptococcal source.\"}, \"eli5\": \"The flu virus wrecked his lung cells, and the broken pieces of his own cells are what set the alarm off. His immune system then attacked everything at once, including his own organs.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_sepsis_cascade\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The sepsis cascade — every arm converges on endothelial damage, tissue injury and organ dysfunction.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 75, \"t\": \"List the important microbial triggers of sepsis and explain the spectrum of interactions between triggers and the human host.\", \"a\": \"s17-sepsis\", \"s\": \"§18\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 41-year-old man with HIV infection comes to the office for follow-up. He has taken several antiretroviral regimens over 15 years with poor adherence, and resistance testing shows mutations affecting reverse transcriptase, integrase and protease. A salvage regimen including subcutaneous enfuvirtide is started, and his HIV RNA falls from 180,000 to fewer than 50 copies/mL. Six months later the viral load rises to 24,000 copies/mL, and sequencing shows a new amino acid substitution in the viral fusion protein, which no longer binds the drug. The altered protein is a product of which of the following viral genes?\", \"choices\": [\"A. env\", \"B. gag\", \"C. nef\", \"D. pol\", \"E. tat\", \"F. vpu\"], \"correct\": 0, \"explanation\": \"Enfuvirtide is a FUSION inhibitor that binds gp41, and gp41 is a product of the env gene.\\n\\nEntry is a two-step event: gp120 binds CD4 plus a chemokine co-receptor (CCR5 or CXCR4) → a conformational change exposes gp41 → gp41's fusogenic domain fuses the viral envelope with the cell membrane. Both glycoproteins are cleaved from a single 160 kDa env precursor — and note that env is cleaved by HOST protease, which is exactly why protease inhibitors do not touch it, while the gag and pol precursors are cleaved by VIRAL protease, which is why they do.\\n\\nMap the drugs onto the genes:\\n• env → fostemsavir (attachment, gp120), maraviroc (CCR5 antagonist), enfuvirtide (fusion, gp41); ibalizumab is a post-attachment inhibitor that blocks CD4 on the host side\\n• pol → nucleoside and non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, integrase strand transfer inhibitors, protease inhibitors\\n• gag → p24, the antigen that makes fourth-generation testing detect acute infection\\n\\nResistance follows the same map: a single substitution in gp41 abolishes enfuvirtide binding, while a single point mutation in a conserved reverse transcriptase domain can knock out the entire non-nucleoside class (a low genetic barrier). Protease inhibitors have a high barrier, and the capsid inhibitor lenacapavir is the extreme case — one mutation confers more than 80,000-fold resistance. The upstream cause is always the same: reverse transcriptase's high error rate generates the variant pool selection acts on.\\n\\nEducational objective: The env gene encodes gp120 and gp41, the surface proteins targeted by attachment and fusion inhibitors; pol encodes reverse transcriptase, integrase and protease; and gag encodes p24, the capsid antigen used in fourth-generation testing.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"1\": \"The gag precursor is cleaved by VIRAL protease into p24 (the capsid antigen detected in fourth-generation testing), p17 (matrix) and the nucleocapsid proteins. None of them is on the virion surface fusing with the host membrane.\", \"2\": \"An accessory gene whose product protects infected cells from killing by T lymphocytes. It is of interest for drug development but is not a structural gene.\", \"3\": \"The pol gene encodes reverse transcriptase, integrase and protease — the targets of the three classes he has ALREADY failed. Enfuvirtide acts before any of them.\", \"4\": \"A regulatory gene: the transactivator of transcription, which also inhibits T-cell proliferation. It has no surface product.\", \"5\": \"An accessory gene that enhances viral release and downregulates surface CD4 expression. Not a structural surface protein.\"}, \"eli5\": \"The virus uses a grappling hook and a harpoon on its surface to get into cells. This drug jams the harpoon, so the virus changed the harpoon's shape — and the instructions for that part come from the env gene.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_hiv_cycle_drugs\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The HIV replication cycle with each drug class mapped to the step it blocks.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 24, \"t\": \"Discuss the principal HIV structural genes and the function of their products.\", \"a\": \"s18-hiv-micro\", \"s\": \"§19\"}, {\"n\": 14, \"t\": \"Describe the prominent antiretroviral drug classes (attachment/fusion inhibitors, post-attachment entry inhibitors, RT inhibitors [NRTI; NNRTI], capsid inhibitors, integrase strand transfer inhibitors, protease inhibitors, pharmacokinetic enhancers) in terms of their mechanisms of action as well as the primary mechanisms of HIV resistance against each.\", \"a\": \"s18-hiv-micro\", \"s\": \"§19\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 34-year-old woman with newly diagnosed HIV infection comes to the office due to 2 weeks of progressive right-sided weakness and difficulty finding words. At diagnosis 6 weeks ago her CD4 count was 38/mm3 and her HIV RNA was 420,000 copies/mL, and antiretroviral therapy was started the same week; she had no opportunistic infection at that time and takes no prophylaxis other than trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. Today her CD4 count is 165/mm3 and her HIV RNA is 780 copies/mL. Magnetic resonance imaging shows multifocal, nonenhancing white matter lesions with several new areas of faint peripheral enhancement and surrounding edema. A brain biopsy specimen is shown. Which of the following best explains this patient's current illness?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Direct neurotoxicity of antiretroviral drugs\", \"B. Emergence of antiretroviral drug resistance\", \"C. Paradoxical immune reconstitution syndrome\", \"D. Progressive decline in immune function\", \"E. Unmasking immune reconstitution syndrome\"], \"correct\": 4, \"explanation\": \"Unmasking immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS), revealing progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy caused by JC virus (a polyomavirus).\\n\\nIRIS is an exaggerated immune response to pathogens that occurs as antiretroviral therapy restores immune function. It takes two forms:\\n• PARADOXICAL — the opportunistic infection is already known and being treated, and symptoms worsen after antiretroviral therapy starts\\n• UNMASKING — no opportunistic infection was diagnosed; the recovering immune system generates inflammation that reveals a subclinical one. That is this patient\\n\\nThe supporting pattern: advanced disease with a very low pre-treatment CD4 count and a high viral load, a robust virologic and immunologic response, and symptom onset within the first months of therapy (median 48 days, here 6 weeks). IRIS occurs in 10%–20% of patients, and the specific regimen chosen does not change the risk.\\n\\nThe biopsy shows the JC virus lesion: demyelination with vacuolated white matter, enlarged oligodendroglial nuclei containing viral inclusions, and reactive astrocytes. Untreated PML has no specific antiviral — immune reconstitution IS the treatment, which is the paradox.\\n\\nThe answer is not to delay antiretroviral therapy: delaying costs more than it saves. Anticipate IRIS, counsel the patient that worsening can occur, and keep it on the differential when a patient deteriorates while the numbers improve.\\n\\nEducational objective: Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome occurs within the first months of antiretroviral therapy in patients with a low pre-treatment CD4 count and a brisk virologic response; the unmasking form reveals a previously undiagnosed opportunistic infection. Improving CD4 count and falling viral load distinguish it from treatment failure.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"Some agents cause neuropsychiatric effects — vivid dreams and mood change with efavirenz, for example — but they do not produce focal deficits with demyelinating white matter lesions and viral inclusions on biopsy.\", \"1\": \"Resistance announces itself as a RISING viral load. Hers has fallen from 420,000 to 780 copies/mL, which is treatment success.\", \"2\": \"Paradoxical IRIS requires an opportunistic infection that was ALREADY diagnosed and being treated when antiretroviral therapy began, which then worsens. She had no known opportunistic infection.\", \"3\": \"An ordinary opportunistic infection occurs as CD4 falls, late in the untreated course. Her CD4 has more than quadrupled.\"}, \"eli5\": \"Her immune system was too weak to notice a slow brain virus. When the HIV medicines let it recover, it charged in and the swelling from that fight is what made her sick.\", \"image\": \"fig_hx_pml_histo\", \"imageCaption\": \"Brain biopsy, hematoxylin and eosin. The white matter is vacuolated and loosely textured, with scattered small lymphocytes and reactive astrocytes. Several glial cells have markedly enlarged, densely staining nuclei filled by a homogeneous inclusion (arrowhead).\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_cd4_oi\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Opportunistic organisms by CD4 threshold, with the clinical clue for each.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 77, \"t\": \"Understand age-related changes which can occur and what should be monitored based upon reported age-related changes.\", \"a\": \"s19-hiv-clinical\", \"s\": \"§20\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 29-year-old man is brought to the emergency department due to 4 days of fever with rigors and sweats occurring at no discernible interval, followed today by confusion and a generalized seizure. He returned 2 weeks ago from a 3-month stay in Nigeria and took no chemoprophylaxis. Temperature is 39.4 C (102.9 F). He is jaundiced. Hemoglobin is 7.6 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL), platelet count is 42,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3), and creatinine is 2.8 mg/dL (N=0.6–1.2 mg/dL). He dies 12 hours after admission. A section of cerebral cortex is shown; the capillary contains erythrocytes packed with organisms and dark brown-black granular pigment. That pigment is produced by which of the following processes?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Aggregation of residual ribosomal RNA\", \"B. Deposition of iron within mitochondria\", \"C. Oxidative denaturation of hemoglobin\", \"D. Polymerization of heme into hemozoin\", \"E. Precipitation of unpaired globin chains\", \"F. Retention of nuclear DNA remnants\"], \"correct\": 3, \"explanation\": \"Severe falciparum malaria with cerebral involvement — sub-Saharan travel with no prophylaxis, IRREGULAR fever (falciparum's erythrocytic cycles are not synchronized, unlike the 48-hour cycle of vivax and ovale or the 72-hour cycle of malariae), hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, acute kidney injury and encephalopathy.\\n\\nWhere the pigment comes from — and why it is also the drug target:\\n• The parasite digests hemoglobin inside the red cell and uses globin as an amino acid source\\n• That liberates free HEME, which is toxic to the parasite itself\\n• HEME POLYMERASE converts toxic heme into inert, crystalline HEMOZOIN — the brown-black pigment seen here\\n• Chloroquine, quinine and mefloquine BLOCK that conversion, so heme accumulates and the parasite poisons itself with its own waste\\n\\nThe neurologic disease is mechanical as well as inflammatory: parasitized erythrocytes adhere in and obstruct cerebral capillaries (exactly what this section shows), producing infarcts, capillary leak and neuronal injury. Patients can die within 24 hours.\\n\\nDiagnosis: a THICK smear is the screen (red cells pre-lysed, so it answers 'is it there?') and a THIN smear identifies the species and quantifies parasitemia; rapid antigen tests give an answer in about 10 minutes. Nigeria is chloroquine-resistant territory, so treatment is artemisinin-based combination therapy.\\n\\nEducational objective: Plasmodium digests hemoglobin and detoxifies the liberated heme by polymerizing it into hemozoin pigment; chloroquine and quinine block that step so heme accumulates and kills the parasite. Falciparum malaria causes irregular fever and can sequester in cerebral capillaries, producing cerebral malaria.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"Aggregated ribosomes produce the coarse basophilic stippling of lead poisoning and thalassemia. That inclusion is blue and diffuse, not a brown-black pigment inside a parasite.\", \"1\": \"This is the ringed sideroblast of sideroblastic anemia, demonstrated with a Prussian blue stain of the marrow — iron the cell cannot insert into protoporphyrin.\", \"2\": \"Oxidized, precipitated hemoglobin forms Heinz bodies in G6PD deficiency, visible only with a supravital stain and bitten out by splenic macrophages to leave bite cells.\", \"4\": \"Unpartnered chains aggregate in thalassemia, killing precursors in the marrow and shortening red cell survival — a disorder of globin quantity, not a parasite product.\", \"5\": \"Howell-Jolly bodies are retained nuclear fragments that a working spleen would remove; they signal asplenia or functional hyposplenism.\"}, \"eli5\": \"The parasite eats the red stuff inside blood cells, and the leftovers are poisonous to it, so it packs them into harmless dark crystals. Some malaria drugs stop it doing that, so it is killed by its own garbage.\", \"image\": \"fig_hx_cerebral_malaria\", \"imageCaption\": \"Section of cerebral cortex, hematoxylin and eosin. A small capillary running through the vacuolated neuropil (arrowhead) is distended and packed with erythrocytes containing coarse dark brown-black granular pigment. Adjacent tissue shows small hemorrhages.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_chloroquine\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"How chloroquine kills the parasite: it blocks the detoxification of heme into hemozoin.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 5, \"t\": \"Compare and contrast the parasitic agents of babesiosis and malaria in terms of geographic distribution, vectors, pathogenesis, clinical manifestations, diagnostic approach and treatment (including malaria vaccine).\", \"a\": \"s19b-vector-infections\", \"s\": \"§21\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 38-year-old man with AIDS comes to the office due to 6 weeks of enlarging red skin lesions. He stopped antiretroviral therapy 2 years ago. His CD4 count is 44/mm3. He has several cats at home, one of them a kitten. Examination shows numerous friable, cherry-red papules and nodules on the face and trunk, some with a collarette of scale, and a violaceous plaque on the forearm. His lesions are shown. Bartonella serology is negative. Biopsy of a papule shows a lobular proliferation of small capillaries lined by plump endothelial cells, with neutrophils and clumps of granular purple material that stain black on Warthin-Starry silver stain. In addition to restarting antiretroviral therapy, which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Doxycycline\", \"B. Itraconazole\", \"C. Liposomal doxorubicin\", \"D. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole\", \"E. Valganciclovir\"], \"correct\": 0, \"explanation\": \"Bacillary angiomatosis caused by Bartonella henselae — the SAME organism as cat scratch disease, producing a completely different disease because the host is different.\\n\\nOne fork organizes the topic: immunoCOMPETENT host (typically a child under 10) → cat scratch disease, a lymphocutaneous illness with an inoculation papule and tender regional lymphadenopathy, self-limiting over 5–6 weeks, treated with azithromycin. ImmunoCOMPROMISED host (AIDS, chemotherapy, transplant) → bacillary angiomatosis, a proliferation of small vessels in skin and viscera, progressive and fatal if untreated, treated with erythromycin and/or doxycycline plus restoration of immune function.\\n\\nTwo exam points are built into this vignette:\\n• The negative serology means nothing. Serology detects the HOST's antibody response, and this host cannot mount one — the test underperforms in exactly the population that gets the disease. Diagnose by lesion biopsy with histopathology and PCR.\\n• The look-alike is Kaposi sarcoma. Both occur in AIDS and look similar, but one is a curable bacterial infection and the other an HHV-8-driven vascular malignancy. You cannot tell them apart by looking, which is why the biopsy is mandatory.\\n\\nThe Warthin-Starry stain identifies argyrophilic ('silver-loving') organisms: Bartonella, spirochetes, Legionella and Helicobacter pylori. The clinical context picks the winner. Note also that the infectious material in a cat scratch is FLEA FECES on the fur and claws — the claw is the needle, not the source.\\n\\nEducational objective: Bartonella henselae causes cat scratch disease in immunocompetent hosts and bacillary angiomatosis in immunocompromised ones; the latter is diagnosed by biopsy with a Warthin-Starry stain (serology is unreliable) and treated with erythromycin or doxycycline plus immune restoration. Kaposi sarcoma is the clinical mimic.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"1\": \"An antifungal for histoplasmosis and other endemic mycoses, which can also cause skin lesions in advanced HIV — but fungal elements, not silver-staining bacilli, would be seen on biopsy.\", \"2\": \"This treats Kaposi sarcoma, the critical look-alike: also violaceous lesions in AIDS, but driven by human herpesvirus 8, and showing spindle cells with slit-like vascular spaces and hemosiderin rather than bacilli.\", \"3\": \"The treatment and prophylaxis for Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia and toxoplasmosis, which he should already be receiving at this CD4 count. It does not treat this infection.\", \"4\": \"Used for cytomegalovirus disease — retinitis, esophagitis, colitis — at CD4 counts below 50. Cytomegalovirus does not produce vascular skin nodules.\"}, \"eli5\": \"A germ from a cat's fleas is making tiny blood vessels grow into red bumps on his skin because his immune system cannot stop it. The right antibiotic clears it, but only if his immune system is also rebuilt.\", \"image\": \"fig_bacillary_angiomatosis\", \"imageCaption\": \"Face of a patient with advanced HIV infection. Numerous smooth, dome-shaped, deep red papules and nodules of varying size are scattered over the cheek, eyelid and nose; several are eroded and crusted.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_hx_kaposi_histo\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The look-alike under the microscope: Kaposi sarcoma, with fascicles of spindle cells, slit-like vascular spaces containing red cells, and hemosiderin.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 22, \"t\": \"Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical manifestations, diagnostic methods and treatment of blood and lymph disorders caused by Bartonella henselae, specifically cat scratch disease and bacillary angiomatosis.\", \"a\": \"s19b-vector-infections\", \"s\": \"§21\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 32-year-old man comes to the emergency department due to 1 day of fever, chills and profound weakness, with a painful lump in the right groin. He has been camping in rural New Mexico and handled several dead prairie dogs near his campsite 4 days ago. Temperature is 39.7 C (103.5 F), blood pressure is 96/56 mm Hg, and pulse is 124/min. Examination shows an exquisitely tender, boggy 5 cm mass in the right inguinal region with overlying erythema, and a small crusted papule on the right calf. There is no pharyngitis and no genital ulcer. Aspiration of the mass is performed. Which of the following is most likely to be seen on stained smears of the aspirate?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Acid-fast beaded bacilli\", \"B. Gram-negative rods with bipolar staining\", \"C. Gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci\", \"D. Intraerythrocytic ring forms\", \"E. Pleomorphic coccobacilli on silver stain\"], \"correct\": 1, \"explanation\": \"Bubonic plague — Yersinia pestis. Every element of the stem is a deliberate pointer: the western United States (endemic in at least 17 states, chiefly Pacific Coast and western states — New Mexico, Colorado, California, Texas, Arizona, Oregon, Nevada), a named epizootic host (squirrels, prairie dogs, chipmunks), abrupt high fever with weakness, and a BUBO in the node draining a flea bite on the leg.\\n\\nThe organism is a gram-negative rod with BIPOLAR 'safety pin' staining on Gram or Giemsa — dark at both ends, pale in the middle. A rapid antigen test is 100% sensitive and 100% specific; culture on blood agar gives sensitivities, and PCR confirms. Treat with gentamicin or a fluoroquinolone. Untreated bubonic plague kills about half of those infected; treated mortality is 1%–15%.\\n\\nWhy a flea transmits it at all: Y. pestis multiplies in the flea's proventriculus until a hard mass BLOCKS it, so the flea can bite but cannot swallow. Starving, it bites frantically and indiscriminately, regurgitating bacteria into each wound — which is how humans, a DEAD-END host that offers the organism nothing, are bitten at all.\\n\\nThe complication to watch is progression to secondary plague pneumonia, after which the patient transmits by aerosol and can seed primary plague pneumonia in contacts — 100% fatal untreated. And a plague isolate with marked antibiotic resistance should raise the possibility of deliberate engineering, since naturally occurring plague is almost uniformly susceptible.\\n\\nEducational objective: Yersinia pestis causes bubonic plague after a rodent-flea exposure in the western United States and appears as a bipolar-staining ('safety pin') gram-negative rod on node aspirate. Treat with gentamicin or a fluoroquinolone, and watch for secondary plague pneumonia, which spreads by aerosol.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"Mycobacterial lymphadenitis (scrofula) produces a chronic, often nontender node over weeks to months, not a 1-day febrile illness with hypotension.\", \"2\": \"Streptococcus pneumoniae causes fulminant sepsis in asplenic patients and pneumonia in the community, but it does not produce a bubo after rodent exposure.\", \"3\": \"Rings inside red cells are Babesia or Plasmodium — and they are sought on a Giemsa smear of BLOOD, not a node aspirate. Babesia would also mean a New England tick, not a western rodent.\", \"4\": \"Bartonella henselae is argyrophilic and causes tender regional lymphadenopathy after a cat scratch — but the illness is indolent and self-limiting rather than an abrupt septic presentation with rodent exposure.\"}, \"eli5\": \"A flea that fed on infected prairie dogs bit him, and the germs travelled to the nearest lymph node and made it swell into a painful lump. Under the microscope those germs look like tiny safety pins.\", \"image\": \"fig_bubo\", \"imageCaption\": \"Photograph of the groin. A large, smooth, dome-shaped swelling distorts the inguinal region beneath intact skin.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_flea_proventriculus\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The blocked flea proventriculus — a starving flea bites repeatedly and regurgitates bacteria into each wound.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 23, \"t\": \"Discuss the epidemiology, transmission and pathophysiology of plague. Describe the clinical forms of plague and their diagnosis and treatment (including vaccine).\", \"a\": \"s19b-vector-infections\", \"s\": \"§21\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 38-year-old man comes to the emergency department due to 5 days of fever, severe headache and vomiting, followed by decreasing urine output. Five weeks ago he returned from rural South Korea, where he spent a month renovating a long-disused grain barn heavily soiled with rodent droppings. He recalls no insect or tick bites and no animal bites. Temperature is 38.9 C (102.0 F) and blood pressure is 88/52 mm Hg. There are petechiae over the trunk and conjunctival hemorrhages. Platelet count is 38,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3), creatinine is 5.1 mg/dL (N=0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and urinalysis shows marked proteinuria. Renal biopsy shows interstitial hemorrhage with a dense mononuclear infiltrate and tubular injury, most severe in the medulla. Which of the following was the most likely route of transmission?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Bite of a female Anopheles mosquito\", \"B. Bite of a Hyalomma tick\", \"C. Bite of an infected rat flea\", \"D. Bite of an Ixodes scapularis nymph\", \"E. Contact with infected fruit bat fluids\", \"F. Inhalation of aerosolized rodent excreta\"], \"correct\": 5, \"explanation\": \"Hantavirus hemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome (HFRS), most likely Hantaan or Seoul virus given Korea.\\n\\nTransmission: the virus is shed in rodent urine, feces and saliva and is acquired by AEROSOL, classically while generating dust in a rodent-infested building. There is NO vector — hantavirus and Lassa are the two agents in this block with no arthropod at all, which is why the stem pointedly denies bites.\\n\\nThe incubation period is the other clue: 3 to 9 WEEKS, far longer than dengue's 4–7 days. An exposure more than a month ago is still within the window and must not be dismissed as too remote.\\n\\nWhy the kidney fails out of proportion: this is an IMMUNOPATHOLOGIC lesion. Infected dendritic cells and macrophages release a cytokine storm; cytotoxic T cells are recruited; infected endothelial cells upregulate HLA and adhesion molecules, marking themselves for antibody-dependent cytotoxicity and complement, and secrete vascular endothelial growth factor, which increases permeability; and the kallikrein-kinin system raises bradykinin. The result is an acute tubulointerstitial nephritis with interstitial hemorrhage — and acute kidney injury is the usual cause of death. Every other viral hemorrhagic fever here fails as a set of organs.\\n\\nOld World hantaviruses → kidneys (HFRS). New World hantaviruses → heart and lungs (the cardiopulmonary syndrome). There is no antiviral and no vaccine available in the United States; care is supportive with volume and electrolyte management and renal replacement therapy.\\n\\nEducational objective: Old World hantaviruses are acquired by inhaling aerosolized rodent urine, feces or saliva with no arthropod vector, have a 3–9 week incubation, and cause hemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome in which immunopathologic acute kidney injury is the usual cause of death.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"This transmits malaria, whose reservoir is humans themselves, in the tropics and subtropics — with cyclic fever and ring forms inside red cells rather than renal failure with hemorrhage.\", \"1\": \"This is Crimean-Congo hemorrhagic fever, acquired from livestock and birds that stay well, in Africa, the Middle East, Asia and southeastern Europe — not the Far East, and not with kidney failure as the dominant organ injury.\", \"2\": \"The rat flea transmits Yersinia pestis, which produces a bubo and acral gangrene, and is a disease of the western United States rather than the Far East.\", \"3\": \"This vector transmits babesiosis, Lyme disease and anaplasmosis in the northeastern United States. Babesia causes hemolysis and thrombocytopenia, not an interstitial nephritis.\", \"4\": \"This is how filoviruses (Ebola and Marburg) are acquired, in Central and West Africa, with generalized multi-organ failure rather than a kidney-dominant illness.\"}, \"eli5\": \"He breathed in dried mouse droppings while cleaning an old barn, and the virus in them attacked the lining of his blood vessels. His kidneys took the worst of it, which is what makes this particular virus stand out.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_hanta_kidney\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The hantavirus renal lesion — cytokines, cytotoxic T cells and complement acting on an infected endothelium.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 13, \"t\": \"Describe the principal agents of viral hemorrhagic fever, including Dengue virus, Hantavirus, Crimean-Congo virus, Lassa virus, Ebolavirus and Marburgvirus, comparing their epidemiology, pathophysiology, vectors (where appropriate), clinical manifestations and treatment.\", \"a\": \"s19c-vhf\", \"s\": \"§22\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 72-year-old woman comes to the office due to 3 weeks of fatigue and a dusky blue discoloration of her fingertips that appears when she goes outdoors and resolves indoors. She had an atypical pneumonia treated with azithromycin 6 weeks ago. She takes no other medications. The sample is drawn in the office and sent to the laboratory.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Erythrocyte count  2.1 million/mm3 (N=3.5–5.5 million/mm3)\\n  Hemoglobin  11.6 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  26% (N=36%–46%)\\n  Mean corpuscular volume  124 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\\n  Mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration  44.6% (N=31%–36%)\\n  Platelet count  244,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\\nThe analyzer flags the specimen. A smear made from the same tube is shown. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Begin vitamin B12 replacement\", \"B. Correct the hemoglobin with a plasma blank\", \"C. Perform a bone marrow biopsy\", \"D. Redraw the specimen in a citrate tube\", \"E. Transfuse one unit of red blood cells\", \"F. Warm the specimen to 37 C and re-run\"], \"correct\": 5, \"explanation\": \"Cold agglutinins causing red cell agglutination in the tube — here following Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection, with acrocyanosis on cold exposure as the clinical counterpart.\\n\\nThe giveaway is an MCHC of 44.6%. MCHC is a CONCENTRATION, so it is bounded by physics: a red cell can only hold so much hemoglobin per unit volume, and the normal range tops out near 36%–37%. A value well above that is almost never biological.\\n\\nTrace the error through the arithmetic, remembering that only three values are MEASURED (red cell count and MCV by impedance, hemoglobin photometrically) and the rest are calculated:\\n• Clumped cells cross the aperture together → counted as ONE very large cell → red cell count falls, MCV rises\\n• Hematocrit = (RBC × MCV)/10 → the count fell further than the volume rose → hematocrit is falsely LOW (note it is also only 2.2 times the hemoglobin, breaking the rule of three)\\n• MCHC = (Hgb/Hct) × 100 → an impossibly HIGH value\\n• The hemoglobin itself is measured on lysed cells and remains accurate\\n\\nThe fix is to warm the sample to 37 C and re-run, which disperses the agglutinates. Clinically, follow with a cold agglutinin titre and a direct antiglobulin test, which is positive for complement C3 in cold agglutinin disease.\\n\\nThe one setting where a genuinely high MCHC is a disease and not an artefact is hereditary spherocytosis, where the cells really are hyperdense.\\n\\nEducational objective: An MCHC above about 37% is nearly always a specimen artefact — either falsely raised hemoglobin (lipemia, marked leukocytosis, hyperbilirubinemia) or a falsely low calculated hematocrit from cold agglutinin-induced red cell clumping, which is resolved by warming the sample to 37 C and re-running it.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"The macrocytosis here is an artefact of clumped cells being sized as one large cell. True megaloblastic anemia gives hypersegmented neutrophils and a raised methylmalonic acid, and would not produce an impossible MCHC.\", \"1\": \"Blank correction is the fix when LIPEMIA, marked leukocytosis or hyperbilirubinemia add absorbance and falsely raise the measured hemoglobin. Here the hemoglobin is measured on lysed cells and is accurate — the hematocrit is the corrupted value.\", \"2\": \"A marrow biopsy investigates unexplained pancytopenia or suspected primary marrow disease. Acting on an artefactual result with an invasive test is the specific harm this flag exists to prevent.\", \"3\": \"Citrate is the workaround for EDTA-dependent PLATELET clumping, which falsely lowers the platelet count. Her platelet count is normal and the abnormality is in the red cell indices.\", \"4\": \"Her true hemoglobin is 11.6 g/dL, which is measured correctly and nowhere near a transfusion threshold.\"}, \"eli5\": \"Her blood cells stuck together into clumps in the cold tube, and the machine counted each clump as one giant cell. Warming the tube unsticks them so the numbers come out right.\", \"image\": \"fig_agglutination\", \"imageCaption\": \"Peripheral blood smear. Erythrocytes are gathered into irregular three-dimensional clumps with clear spaces between them, rather than lying separately; a neutrophil is present at left.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_coulter\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The Coulter principle — each cell crossing the aperture makes one pulse, and pulse height is read as that cell's volume.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 27, \"t\": \"Given a clinical vignette and laboratory and/or diagnostic values, identify the key components of the CBC, methodology and troubleshooting of interferences.\", \"a\": \"s22-cbc-interference\", \"s\": \"§25\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 47-year-old woman is hospitalized on day 12 after induction chemotherapy for acute myeloid leukemia due to fever. She reports 2 days of deep perianal pain. Temperature is 38.9 C (102.0 F). Examination shows tenderness lateral to the anus with minimal erythema and no fluctuance, induration or drainage. Chest examination is normal and chest radiography shows no infiltrate, although she reports a cough.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  8.4 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  25% (N=36%–46%)\\n  Leukocyte count  600/mm3 (N=4,500–11,000/mm3)\\n  Segmented neutrophils  12% (N=54%–62%)\\n  Platelet count  24,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\\nWhich of the following best explains the paucity of local findings in this patient?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Absent antibody production by B lymphocytes\", \"B. Defective oxidative burst within phagocytes\", \"C. Loss of T-cell help for macrophage activation in tissue\", \"D. Margination of neutrophils along vessel walls\", \"E. Too few neutrophils to form an inflammatory exudate\"], \"correct\": 4, \"explanation\": \"Febrile neutropenia after induction chemotherapy, with a perianal infection.\\n\\nCompute the number, because a percentage always misleads: ANC = white cell count × % neutrophils = 600 × 0.12 = 72/µL.\\n\\nThe three thresholds:\\n• < 1,000/µL — sharply increased risk of infection\\n• < 500/µL — control of endogenous skin and gut flora is impaired; this is absolute neutropenia\\n• < 200/µL — NO inflammatory process can be mounted in response to immune challenge\\n\\nShe is at 72/µL, and the classic signs of infection ARE the inflammatory response. Pus is dead neutrophils. Consolidation on a chest film is largely neutrophilic exudate. Erythema, swelling, induration and fluctuance all depend on neutrophil recruitment. With too few cells to produce any of it, a serious infection — here a perianal infection that could be a deep abscess — can be present with almost no localizing signs, and the chest film can stay clear in a patient with pneumonia.\\n\\nHence fever alone is the emergency: obtain cultures and start empiric broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics immediately rather than waiting for a source to declare itself, because the progression is fever and chills → sepsis → multi-organ failure. Note also that bacteriostatic agents rely on a functioning immune system to clear the organisms already present, which is why bactericidal therapy is preferred here.\\n\\nEducational objective: The absolute neutrophil count is the white cell count times the neutrophil percentage; below 500/µL endogenous flora become pathogens, and below 200/µL no inflammatory response can be generated, so infections present with fever and few localizing signs. Febrile neutropenia requires immediate empiric broad-spectrum antibiotics.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"An antibody deficiency such as Bruton agammaglobulinemia causes recurrent pyogenic infections — but those patients still form pus, because their neutrophils are present and functional.\", \"1\": \"In chronic granulomatous disease the neutrophil COUNT is normal and the cells are recruited normally; they simply cannot kill catalase-positive organisms, so patients form abscesses and granulomas — the opposite of no inflammation.\", \"2\": \"This is the defect in advanced HIV infection and DiGeorge syndrome, producing viral, fungal and opportunistic infections. It does not abolish the neutrophilic exudate.\", \"3\": \"Excessive peripheral pooling makes the measured count understate the total neutrophil pool — but those cells are still available to enter tissue, and this patient's marrow has been ablated by chemotherapy.\"}, \"eli5\": \"The redness, swelling and pus you normally see with an infection are made OF white blood cells, and chemotherapy has wiped hers out. So a dangerous infection can be there with almost nothing to see — a fever is the only warning.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_five_wbc\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The five normal circulating leukocytes — the neutrophil is the cell that builds the inflammatory exudate.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 76, \"t\": \"Recognize normal leukocytes present in peripheral blood and identify the specific cell levels and characteristics.\", \"a\": \"s23-wbc-normal\", \"s\": \"§26\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 70-year-old man is admitted to the hospital due to 3 days of fever and left lower quadrant pain. Computed tomography shows perforated sigmoid diverticulitis with a 7 cm pelvic abscess, which is drained percutaneously. He has no lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, night sweats or weight loss.\\n\\nLaboratory studies show:\\n  Hemoglobin  13.1 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\\n  Hematocrit  39% (N=41%–53%)\\n  Leukocyte count  58,000/mm3 (N=4,500–11,000/mm3)\\n  Platelet count  388,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\\n  Basophils  0% (N=0%–0.75%)\\nThe peripheral smear shows abundant neutrophils with coarse dark cytoplasmic granules, pale blue cytoplasmic inclusions and vacuoles, along with occasional myelocytes and metamyelocytes; blasts account for less than 1% of cells. Which of the following is most likely to occur in this patient?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Appearance of myeloblasts containing Auer rods\", \"B. Detection of a BCR-ABL1 fusion transcript\", \"C. Development of a leukoerythroblastic smear\", \"D. Identification of a monoclonal population by flow cytometry\", \"E. Persistent absolute basophilia on repeat counts\", \"F. Return of the leukocyte count to normal after drainage\"], \"correct\": 5, \"explanation\": \"A leukemoid reaction — severe leukocytosis with immature cells in the blood that mimics a neoplastic proliferation. The discrimination decides whether this patient gets antibiotics or a bone marrow biopsy.\\n\\nEverything here favours REACTIVE:\\n• Toxic granulation, Döhle bodies (the pale blue inclusions) and vacuolization — the three changes of a neutrophil working hard, never of a malignant clone\\n• The blast pyramid is the right way up: a few blasts, more myelocytes and metamyelocytes, and mostly mature cells. Inverting that pyramid is the concerning pattern\\n• Other cell lines are preserved — a marrow crowded out by a clone cannot keep making red cells and platelets\\n• No basophilia, no B symptoms, and a named precipitant that has been treated\\n\\nThe six morphologic criteria, reactive versus malignant: nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio (decreased vs increased), cytoplasm (abundant, moulding around neighbouring red cells vs scant), population uniformity (heterogeneous and polyclonal vs monoclonal), chromatin (coarse and clumped vs open and powdery), nucleoli (occasional and small vs prominent) and other cell lines (normal vs decreased). A nucleolus alone is not abnormal — any hard-working cell has one; many or very large nucleoli are the problem, and Auer rods are unambiguous.\\n\\nDuration settles most cases: reactive morphology resolves as the source is controlled, whereas a leukemia persists after the patient recovers. When in doubt, have a low threshold for flow cytometry, which demonstrates clonality directly.\\n\\nEducational objective: A leukemoid reaction is a severe reactive leukocytosis with toxic granulation, Döhle bodies and a left shift that preserves the normal maturation pyramid and other cell lines, and it resolves once the underlying stimulus is treated. Basophilia, an excess of blasts, Auer rods, cytopenias or a monoclonal population point to malignancy.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"Needle-shaped granule inclusions mark a malignant myeloid cell and would indicate acute myeloid leukemia. His blasts are under 1% and his other cell lines are intact.\", \"1\": \"This defines chronic myeloid leukemia, which also produces a very high white count with circulating immature cells — but characteristically with ABSOLUTE BASOPHILIA, which he does not have, and it does not resolve when an abscess is drained.\", \"2\": \"Immature white cells plus nucleated red cells plus tear-drop cells together indicate disturbed marrow architecture — myelofibrosis or marrow infiltration by tumour. His red cell morphology is unremarkable.\", \"3\": \"Flow cytometry demonstrates clonality directly and is the test to send when you are unsure. Here the response is polyclonal and reactive: heterogeneous cells with toxic changes, and normal other lineages.\", \"4\": \"Unexplained persistent basophilia is one of the few findings that points directly at a myeloproliferative neoplasm. His basophil count is zero.\"}, \"eli5\": \"His body is pumping out huge numbers of infection-fighting cells because of the abscess, which can look like leukemia on a count. Once the abscess is drained, the number comes back down — leukemia would not.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_toxic_granulation\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"Toxic granulation — coarse dark granules in a neutrophil responding hard to a stimulus, not a malignant change.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 66, \"t\": \"Identify clinical, laboratory and diagnostic features that distinguish reactive versus malignant lymphadenopathy.\", \"a\": \"s24-reactive\", \"s\": \"§27\"}]}, {\"stem\": \"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the office due to 3 weeks of fever and cough. He has had two episodes of cervical lymphadenitis requiring surgical drainage, both growing Staphylococcus aureus, and a liver abscess at age 2 that grew Serratia marcescens. He is at the 10th percentile for weight. Chest computed tomography shows a cavitating right upper lobe consolidation, and lung biopsy grows Aspergillus fumigatus; histologic sections show granulomas with multinucleated giant cells. Leukocyte count is 9,400/mm3 with 58% segmented neutrophils, and serum IgG, IgA and IgM concentrations are normal. The organisms that have infected this patient share which of the following properties?\", \"choices\": [\"A. Production of a polysaccharide capsule\", \"B. Production of catalase\", \"C. Production of coagulase\", \"D. Production of IgA protease\", \"E. Survival within resting macrophages\"], \"correct\": 1, \"explanation\": \"Chronic granulomatous disease — a QUALITATIVE phagocyte defect. Note the normal neutrophil count and normal immunoglobulins: the cells are present in normal numbers and simply do not work.\\n\\nThe respiratory burst, and where it breaks:\\n• O2 → superoxide, by NADPH OXIDASE — the step that fails (mutations in cytochrome b558; 70% X-linked recessive, and 86% of patients are male)\\n• Superoxide → hydrogen peroxide, by superoxide dismutase\\n• Hydrogen peroxide → hypochlorous acid, by myeloperoxidase — the neutrophil is making bleach inside a vacuole\\n\\nThe catalase logic: phagocytosis is intact, so organisms are ingested normally. A phagocyte with no NADPH oxidase makes no hydrogen peroxide of its own — but many bacteria generate hydrogen peroxide as a metabolic by-product, and the defective phagocyte simply borrows theirs. CATALASE-POSITIVE organisms destroy their own hydrogen peroxide, leaving nothing to borrow, so they survive inside the cell. The offenders to name: Staphylococcus aureus, Aspergillus, Serratia, Nocardia and Burkholderia. Catalase-negative organisms are still killed.\\n\\nThe standoff that follows is the granuloma: a necrotic centre with living organisms, walled off by macrophages and multinucleated giant cells, with T and B cells outside holding the line.\\n\\nDiagnosis: the nitroblue tetrazolium test (normal neutrophils reduce a yellow dye to a blue-black precipitate; these cannot) or the modern neutrophil oxidative index by flow cytometry. Management: prophylactic antibiotics and antifungals, recombinant interferon gamma (reduces infection rate by up to 70%), and corticosteroids when granulomas obstruct a viscus.\\n\\nEducational objective: Chronic granulomatous disease is a defect of NADPH oxidase that leaves phagocytes unable to generate reactive oxygen species, so catalase-positive organisms — Staphylococcus aureus, Serratia, Nocardia, Burkholderia and Aspergillus — survive ingestion and produce recurrent abscesses and granulomas. The neutrophil count is normal.\", \"wrongExplanations\": {\"0\": \"Encapsulated organisms — Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis, Haemophilus influenzae type b — overwhelm patients without a functioning SPLEEN or without antibody. Serratia and Aspergillus are not in that group.\", \"2\": \"Coagulase distinguishes Staphylococcus aureus from other staphylococci. It says nothing about Serratia or Aspergillus, so it cannot be the shared property.\", \"3\": \"IgA protease helps organisms colonize mucosal surfaces and is made by pneumococcus, meningococcus and Haemophilus — a different group entirely.\", \"4\": \"Intracellular survival characterizes mycobacteria, Listeria and Salmonella, and those infections dominate when interferon-gamma signalling or T cells fail — not this pattern of abscesses and fungal pneumonia.\"}, \"eli5\": \"His infection-fighting cells can swallow germs but cannot make the bleach that kills them. Germs that clean up their own bleach survive inside, and the body walls them off into little lumps.\", \"explanationImage\": \"fig_slide_granuloma\", \"explanationImageCaption\": \"The granuloma — a necrotic centre with living organisms, walled off by macrophages and lymphocytes. A siege, not a cure.\", \"los\": [{\"n\": 4, \"t\": \"Compare and contrast the clinical characteristics, pathophysiology, and treatments of primary immune deficiency diseases. Identify key labs used to diagnose immunodeficiencies.\", \"a\": \"s25-immunodef\", \"s\": \"§28\"}]}]";
var PRELOADED_UI_MODE="nbme";
var DEFAULT_QUESTION_COUNT=15;
var MAX_AI_QUESTION_COUNT=25;
